      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 732's STORAGE arm, THE FIRST RUNG IN THIS PROJECT THAT PUTS A COMMAND ON THE BUS WITH THE INTERRUPT ENABLE STANDING, AND THE FIRST THAT WRITES \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` BEFORE THE COMMAND RATHER THAN AFTER IT** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=14, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is 729's arm's whole act - the same two commands with the driver's own arguments and order, the same three gates, the same census, the same \`st_int_report\` body - **plus ONE 32-bit store to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` (\`SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\`, \`0x00000001\`) IMMEDIATELY BEFORE CMD0 IS PUT ON THE BUS, AND ITS RESTORE ON ONE UNCONDITIONAL LINE IMMEDIATELY AFTER CMD0'S PUBLISHES**, with \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` still ZERO. **THE WINDOW IS ONE ENTRANCE AND ONE EXIT, BOTH UNCONDITIONAL**, and every branch of the body - the census's refusal and the register half of the gate above the window, the between-commands gate below it - stands outside them, so the enabled interval is exactly CMD0's own send and its poll. **The reason is 730's press and not a preference.** Gate 1 is NARROWED rather than removed: \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` must still read ZERO and \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`'s only permitted bit is RESPONSE, both checked BEFORE anything is written, and the refusal publishes WHICH of the two fired (\`_cmd_gate_kind\`: 1 = \`SIGNAL_ENABLE\` non-zero, 2 = a bit of \`INT_ENABLE\` other than RESPONSE) instead of reporting a boolean. **THE CELL THAT SAYS THE RUNG WORKED IS \`_cmd_gated = 0\` WITH \`_cmd1_*\` KEYS PRESENT, AND THE CELL BESIDE IT IS \`_cmd0_complete = 1\`** - the first command this line drives to a completion, the first CMD1 it ever issues, and the pair that says the enable was the mask; \`_cmd0_status_any\` beside \`_cmd0_any_polls\` then says WHEN the bit appeared (the first sample is a bit the block was already holding, a later one is a block that latched it while the poll ran). **THE PREDICTED VALUES COME OFF 730'S OW