      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 743's STORAGE arm, AND IT IS THE SAME COMMAND AS THE RUNG BELOW WITH ONE CONSTANT MOVED - THE RESPONSE DEMAND REMOVED** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=19, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`). It is 742's arm's whole act: opcode 3, the argument \`0x00010000\`, rung 17's one-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` window and its single unconditional restore, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` READ AND NEVER WRITTEN, no data path, no \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\`, no GCC word, no \`core_mem\` word, no byte of the medium - and THE SAME GATE, \`st_all_send_cid\`'s returned \`cid_sent\`, so the two rungs' logs are comparable at the same place in the same driver order. **WHAT MOVED IS ONE FLAG WORD**: \`MMC_RSP_R1\` becomes \`MMC_RSP_NONE\` (a literal zero, the ABSENCE of \`MMC_RSP_PRESENT\`), and because every other term \`sdhci_cmd_to_flags\` adds is conditioned on it, the command word goes \`0x031A\` to \`0x0300\` and the ladder's FIRST \`INDEX\` bit does not survive. **AND THE ARM'S OWN CELL IS A CONTRAST AGAINST THE TWO COMMANDS IN LOGS THE PROJECT ALREADY HAS**: read this command's inhibit triplet and completion beside CMD0's (inhibit bit seen in 537 of its 1024 samples, and the command COMPLETED) and beside 742's CMD3 (the bit set in ALL 1024 samples, and no completion) - **that is the one comparison the ladder could not make until this rung existed**, and this file's own log carries neither of those rows. RUNG 19's BODY IS NOT IN THIS IMAGE - \`st_set_relative_addr\` is absent, asserted on the symbol - because the two bodies carry the same opcode and the same argument with a different flag word, and an image with both would put two response-demand variants of one command on the same bus in one boot, making each reading unattributable to its own flag word. **The three R1 decode cells rung 19 publishes are ABSENT here by design**: this command asks for no response, so \`rsp_present\` is zero and the register holds the previous command's word, and decoding that as this command's answer is the stale-word trap in its cheapest form. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt**"
      entry_conseq+=" **FOUR CELLS ARE THIS RUNG'S OWN, AND THEY ARE THE WHOLE ANSWER.** \`_nrsp_complete\` with \`_nrsp_status_any\` says whether a command that asks for nothing back ever FINISHES: \`_nrsp_complete = 1\` with a non-zero \`_nrsp_status_any\` means the response DEMAND was the stall, and the subject becomes the response path; \`_nrsp_inhibit_seen\` greater than zero beside \`_nrsp_complete = 0\` means the block accepted and started this command and never finished it either, which EXONERATES the demand and moves the census to the registers 726 section 3 named and no rung has read with a completion to report (\`SLOT_INT_STATUS 0xFC\`, \`CORE_PWRCTL_STATUS 0xDC\`); \`_nrsp_inhibit_seen = 0\` with \`_nrsp_word_read\` reading the arm's own word means the block never started it at all. And \`_nrsp_resp_post\` is the register read a THIRD time, through the same driver derivation as \`_nrsp_resp_pre\` and the command object's own word: 742 measured that register going from CMD1's word to ZERO across its CMD3, so a value here that is still CMD1's \`0x40ff8080\` CONFIRMS that the clearing was tied to the response demand - while a zero means the register is cleared by something else and 742 section 1's reading is REFUTED. **The window's cells read as every rung since 13's do**: \`_nrsp_ena_held\` = \`0x8001\` for a store of \`0x00000001\` and \`_nrsp_readback\` = \`0x8000\` for the PARTIAL restore, with \`_nrsp_sig_enable\` reading ZERO and never written. **The failure mode is a diagnosis and not a lost device**: the 1.2 s bound is inside \`st_send_command\`, and a delivery on the block's own line ends the run at the dispatcher, an ending this image already reads and survives (709 ended that way on intid 170)."
    elif [[ $wst == 20 ]]; then
      # **747: THE LADDER'S FIRST TEST OF A RULE THAT FITS EVERY WORD IT HAS EVER PUT ON THIS BUS, AND
      # THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE ANSWER IS A VERDICT ON A RULE RATHER THAN ON THE BLOCK.** 746's press
      # answered rung 20's own question and the same capture answered one nobody had asked: CMD1
      # (`0x0102`) and CMD2 (`0x0209`) were NEVER STARTED - `_cmd1_inhibit_after = 0` with
      # `_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0` over 5,091,328 samples, `_cid_inhibit_seen = 0` over 5,090,304, the word
      # (**778: BOTH DECIMALS WERE WRONG. The sentence is about 746's press, whose capture - rung20's -
      # reads cmd1_polls = 0x004DB000 = 5,091,328 and cid_polls = 0x004DAC00 = 5,090,304. 5,088,256 is
      # rung 18's CMD1 count and 5,088,000 is no capture's count at all. The words `NEVER STARTED` above are
      # UNMOVED: they rest on `_cmd1_inhibit_after` and `_cmd1_inhibit_seen`, not on a count.**)
      # read back out of `COMMAND 0x0E` in both cases - and identically on all three of the last three
      # presses. Five words are on the record: `0x0000` started and completed, `0x0300` started and
      # completed in 0.255 ms, `0x031A` started and never finished, `0x0102` and `0x0209` never started.
      # **The one rule that fits all five is that a word asking for a response without an `INDEX` bit is
      # DECLINED**, and this rung moves ONE bit to test it.
      #
      # **AND 747 ARMED THIS ARM WITHOUT WRITING THIS PARAGRAPH, WHICH IS WHY THIS ROW REFUSED UNTIL
      # 2026-09-27**: the chain above stopped at `wst == 19`, so this value fell to the ELSE arm's
      # sentence and the name this row printed would have been 693's. That is the 698 defect exactly,
      # and the check written for it is what caught it - a narration can be WRONG and still non-empty.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 747's STORAGE arm, AND IT IS ONE BIT FROM EACH ARM OF THE PAIR THAT IS ALREADY MEASURED** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=20, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 20, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 21**, the two spellings being one apart. It is 743's arm's whole act: opcode 3, the argument \`0x00010000\`, **the demand PUT BACK and the \`INDEX\` bit taken out instead** - \`ST_MMC_RSP_R1_NOIDX\` is \`PRESENT|CRC\` = \`0x05\`, so the word is \`0x030A\`: \`0x031A\` with the \`INDEX\` gone, and \`0x0300\` with the demand put back. It is 742's gate UNCHANGED (CMD2 was SENT) and 743's \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` window, so rungs 19, 20 and 21 are three logs comparable at the same place in the same driver order. **WHAT PROVES THE RUNG DID NOT WANDER IS A BUILD CLAUSE AND NOT THIS SENTENCE**: \`build_entry.sh\` refuses an image carrying both this body and rung 19's, because the two share an opcode and an argument and an image with both would put two response-demand variants of one command on the same bus in one boot, making each reading unattributable to its own flag word - asserted on the absent \`st_set_relative_addr\` symbol. **NO NEW REGISTER CLASS AND NO NEW STORE CLASS**: two stores, both to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`; \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` read and NEVER written; no data path, no \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\`, no GCC word, no \`core_mem\` word, no \`INT_STATUS\` write and no byte of the medium. **AND IT CARRIES 746's REPAIR RATHER THAN THE DEFECT**: its response pair is one \`readl(RESPONSE 0x10)\` at BOTH moments - the same expression \`st_send_command\` reads for its own response - where rungs 19 and 20 took theirs through the 136-bit formula whose top word is a different offset, so this arm's cells are ONE arithmetic at last (m756). **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which**"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S OWN ANSWER IS A THREE-WAY, AND EACH WAY NAMES THE NEXT ACT.** \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0\` with \`_nidx_word_read = 0x030A\` reading this arm's own word means the block never started it: **the rule is CONFIRMED** and CMD1's and CMD2's silence is explained in the same stroke. \`_nidx_inhibit_seen > 0\` with \`_nidx_complete = 0\` means the block ACCEPTED and STARTED a word that asks for a response and carries no \`INDEX\`, which **kills the rule's \`INDEX\` half** and puts the subject back on the response itself. \`_nidx_complete = 1\` **kills the rule outright**, and then the word that completed is the one to compare against the 0.255 ms \`0x0300\` took. **THE BRACKETING CELLS ARE \`_nidx_inhibit_after\`, \`_nidx_inhibit_last\`, \`_nidx_any_polls\`, \`_nidx_polls\`, \`_nidx_ticks\`, \`_nidx_status_any\`, \`_nidx_clear_after\` and \`_nidx_err\`** - and read \`_nidx_inhibit_last\` as the WHOLE \`PRESENT_STATE\` word and not bit 0 alone, because bit 24 is the CMD line's own signal level and it is the bit that separated 749's two producers. **THE R1 DECODE IS BACK AND IS NOT RUNG 20'S STALE-WORD TRAP**: this command DOES ask for a 48-bit response, so \`_nidx_state\`, \`_nidx_ready\` and \`_nidx_illegal\` are decoded from a register this command itself wrote - with the caveat that a field is a decode of whatever the register holds, and whether anything answered is the separate question \`_nidx_complete\` and \`_nidx_inhibit_last\` also ask. **AND \`_nidx_resp_moved\` / \`_nidx_resp_is_arg\` ARE THIS RUNG'S OWN CONTROL ON THAT**: \`_nidx_resp_pre\` and \`_nidx_resp_post\` are the register at two moments through ONE derivation, and \`_nidx_resp_is_arg = 1\` would mean the word read back is this arm's own argument \`0x00010000\` rather than anything a card sent. **The window's cells read as every rung since 13's do**: \`_nidx_ena_held\` for the store of \`0x00000001\`, \`_nidx_readback\` for the restore, \`_nidx_sig_enable\` reading ZERO and never written, and \`_nidx_wrote_back\` with \`_nidx_status_post\` on the one unconditional exit line. **\`_nidx_gated = 1\` with \`_nidx_gated_reason = 1\` is a complete answer and not a failed run**: \`st_all_send_cid\`'s returned value and the \`_cid_sent\` it publishes are one reading with two consumers, so this gate and that cell cannot disagree - and the gate is rungs 19's and 20's unchanged, so a gated run is comparable with theirs. **The failure mode is a diagnosis and not a lost device**: the 1.2 s bound is inside \`st_send_command\`, the ending is unmoved, and a delivery on this block's own line ends the run at the dispatcher, an ending this image already reads and survives."
    elif [[ $wst == 21 ]]; then
      # **762: THE LADDER'S FIRST NEW ARM SINCE 747, AND ITS FIRST ARM WHOSE WHOLE NEW ACT IS A READ
      # OF TWO REGISTERS THE VENDOR'S OWN BRING-UP PROGRAMS AND THIS LADDER HAS NEVER TOUCHED.** 756
      # section 1 named them - `CORE_DLL_CONFIG 0x100` and `HOST_CONTROL2 0x3E` - and 756 section 6
      # pre-registered the two-read discriminator; 761 section 5 closed the alternative space by
      # enumerating every register the vendor's power-up path writes, leaving exactly TWO candidates
      # and both of them DISABLES the ladder never makes. `sdhci_msm_set_uhs_signaling`
      # (`sdhci-msm.c:2537-2600`) is called on the power-up `set_ios` (`sdhci.c:1784-1785`, inside the
      # `host->version >= SDHCI_SPEC_300` block at `:1729` with NO timing test) and at
      # `host->clock <= CORE_FREQ_100MHZ` (`:2567`; the ladder is at 400 kHz) it sets `CORE_DLL_RST`
      # (`:2578`) and `CORE_DLL_PDN` (`:2583`) with the vendor's own comment *"the feedback clock must
      # be provided and DLL must not be used so that tuning can be skipped"* - a statement about HOW
      # THE CONTROLLER SAMPLES, and this ladder's measured stall is confined to receiving: CMD0 and the
      # no-response CMD3 complete in 0.255-0.30 ms while every word that demands a response is taken
      # and never completes with `_status_any = 0` over 5,088,256 polls.
      #
      # **THE WINDOW IS `hc_mem` AND NOT `core_mem`, WHICH IS THE ONE ERROR THAT WOULD MAKE THE THREE
      # READINGS MEAN SOMETHING ELSE.** The vendor reaches both registers as
      # `host->ioaddr + CORE_DLL_CONFIG`, and `host->ioaddr` is `sdhci_pltfm_init`'s FIRST memory
      # resource - `hc_mem` (`msm8974.dtsi:500`, `reg-names = "hc_mem", "core_mem"`). The DT settles
      # it: `msm8974pro.dtsi:1765` widens `&sdhc_1` from the base node's `0x11c` to `0x1a0`, and
      # `0x100`/`0x108` are beyond `0x11c` - the window was widened to cover exactly this pair.
      # `core_mem + 0x100` is a different register file one window away, and the two windows share
      # their high half `0xf982`, so a reading taken there would publish under these names with every
      # cell in the log agreeing with the record.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 762's DLL arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=21, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 21, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 22**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS PURELY ADDITIVE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE**: it adds a body \`st_dll_census\` of THREE READS AND NO STORE, and it changes NO GUARD ON A RUNG THAT HAS ALREADY BEEN BUILT, ARMED AND PRESSED - rung 21's call site is guarded \`>= 20\`, so a value-21 image carries BOTH this census and the \`0x030A\` CMD3, and the log carries the three DLL readings taken BEFORE the first command (the position rung 18 uses) beside a second sample of the stall. **WHAT PROVES THE RUNG DID NOT WANDER IS A BUILD CLAUSE AND NOT THIS SENTENCE**: \`build_entry.sh\` asserts that \`st_dll_census\`'s device store set is EMPTY, that its three addresses are each accessed exactly once (\`f982493e:ldrh=1 f9824a00:ldr=1 f9824a08:ldr=1\`) in program order, that its image-side set is empty, that it is called EXACTLY ONCE from \`entry_storage_probe\`, and that its call line precedes \`st_cmd_path\`'s - and the EMPTY STORE SET is the whole safety argument for reading above the command gate, which is 739's argument for rung 18 and 736's press is the measurement of what a store there costs (bit 15 rides with any write to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`, the gate read \`0x00008000\`, and NO COMMAND WENT ON THE BUS). **NO NEW REGISTER CLASS, NO NEW WINDOW AND NO NEW MAPPING**: all three addresses are in the megabyte \`0xf98\` this image already installs and reads, inside \`hc_mem\`'s declared \`0x1a0\`, so 692's \`addr >> 20\` interlock is satisfied by construction. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which**"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS THREE CELLS AND EACH CELL NAMES THE NEXT ACT.** \`_dll_rst = 1\` AND \`_dll_pdn = 1\` together mean the DLL is ALREADY reset and powered down by something that ran before this image, so **756 section 3's hypothesis is DEAD and nothing further is owed on this path** - and that is the cheapest possible answer, bought with three reads. \`_dll_rst = 0\` OR \`_dll_pdn = 0\` means the block is running with an undischarged DLL at 400 kHz against the vendor's own comment, and then **DOING WHAT THE VENDOR DOES - setting both bits - becomes the cheapest untried thing on this path**, which would be the NEXT rung and would need its own pre-registration because it is a STORE TO A REGISTER NO RUNG HAS WRITTEN. \`_dll_ctrl2_uhs\` (= \`_dll_ctrl2\` & 7) answers 756 section 4's second half in one cell: 0 means rung 4's \`SDHCI_RESET_ALL\` did reach \`HOST_CONTROL2\` and the spec argument holds; nonzero means the reset does NOT clear that field on this IP and the vendor's \`ctrl_2\` write at \`:2597\` becomes live too. **AND READ \`_dll_status\` AS A REGISTER AND NOT AS A VERDICT**: bit 7 is \`CORE_DLL_LOCK\`, published as \`_dll_lock\`, and a LOCKED DLL beside \`_dll_en = 1\` is the block sampling on a DLL the vendor says must not be used at this clock - while \`_dll_lock = 0\` beside the same enable is a DLL that never locked at all. **THE OFF-MEANING OF A BIT IS NOT THE SAME READING AS THE BIT**, which is why \`_dll_config\` and \`_dll_status\` are published WHOLE beside their named bits: the four bits this arm names are four of thirty-two, and a reader who wants the other twenty-eight should not have to spend a press on them (697's debt at \`HOST_VERSION 0xFE\` is the same lesson one register family over)."
    elif [[ $wst == 22 ]]; then
      # **766: THE LADDER'S FIRST ARM THAT CHANGES WHAT THE BLOCK'S OWN INTERRUPT-ENABLE REGISTER
      # CARRIES SINCE RUNG 14, AND ITS ANSWER IS A TABLE RATHER THAN A CELL.** 765 measured the
      # distance this rung closes: the vendor's own \`sdhci_init\` (\`sdhci.c:284-296\`, called once from
      # \`sdhci_add_host\` at \`:3589\`) writes ELEVEN status bits into \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` AND
      # \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` - \`0x01FF0003\` - while this ladder has enabled exactly ONE bit in its
      # whole history. And 730 pressed the rule that makes the difference decisive on THIS block: a
      # status bit is latched only while its enable stands ("the completion was there all along and
      # \`INT_ENABLE\` was the mask"). So the ladder's headline negative - \`_status_any = 0\` over
      # 5,093,376 samples at rung 22 - is a statement about a MASK as much as about the block, and
      # \`765\` section 2 is the narrowest true form of it. **This rung is the press that tells the two
      # apart, and it changes ONE CONSTANT.**
      #
      # **The window is rung 21's in every respect but the enable word**: same position (inside
      # \`st_cmd3_noidx\`, after the gate, with the same opcode, the same argument and the same flag word
      # \`0x030A\`), same TWO STORES both to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`, same \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` read and
      # never written, same restore. What is new is \`ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD\` = \`0x000F0001\` - the
      # completion plus TIMEOUT, CRC, END_BIT and INDEX, five bits, exactly the five 765 section 4
      # pre-registered - and TWO READS: \`_nidx_status_pre\` (\`INT_STATUS 0x30\` between the enable store
      # and its readback, with the wider mask in place and no command of this arm's on the bus) and
      # \`_nidx_tout_ctl\` (\`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` as a BYTE after the restore - the register no rung has
      # ever read). **768 MEASURED THIS AND CORRECTED IT (m776): \`0x00\` is the field's SHORTEST
      # setting, not its longest.** The value v selects 2^(13+v) TMCLK cycles, so 0x00 is 2^13 and
      # 0x0E is the longest - and the press measured the 0x00 case exactly: 2^13 cycles came out at
      # **665.2 us** against this platform's 12.288 MHz TMCLK, i.e. 666.7 us predicted. The
      # sentence's POINT survives - do not read 0 as "the block cannot time out" - and it is now
      # carried by a number instead of by a direction word. The same sentence is OWED in
      # src/entry/entry_storage.c, which is one of the entry image's 23 recorded sources.)
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 766's WIDER-ENABLE arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=22, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 22, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 23**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **ONE CONSTANT MOVED AND TWO READS ADDED, AND NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE ARM CHANGES**: \`st_cmd3_noidx\` sends the SAME opcode, the SAME argument (\`0x00010000\`) and the SAME flag word (\`0x030A\`) as rung 21, with the SAME TWO STORES both to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` and \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` READ AND NEVER WRITTEN - which is the half of the conjunction 730's own press measured is what keeps this block's shared SPI 123 line down (that press wrote \`INT_ENABLE\`, took no command and took no interrupt: \`_post_end_calls = 7\`, no \`_irq_other_*\` key), and which rungs 17, 19, 20, 21 and 22 have each relied on since. **WHAT THE WIDER WORD IS: FIVE BITS AND NOT THE VENDOR'S ELEVEN.** 765 section 4 pre-registered \`RESPONSE | TIMEOUT | CRC | END_BIT | INDEX\` = \`0x000F0001\`; the vendor's \`0x01FF0003\` adds \`AUTO_CMD_ERR\` (the block's own auto-command mechanism failing) and the \`DATA_*\` half (about a transfer this rung never starts), and neither is a fact about the card - so an arm enabling all eleven would widen the answer space with bits that name none of the four pre-registered outcomes. **WHAT PROVES THE RUNG DID NOT WANDER IS A BUILD CLAUSE AND NOT THIS SENTENCE**: \`build_entry.sh\` holds this body's access set and counts EXACTLY, value by value, so the rung-21 and rung-23 shapes are two expected sets and a source whose \`>= 22\` guard was widened to \`>= 21\` is refused at value 21 AND at value 20 - the two arms it would have silently replaced - and its store set is asserted to be UNCHANGED (\`f9824934:str\`, twice, and nothing else anywhere). The enable word's own VALUE is carried by six \`_Static_assert\`s in \`src/entry/entry_storage.c\`, because a body's memory accesses and its \`bl\` sites carry no immediate and no clause can read a constant. **NO NEW REGISTER CLASS AND NO NEW WINDOW**: \`0x2E\`, \`0x30\`, \`0x34\` and \`0x38\` are all in \`hc_mem\`, the window the Pro override widens to \`0x1a0\`, so 692's \`addr >> 20\` interlock is satisfied by construction. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which**"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS A FOUR-ROW TABLE, AND EVERY ROW NAMES THE NEXT ACT - WHICH IS WHY A PRESS HERE CANNOT BE WASTED.** \`INT_STATUS\`'s error half afterwards reads as: \`0x00010000\` (\`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT\`) LATCHED means **the card did not answer and the controller knew it**, and that one bit REFUTES the whole 'this controller runs a command and never sets its status' reading of rungs 13-22 - the subject then becomes the CARD (bus width, the CMD/DAT pads, the clock at the card's pins, the board file's \`qcom,pad-*\` settings) and NOT the controller's register file. \`0x00020000\` / \`0x00040000\` / \`0x00080000\` (CRC / END_BIT / INDEX) mean **the card answered and the word was malformed** - a live card on a mis-sampled or mis-configured bus - and the subject is the sampling and the bus width. **NOTHING, WITH THE ENABLES UP** is the STRONG form of the negative: a command taken, started and held for 1.2 s that never armed its own response timeout, which is a statement about the block's internal command path rather than about the card, and the subject becomes \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\`'s value, \`CLOCK_CONTROL\`'s divider at the moment of the command, and whether \`PRESENT_STATE\`'s bit 24 ever moves for a response-demanding word. Bit 15 only, or the same \`0x00008000\`-shaped value as before, means the sticky bit is a property of the IP and not a report. **TWO CELLS ARE NEW AND NEITHER SUBSTITUTES FOR THE TABLE**: \`_nidx_status_pre\` is what the block holds with the wider mask in place BEFORE this arm's command, and \`_nidx_tout_ctl\` is a register the vendor's own path leaves at its reset value for every data-less command (\`sdhci.c:827-828\` writes it only under \`if (data || (cmd->flags & MMC_RSP_BUSY))\`) - so a NON-ZERO \`_nidx_tout_ctl\` would be a finding and not a confirmation. **AND READ \`_nidx_status_any\` AS THE PRIMARY CELL**: the poll inside \`st_send_command\` publishes the RAW, unmasked \`INT_STATUS\` word it first saw non-zero in, over 5.09 million readings, so the same poll that has always read zero is the poll that will read \`0x00010000\` if the card is silent and the block knows it - this rung needed no new machinery to ask its question, only a wider mask."
    elif [[ $wst == 23 ]]; then
      # **767: THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER THAT ASKS ABOUT A PIN RATHER THAN ABOUT A REGISTER'S
      # STATE.** Every arm since rung 13 has asked what the block's own registers hold; 764 section 4
      # then reduced the remaining candidate space to three and left two of them indistinguishable
      # from any log the ladder has produced: candidate 1 (the card is not powered and stays silent)
      # and candidate 3 (the block never actually put anything on the bus). **The one measurement that
      # separates them is the CMD line's own SIGNAL LEVEL over the command's own window, and no arm
      # has ever been able to make it**: \`_nidx_inhibit_last\` publishes PRESENT_STATE bit 24 as a
      # SAMPLE AT THE END of the run, and HIGH at the end is what an IDLE line and a FINISHED
      # transmission BOTH look like, so one sample at the end cannot tell them apart. **This arm
      # COUNTS instead of sampling.**
      #
      # **And the count costs nothing.** The 1024-sample window rung 24 inherits from 724 already
      # reads PRESENT_STATE 0x24 once per iteration into \`inhibit_last\`; the new cell is one mask on
      # a value already in a register - no new register, no new width, no new window, no new
      # megabyte, and **NO NEW STORE TO THE BLOCK**. That was MEASURED rather than argued: the
      # classifier reports \`st_send_command\`'s device set and its image-side set UNCHANGED from rung
      # 23's - \`f9824924:ldr f9824930:ldr f9824930:str f9824908:str f982490e:strh f982490e:ldrh
      # f9824910:ldr\` and \`UNK:ldr UNK:str\` - so a relaxation of that clause was drafted and then
      # DELETED, because a check weakened for a reason that is not true is worse than no change.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 767's CMD-LINE-LEVEL arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=23, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 23, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 24**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS RUNG 23's WINDOW PLUS ONE CELL, AND IT MOVES NO GUARD ON A RUNG THAT HAS BEEN PRESSED**: \`st_cmd3_noidx\` still sends the SAME opcode (3), the SAME argument (\`0x00010000\`) and the SAME flag word (\`0x030A\`), still writes \`ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD\` = \`0x000F0001\` into \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` in the SAME two stores, still reads and never writes \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\`, and still reads \`_nidx_status_pre\` and \`_nidx_tout_ctl\`. What is new is \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen\`: over the SAME first 1024 poll samples \`_nidx_inhibit_seen\` counts, the number in which PRESENT_STATE's bit 24 - \`ST_SDHCI_CMD_LINE_LEVEL\`, the CMD line's LEVEL - was LOW. **Because it is a count over the SAME window as \`_nidx_inhibit_seen\`, the two cells are read AGAINST EACH OTHER**: \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0x400\` with \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen = 0\` is a block that held its inhibit for all 1024 samples while the line never moved at all. **NO NEW REGISTER, NO NEW WIDTH, NO NEW STORE TO THE BLOCK**: \`build_entry.sh\` asserts \`st_send_command\`'s device set and its image-side set EXACTLY, and both are unchanged from rung 23 - which is also what the arm's own safety reading is,\`_nidx_sig_enable\` still \`0\`, so no interrupt line can rise. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS ONE CELL WITH TWO READINGS, AND THE NEGATIVE ONE IS THE VALUABLE ONE.** \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen\` GREATER THAN ZERO means the line MOVED during the command: something drove it, which RETIRES 764 section 4's candidate 3 (the block never put anything on the bus) and leaves the card's own silence as the subject - and the block reading back its own drive over the pad counts as movement exactly as a card's answer does, so the cell separates 'nothing was ever on the line' from 'something was', not 'the block' from 'the card'. \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen = 0\` is the STRONG FORM: over 1024 samples the CMD line never went LOW while \`_nidx_inhibit_seen\` held its inhibit, which says NOTHING WAS EVER DRIVEN onto the bus and puts the subject on the block's own command path rather than on the card. **AND ITS ZERO IS A READING ONLY IF THE SAMPLER OUTRAN THE BUS** - that is the caveat this record carries and it is why the counting rung also publishes \`_nidx_polls\` and \`_nidx_ticks\`, which give the sampling period over exactly the window the count is over: a sampler slower than one bit period could MISS a transmission entirely and report a zero, so the sampling period is read BESIDE the count and never instead of it. **THIS ARM STILL CARRIES RUNG 23's FOUR-ROW TABLE UNCHANGED**, so the same press that answers the pin question also answers the enable question: \`_nidx_status_any\` = \`0x00010000\` alone still means the card is silent and the controller knows it, a CRC/END_BIT/INDEX bit still means the card answered wrongly, nothing with the enables up is still the strong negative about the block's own timeout, and bit 15 alone is still the sticky-bit reading."

    elif [[ $wst == 24 ]]; then
      # **770: THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER THAT READS A REGISTER IN A SECOND DEVICE, AND THE
      # SMALLEST ONE IT HAS EVER CARRIED - ONE READ, ONE NEW MEGABYTE, ZERO STORES.**
      #
      # 768's press moved the frontier off the controller and onto the card's pins. 769 section 2 then
      # retired one of the two things that live between them by a REGISTER LAYOUT rather than by a
      # reading: SDC1 is routed through DEDICATED PADS and not GPIOs (`sdhci-msm.c:267-270`
      # documents the distinction and `:1266` picks the pad branch because `sdhc_1` has no `gpios`
      # property), and `SDC1_HDRV_PULL_CTL` - physical `0xFD512044` - has **no function-select field
      # at all**: every bit of it is drive strength or pull (`gpio-msm-common.c:59-88`). What is left
      # is that register's own CONTENT, and the vendor writes it in exactly one place this ladder has
      # never been: inside `sdhci_msm_pwr_irq`'s `CORE_PWRCTL_BUS_ON` branch
      # (`sdhci-msm.c:2015-2032`), through `sdhci_msm_setup_pins` -> `sdhci_msm_setup_pad`. Rung 7
      # wrote the power byte and rung 8 registered the handler, but the ladder's handler acks the
      # latch rather than performing the vendor's BUS_ON work, so **the pads have been attempted by no
      # rung.**
      #
      # **THE FIELD THE MECHANISM RESTS ON IS `PULL SDC1_CMD`.** MMC's CMD line is OPEN-DRAIN during
      # identification: both sides pull it LOW and it returns HIGH only through a pull-up, which on
      # this SoC is this pad register and not a discrete resistor. A board whose pads sit at reset
      # reads `0x00000000` - no pull-up on CMD, none on DATA, drive strength at minimum.
      #
      # **AND THE EXPECTATION IS DERIVED, NOT TYPED.** `qcom,pad-pull-on = <0x0 0x3 0x3 0x1>` and
      # `qcom,pad-drv-on = <0x4 0x4 0x4>` (`msm8974pro-ac-pm8941-mtp-v5.dts:25-29`) through
      # `msm_tlmm_set_field`'s `reg &= ~(mask<<off); reg |= (val&mask)<<off;` (hdrive width 3, pull
      # width 2, `gpio-msm-common.c:481-496`) evaluate to **`0x00009F24`**, and six `_Static_assert`s
      # in `src/entry/entry_storage.c` fix the address (`== 0xfd512044`), its megabyte
      # (`>> 20 == 0xFD5`), its alignment, the seven masks' disjointness (their sum equals their OR)
      # and the CMD-pull mask's overlap - so the value this arm compares against is computed from the
      # board's own arrays on every build and is not pasted from `xnu_arm_entry.bin`'s paragraph.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 770's SDC1-PAD arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=24, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 24, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 25**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS RUNG 24's WINDOW UNCHANGED PLUS ONE READ-ONLY BODY, AND THE BODY'S WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT IS THAT IT HAS NO STORE IN IT.** \`st_pad_census\` installs the \`0xFD5\` megabyte through \`entry_mmio_section\` (693's design, and 692's press is why the install cannot be skipped: an uninstalled megabyte faults with \`fsr = 0x5\`), then takes **ONE 32-bit read of \`0xFD512044\`** - a second device's megabyte, \`0xFD512044 >> 20 == 0xFD5\`, where no other device in this image lives, so 692's \`addr >> 20\` interlock is satisfied by construction. It publishes the word RAW (\`xnu_live_storage_pad_raw\`) beside seven field decodes - the three hdrive fields at bits 6/3/0 and the four pull fields at 13/11/9/15 - plus the derived expectation (\`_pad_expect\`), a one-bit \`_pad_match\`, the install's own outcome (\`_pad_map\`, \`_pad_slot_before\`, \`_pad_desc\`, \`_pad_section\`) with \`_pad_read = 1\` beside it and **\`xnu_live_storage_pad_writes = 0\`, a count that never leaves zero**. **READING A PAD-CONTROL REGISTER IS SAFE BY THE VENDOR'S OWN CODE**: \`msm_tlmm_set_field\` itself opens with \`__raw_readl(reg)\` on exactly this address (\`gpio-msm-common.c:487\`), it is not write-one-to-clear and not a FIFO. **AND THE BUILD IS WHAT SAYS SO, NOT THIS SENTENCE**: the rung-25 clause in \`src/entry/build_entry.sh\` reports the body's device set as \`[fd512044:ldr ]\` with counts \`[fd512044:ldr=1 ]\`, an EMPTY image side, in program order, called exactly once from \`entry_storage_probe\` and BEFORE \`st_cmd_path\` - refused rather than noted if any of those changes. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS A TWO-ROW TABLE AND BOTH ROWS NAME THE NEXT ACT.** \`_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` (every field as the board declares it) means **THE PADS ARE CONFIGURED**, 768 section 5's other half is retired, \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` is present, and the subject moves to the PMIC rails - \`sdhci_msm_setup_vreg\` (\`sdhci-msm.c:2019\`), a bus this ladder cannot reach from \`hc_mem\` - so one read buys the elimination of an entire candidate. \`_pad_raw = 0x00000000\`, or any field at odds with the derived value, means **THE VENDOR ACT WAS NEVER APPLIED ON THIS PATH** - no pull-up on CMD, no pull-up on DATA, drive at minimum - and the next rung is then a single READ-MODIFY-WRITE of ONE register in a megabyte whose section this arm has already installed. **ONE CAVEAT THE CELL CANNOT CARRY, CARRIED HERE INSTEAD**: the read is taken at the arm's own moment, which is BEFORE the command path, so row 1 is a reading about the pads' STATE and NOT a proof that this image applied them - the boot chain may have left exactly the same values. **Only row 2 carries an act.** **A CELL THAT DOES NOT DECIDE THE CARD AND IS SAID SO**: \`_pad_match = 1\` does not mean the eMMC will answer, because \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` being present is a NECESSARY and not a sufficient condition - \`CLOCK_CONTROL\` at the pins, the bus width and the card's own power-on state are all still unmeasured, and 768 section 6 already notes the idle line reads HIGH on every arm on record, which is what a pull-up produces AND what a floating line that happens to sit high looks like. **The register is the only thing that can tell those two apart**, which is why this rung reads it rather than reasoning about it."
    elif [[ $wst == 25 ]]; then
      # **772: THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER WHOSE WHOLE ACT IS TO PRINT VALUES IT WAS ALREADY
      # MEASURING - FOUR CELLS THAT WERE BEING THROWN AWAY.**
      #
      # 771 read the rung-24 press's log as ONE FOUR-ROW TABLE and found that the four commands this
      # ladder issues are NOT the same experiment: `_cmd0_inhibit_seen` is 0x218 beside a command that
      # completed and latched `INT_RESPONSE`, `_cmd1_inhibit_seen` and `_cid_inhibit_seen` are 0 beside
      # 5.09 M polls each with NOTHING latched at all, and `_nidx_inhibit_seen` is 0x400 beside a block
      # that armed AND FIRED its own response timeout at 665.2 us. **AND THE ONLY TRACE OF WHAT THE CMD
      # LINE WAS DOING DURING CMD1 OR CMD2 IS ONE SAMPLE** - `inhibit_last` bit 24, LOW for CMD1 and
      # HIGH for CMD2 on every capture in the archive (10 cmd0, 8 cmd1, 7 cid) - because the sampler's
      # own COUNT of that bit (`cmdlow_seen`) is filled for EVERY command and rung 24 published it for
      # `nidx` alone. A single sample at the end of a window is exactly the reading rung 24 replaced
      # with a count for CMD3; this arm finishes that repair for the other three.
      #
      # **AND IT IS THE CHEAPEST ARM THIS LADDER CAN BUILD**, because all four cells already exist in a
      # register: `cmdlow_seen` for cmd0/cmd1/cid, and `cid_inhibit_after` - the fourth row of 771's
      # table and the only row the log does not carry - which `st_send_command` fills for every command
      # and NO publish block has ever printed. **No new device access, no new register, no new window,
      # no new megabyte and no store of any kind.** The build's own `st_send_command` clause asserts
      # that body's device set and it is UNCHANGED from rung 24.
      #
      # **AND IT IS RUNG 25 PLUS THOSE FOUR CELLS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: the value-24 build
      # from this same source is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked `armed-storage-f72e9f18`, so one press
      # answers the SDC1 pad question AND these four cells - and rung 25's park need never be pressed.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 772's COMMAND-TABLE arm, and it is RUNG 25 PLUS FOUR CELLS** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=25, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 25, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 26**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER WHOSE WHOLE ACT IS TO PRINT VALUES IT WAS ALREADY MEASURING.** Four keys are new and all four were already in a register: \`xnu_live_storage_cmd0_cmdlow_seen\`, \`xnu_live_storage_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` and \`xnu_live_storage_cid_cmdlow_seen\` - the count of the first 1024 samples in which \`PRESENT_STATE 0x24\` bit 24, **the CMD line's own level**, was LOW, which the 724 sampler has computed for EVERY command since rung 24 and which rung 24 published for \`nidx\` alone - and \`xnu_live_storage_cid_inhibit_after\`, the fourth row of 771's table and the only one the log has never carried, because \`st_send_command\` fills it for every command and NO publish block has ever printed it. **AND NOTHING ELSE MOVED: no new device access, no new register, no new window, no new megabyte and NO STORE OF ANY KIND** - the build's own \`st_send_command\` clause asserts that body's device set and it is UNCHANGED from rung 24, and the SDC1 pad read rung 25 installed is inherited whole. **THE ARM STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 25, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 25\`, and the value-24 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-f72e9f18\` (\`f72e9f18...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so one press answers the SDC1 pad question AND these four cells, and rung 25's park need never be pressed. $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS THREE COUNTS BESIDE THE ONE SAMPLE THEY REPLACE.** \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` is the cell 771 needs most and it exists because of a CONTRADICTION the archive already carries: \`_cmd1_inhibit_last = 0x00F80000\` - bit 24 LOW - on every capture while \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` over all 1024 samples, so on this block the line can sit LOW with \`CMD_INHIBIT\` never rising, and **\`the block never started this command\` and \`the block drove the line and the inhibit bit is not a reliable witness\` are INDISTINGUISHABLE from one sample.** The count separates them: 0 says the line never moved at all, and anything else says it did. **READ EACH COUNT BESIDE ITS OWN NEIGHBOUR AND NEVER ALONE**: \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen\`, \`_cid_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cid_inhibit_seen\`, \`_cmd0_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen = 0x218\` - and \`_cid_inhibit_after\` beside \`_cmd0_inhibit_after = 1\` and \`_nidx_inhibit_after = 1\`, the two rows of 771's table that DO carry it. **ITS ZERO IS A READING ONLY IF THE SAMPLER OUTRAN THE BUS**: a sampler slower than one bit period could miss a transmission and report a zero, so the sampling period is read BESIDE the count - \`_cmd1_polls\` and \`_cmd1_ticks\` are over exactly the window the count is over - and never instead of it. **AND NEITHER CELL IS A VERDICT ABOUT THE CARD**: they say what the block did with the line, and 771 already retired \`RESPONSE\` in both directions, so a nonzero count is not a response and a zero is not proof the card was never addressed. **AND ROW 1 OF RUNG 25'S PAD TABLE IS CARRIED UNCHANGED HERE**: \`xnu_live_storage_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` retires 768 section 5's other half and sends the next rung to the PMIC rails, while \`0x00000000\` makes it a single read-modify-write of one register whose section this arm has already installed - so this press buys that answer too, at no extra cost."
    elif [[ $wst == 26 ]]; then
      # **775: THE THIRD ARM IN A ROW WHOSE ACT IS TO PRINT SOMETHING THAT WAS ALREADY MEASURED - AND
      # THE ONE WHOSE NEW KEY ADDRESSES THE LADDER'S OWN ANOMALY.**
      #
      # 772 published four cells the 724 sampler fills for EVERY command and rung 24 printed for one.
      # This arm is the other half of that same gap, one publish block over: `st_all_send_cid` was
      # written at rung 16, before rungs 12 and 24 added the entrance state and the inhibit gate to
      # `struct st_cmd_result`, so its block prints TEN of twenty-seven fields and never grew.
      #
      # The eleven it gains are the entrance (`ps_before`, `inhibit_before`), the inhibit gate
      # (`inhibit_polls`, `inhibit_ticks`, `inhibit_timeout`), the clear (`clear_wrote`), the argument,
      # the exit state (`ps_after`) and the two companions that make a zero response a reading
      # (`rsp_present`, `resp_read`) - plus **`stale`**, which is the one that decides something.
      #
      # **`_cid_stale` IS `INT_STATUS 0x30` AS `st_send_command` FINDS IT FOR CMD2** - the first read of
      # that register since the poll of CMD1 gave up 1.2 seconds earlier. That matters because the
      # rung-24 press shows `_nidx_status_pre = 0x00018000`, i.e. `ERROR | TIMEOUT` latched after the
      # poll of CMD2 gave up, and the only write in that interval is the enable store, which 736
      # measured sets bit 15 by itself. So the archive cannot separate "CMD2 timed out late" from "the
      # store set it" - and `_cid_stale` asks the same question one command earlier.
      #
      # **AND 777 CORRECTED THE SENTENCE THIS COMMENT USED TO CARRY.** It read *in an interval with NO
      # write to `0x34` at all*, and that is FALSE: `st_all_send_cid` writes `0x34` at
      # src/entry/entry_storage.c:3110, and `_cid_stale` is `c2.stale`, read inside `st_send_command`
      # at :2560 - after that store and after its own inhibit gate. So the interval DOES contain one,
      # and by 736's measurement it raises bit 15. **The consequence is not that the cell is
      # worthless; it is that its answer space has no zero row and never had one**:
      # `_cid_stale == 0x00008000` EXACTLY says nothing but this arm's own store is in the register,
      # and anything ABOVE that says something was latched that this arm did not put there. The
      # boundary moves up one bit; the `zero` row 775 pre-registered is unreachable by construction.
      # 777 carries the derivation and names the entry source's own copy of the same sentence as owed
      # to the next build (a comment there forces one, and 771's COST rule is why it is not paid here).
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 775's COMPLETE-THE-CMD2-ROW arm, and it is RUNG 26 PLUS ELEVEN CELLS** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=26, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 26, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 27**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **ITS WHOLE ACT IS TO PRINT THE REST OF THE CMD2 RESULT.** \`st_all_send_cid\` publishes TEN of the twenty-seven fields \`st_send_command\` fills, because its block was written at rung 16 and never grew when rungs 12 and 24 added the entrance state and the inhibit gate to the struct; this arm prints eleven more - \`xnu_live_storage_cid_ps_before\`, \`_inhibit_before\`, \`_inhibit_polls\`, \`_inhibit_ticks\`, \`_inhibit_timeout\`, \`_stale\`, \`_clear_wrote\`, \`_arg\`, \`_ps_after\`, \`_rsp_present\` and \`_resp_read\`. **NO NEW DEVICE ACCESS, NO REGISTER, NO WINDOW, NO MEGABYTE AND NO STORE**: every one of the eleven is already in the struct when the block runs, and the build's \`st_send_command\` clause asserts that body's device set UNCHANGED from rung 26. **AND ONE OF THE ELEVEN DECIDES SOMETHING NO OTHER CELL IN THE ARCHIVE CAN**: \`_cid_stale\` is \`INT_STATUS 0x30\` as \`st_send_command\` finds it for CMD2 - the first read of that register since the poll of CMD1 gave up 1.2 seconds earlier - and the rung-24 press shows the register reading \`0x00018000\` at the entrance of CMD3, with the enable store (which 736 measured sets bit 15 by itself) the only write in between. **So the archive cannot separate a late CMD2 timeout from the store's own effect, and this cell asks the same question one command earlier.** **AND 777 CORRECTED THIS CELL'S ANSWER SPACE: ITS ZERO ROW DOES NOT EXIST.** The interval 775 called free of a \`0x34\` write contains one - \`st_all_send_cid\` stores it at \`entry_storage.c:3110\` and \`_cid_stale\` is read inside \`st_send_command\` at \`:2560\`, after that store - so by 736's measurement the cell is \`>= 0x00008000\` on EVERY run by construction. Read it as \`== 0x00008000\` (this arm's own store and nothing else) versus anything ABOVE that; a zero would mean \`CMD1 did nothing\`, and no run of this arm can print one. **AND IT STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 26, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 26\` and the value-25 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-a1378a48\` (\`a1378a48...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so **one press answers the SDC1 pad question, rung 26's four cells AND these eleven, and rungs 23, 25 and 26 need never be pressed.** $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS THE CMD2 ROW OF 771's TABLE, COMPLETED.** 771 found that the four commands this ladder issues are not the same experiment - \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen = 0x218\` beside a command that completed and latched \`INT_RESPONSE\`, \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` and \`_cid_inhibit_seen = 0\` beside 5.09 M polls each with NOTHING latched at all, \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0x400\` beside a block that armed and FIRED its own response timeout at 665.2 us - and the CMD2 row was the EMPTIEST of the four, because fifteen of its twenty-seven fields were never printed. **READ \`_cid_stale\` FIRST AND BESIDE ITS NEIGHBOURS, NEVER ALONE**: it is the same register as \`_cid_clear_after = 0\` and \`_cmd1_status_any = 0\` at two other moments and as \`_cid_status_any\`, which is what the poll itself saw over 5,088,256 reads - so a non-zero \`_cid_stale\` beside a zero \`_cid_status_any\` is the block setting a bit AFTER its poll gave up, and a zero everywhere is the block doing nothing between CMD1 and CMD2. **AND \`_cid_inhibit_timeout\` IS THE SECOND READING THAT MATTERS**: a 1 there would make CMD2 a REFUSAL AT THE GATE - no command on the bus at all - rather than a command that ran and stalled, which is a different entry in 771's answer space and would move the frontier off the card entirely. \`_cmd1_inhibit_timeout = 0\` on record, so this is the first time that gate has been read for CMD2. **AND THE OTHER NINE ARE NOT DECORATION**: \`ps_before\`/\`ps_after\` are the block's own state either side, and \`rsp_present\` beside \`resp_read\` is the pair rung 12 introduced because a zero \`RESPONSE\` on rung 11 was the ABSENCE OF A READ wearing the shape of a value. **AND NEITHER IS A VERDICT ABOUT THE CARD**: they are about what the BLOCK did, and 771 already retired \`RESPONSE\` in both directions, so a non-zero here is not a response and a zero is not proof the card was never addressed. **AND ROW 1 OF RUNG 25'S PAD TABLE IS CARRIED UNCHANGED HERE**, so this press buys that answer too, at no extra cost."
    elif [[ $wst == 27 ]]; then
      # **776: THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER THAT STORES TO A SECOND DEVICE - AND THE STORE IS GUARDED BY
      # THE READ THAT PRECEDES IT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ARM A FORK AND NOT A FIX.**
      #
      # 773 section 4 pre-registered this design in the order 769 section 6 pre-registered rung 25 and
      # 770 built it, and 773 section 5 fixed its shape: read 0xFD512044, compare against the value the
      # board's own arrays produce, write ONLY if it does not match, read back. The registered rung
      # number moved by one because value 26 was spent on 775's eleven publishes, and that is the only
      # thing about the design that changed.
      #
      # Two things this record must not let a reader conflate:
      #   (1) the arm's answer is a FORK, and its first row is the one nothing is written on;
      #   (2) the store's safety is NOT the guard's - the guard is what makes the answer readable. The
      #       value written is exactly what the vendor's own `sdhci_msm_setup_pad` writes on this board,
      #       which is why an unguarded fire would still be a word Linux itself stores at every BUS_ON.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 776's GUARDED-PAD-WRITE arm, and it is RUNG 27 PLUS ONE CONDITIONAL STORE** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=27, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 27, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 28**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **THE ONE ACT IS ONE MASKED READ-MODIFY-WRITE OF ONE REGISTER, AND IT IS THE ONLY STORE THIS LADDER HAS EVER MADE OUTSIDE THE CONTROLLER AND THE CLOCK CONTROLLER.** \`st_pad_census\` - the body rung 25 added to READ the TLMM's SDC1 pad-control register at physical \`0xFD512044\` - now ends in an \`if\`: when the 32-bit read already equals \`ST_TLMM_SDC1_EXPECT\` = \`0x00009F24\` (the value this board's own \`qcom,pad-pull-on\`/\`qcom,pad-drv-on\` arrays produce through the kernel's own shift and mask) it publishes \`_pad_write_skipped = 1\` and \`_pad_writes = 0\` and **NO STORE IS MADE AT ALL**; otherwise it writes \`(raw & ~MASK) | EXPECT\` over the UNION OF THE SEVEN FIELDS - hdrive clk/cmd/data at bits 6/3/0 and pull clk/cmd/data/rclk at 13/11/9/15, union \`0x0001FFFF\` - and publishes \`_pad_write_skipped = 0\`, \`_pad_writes = 1\`, \`_pad_wrote\`, \`_pad_after\` and \`_pad_after_match\`. **THE BITS OUTSIDE THE SEVEN ARE THE BITS THE READ HANDED BACK**, carried through unchanged, which is what makes this a field write and not a whole-word write to a register a bootloader may have owned. **AND THE VALUE IT WRITES IS THE VENDOR'S OWN**: \`sdhci_msm_setup_pad\` (\`sdhci-msm.c:964\`) walks those same seven fields to those same two arrays, so even an unguarded fire would be a word Linux itself stores on this board at every \`CORE_PWRCTL_BUS_ON\` (\`sdhci-msm.c:2015-2032\`) - **the guard is what makes the ANSWER readable, not what makes the store safe.** **THE STORE IS A PLAIN READ-MODIFY-WRITE WITH A MASK** - field for field the one \`msm_tlmm_set_field\` performs (\`gpio-msm-common.c:481-496\`): no unlock sequence, no write-one-to-clear, no FIFO, no shadow register - so a SINGLE masked RMW over the union makes the vendor's six-call ORDER irrelevant, and that is why one store is safe here where six interleaved ones would need an argument. **AND THE BUILD REFUSES THE ARM WITHOUT THE GUARD, TWICE AND INDEPENDENTLY**: the \`str\` must be PREDICATED or SPANNED BY A CONDITIONAL BRANCH in \`st_pad_census\`'s own disassembly (this arm's compiled form is \`SPANNED-BY\`, the arm's own clause prints which) **and** the SOURCE must carry exactly one \`if (raw == ST_TLMM_SDC1_EXPECT) {\` with the store in its ELSE arm, read by line number - a disassembly reading and a C-level reading, so a perturbed build that puts the store on the only path is refused rather than reported. $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **779's DISCOUNT, AND IT IS ABOUT THE FORK'S SECOND ROW: \`_pad_raw\` IS THE READING AND THE FORK IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS.** The value the guard compares against is a word SOME tree on this device declares, not the word THIS device's tree declares: the dt.img this image packs (\`\$QCDT_DT\` in scripts/build.sh, the Mi 4's own backup) holds TWO of them - \`0x9F24\` on four of its trees and \`0x9FE4\` on the fifth - and which one the bootloader selects is not established anywhere in this tree. So \`_pad_write_skipped = 1\` is sound (*the register already holds the word the source expects*), and **\`_pad_write_skipped = 0\` is NOT evidence that the vendor's act was never applied**: it says only that the register did not hold \`0x9F24\`, and a register holding \`0x9FE4\` is a CONFIGURED pad that this arm has just re-configured to the other board's word - both words being ones a tree on this device declares. Read the fork as \`raw == 0x9F24\` versus \`raw != 0x9F24\`, read \`_pad_raw\` as the reading, and do not read \`_pad_after_match = 1\` as *the fix landed on an unconfigured pad*. **AND THE SOURCE'S CITATION IS WRONG AND OWED**: \`entry_storage.c:302-303\` calls \`msm8974pro-ac-pm8941-mtp-v5.dts\` *the Mi 4's own board file*, and that checkout holds no cancro device tree at all - only Qualcomm's reference boards - so the constant is MTP-V5's word and not a reading of this device. \`tools/derive_sdc1_pads.py\` re-derives the candidates from the device's own dt.img and \`records/sdc1-pad-candidates.txt\` is the TRACKED reading of them; \`tools/check_sdc1_pad_expectation.py\` in \`make check\` refuses a constant that matches NONE of them (779). **THE ARM'S ANSWER IS A FORK AND ITS FIRST ROW COSTS NOTHING BECAUSE IT IS THE ROW NOTHING IS WRITTEN ON.** \`_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` with \`_pad_write_skipped = 1\` and \`_pad_writes = 0\` says **THE PADS WERE ALREADY CONFIGURED** - by the bootloader, since no rung and no Linux on this path wrote them, and 773 section 2 is the latch that says the vendor act runs once per Linux boot and never here - and on that row **768 section 5's other half is retired and the pad candidate is CLOSED**: the frontier is then the PMIC rails alone, acts 1 and 3 of the vendor's BUS_ON branch, which 773 section 3 counted as unreachable from \`hc_mem\`, so the goal's storage clause needs a different approach and this ladder has said so before spending anything. **(780: AND THAT ROW'S FRONTIER IS WEAKER THAN IT READS.** The record gave a driver reason for closing the power candidate and the reason does not apply to this board: \`sdhci_msm_setup_vreg\`'s early return is keyed on \`vreg_data == NULL\`, which \`populate_pdata:1397\` makes unreachable here because it allocates \`vreg_data\` unconditionally while both \`-supply\` phandles exist; and \`qcom,vdd-always-on\`'s only behavioural effect is in the RELEASE path (\`vreg_disable:1796\`/\`:1813\`), where it demotes to LPM instead of disabling. **The vendor's own path ENABLES both rails** - \`probe:2826\` -> \`vreg_init\` -> \`vreg_reset:1865\` \`setup_vreg(pdata,1,true)\` -> \`vreg_enable:1765\` \`regulator_enable\` with no \`is_always_on\` test - and nothing in this tree disables them, so the candidate is UNMEASURED and not a loose end to expect productive. The record's \`vmmc\` half is true of \`sdhci.c\`'s OWN handle and says nothing about the rails \`sdhci-msm\` uses. And \`the PMIC is unreachable\` is false as stated: the SPMI arbiter's three pages (\`msm8974.dtsi:848-854\`, \`0xFC4C{A,B,F}000\`) sit INSIDE the \`0xFC4\` megabyte this ladder installs and has read through since 694 (\`_gcc_map = 1\`), while the PMIC's own registers are SPMI-addressed and not memory mapped - and the AP's route to a rail is \`qcom,rpm-smd\`, a vote over shared memory, not the arbiter. No rung is proposed from any of it and the ladder has never read \`0xFC4Cxxxx\`.) **\`_pad_raw\` anything else, with \`_pad_write_skipped = 0\`, says the vendor act was never applied on this path and the write has been MADE**: \`_pad_after = 0x00009F24\` means the fix landed, and \`_pad_after_match = 1\` is the same statement as a bit - read \`_pad_wrote\` beside \`_pad_after\` and never instead of it, because the value we asked for is not the value the word holds. **AND THE SAFETY READING IS A COUNT AND NOT A CONSTANT**: \`_pad_writes\` is published as what happened, so \`0\` and \`1\` are the only two values a well-formed run can carry - anything else is a build that is not this arm. **WHAT THIS STORE CANNOT DO, STATED BECAUSE A FIRST STORE TO A SECOND DEVICE IS WHERE AN UNSTATED RISK LIVES**: it cannot reach any OTHER REGISTER (one word, no indexed or block access); cannot reach ANOTHER PAD BANK (\`0x2044\` is not the SDC2 register at \`0x2048\`, and the seven masks are disjoint so their union is their sum, which is rung 25's own \`_Static_assert\`); cannot change a FUNCTION (every field of this register is drive strength or pull - 769 section 2 established by layout that there is no function-select field to write); does not put the bus at risk (no transfer is in flight at that moment); and does not touch the PMIC, the rails or any clock - acts 1 and 3, the ones that could brown out a rail, are the two 773 says are unreachable and this arm does not reach them. **AND IT DOES NOT SAY THE PADS ARE THE CAUSE**: a register already holding \`0x9F24\` is the MORE LIKELY row, because a board that boots from this eMMC to \`fastboot\` has had something drive that bus successfully - and even a register that now holds it does not say the card will answer, because \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` is NECESSARY and not sufficient. **AND IT STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 27, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 27\`, and the value-26 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-8096cb2c\` (\`8096cb2c...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so **one press answers the SDC1 pad question, the fix question, rung 26's four cells, rung 27's eleven publishes and rung 23's window, and rungs 23, 25, 26 and 27 need never be pressed.** **AND A COST ALREADY ON RECORD IS PAID IN THIS SAME BUILD**: 771 refuted the 737 window paragraph in \`src/entry/entry_storage.c\` and left it standing for a COST reason rather than a lane reason - correcting a comment forces a build, and this build is the arm that carries the correction - so the paragraph's rationale-as-a-prediction is withdrawn in place, with 771's measurement named beside it. **AND THIS ARM'S ANSWER IS \`xnu_live_storage_pad_raw\`, \`_pad_write_skipped\`, \`_pad_writes\`, \`_pad_wrote\`, \`_pad_after\` and \`_pad_after_match\`.**"
    elif [[ $wst == 28 ]]; then
      # **785: THE LAST MASKED WINDOW, AND IT IS THE WIDENING RUNG 23 GAVE THE LADDER'S LAST COMMAND
      # APPLIED TO THE TWO WINDOWS THAT CARRY ITS FIRST THREE.**
      #
      # 783 read the SCOPE of 766's repair rather than its rule and found it had reached ONE OF THREE
      # command windows: `st_cmd3_noidx` took `ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD` (0x000F0001) while `st_cmd_path`
      # (CMD0 and CMD1, src/entry/entry_storage.c:4351) and `st_all_send_cid` (CMD2, :3110) still wrote
      # the lone `ST_SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE` (0x00000001). 785 is that same constant at those two windows
      # and NOTHING ELSE: the two stores are the same two stores both windows have made since rungs 14
      # and 16, the count is unchanged, `SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38` is still read and never written, and there
      # is no new register, no new width, no new window, no new megabyte and no new device access.
      #
      # **The reason it matters is 765 section 1 and it is about what a zero means**: this block's
      # `INT_STATUS` is a GATED VIEW, so a status bit is visible only while its enable stands - and on
      # the rung-28 arm `_cmd1_status_any` and `_cid_status_any` read 0 over their full 1.2 s windows on
      # a one-bit mask. Those zeros were readings about the MASK, and CMD1 is `SEND_OP_COND`, which is
      # the one command an eMMC must answer for this ladder to move at all.
      #
      # The branch is written as a whole `#if`/`#else` pair so that a value below 28 compiles to rung
      # 14's and rung 16's exact lines, which is what makes the containment claim MEASURED: the value-27
      # payload and the value-27 entry image from this same source are byte-identical to the parked
      # armed-storage-d5d98738 and its own `xnu_arm_entry.bin`.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 785's TWO-WINDOW-WIDENING arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=28, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 28, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 29**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **ITS WHOLE ACT IS ONE CONSTANT APPLIED AT TWO WINDOWS, AND IT IS THE CONSTANT RUNG 23 ALREADY PUT IN THE THIRD.** \`st_cmd_path\` and \`st_all_send_cid\` now write \`ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD\` = \`0x000F0001\` - the completion with the four command-level error bits, \`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT\` \`0x00010000\`, \`SDHCI_INT_CRC\` \`0x00020000\`, \`SDHCI_INT_END_BIT\` \`0x00040000\` and \`SDHCI_INT_INDEX\` \`0x00080000\` - in place of the one-bit \`ST_SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\` = \`0x00000001\`; \`st_cmd3_noidx\` keeps exactly what rung 23 gave it. **AND 786 CORRECTED A SCOPE CLAIM HERE: \`st_cmd_path\`'s window is CMD0 ALONE.** 785 and 783 both wrote \"CMD0 and CMD1\" and the press refuted it by the log's own line order - \`_ena_wrote_back = 0x00000000\`, the restore, prints BEFORE \`_cmd1_status_any\` in the same capture, so CMD1 ran under \`INT_ENABLE = 0x00008000\` and its \`_cmd1_status_any = 0\` over \`_cmd1_polls = 0x004da000\` is a masked reading rather than the widened one. The source's own comment always said so: the window \"was open for exactly CMD0's own send and poll\". A window is an INTERVAL and 783 read the scope by counting which command each FUNCTION carries. **NO NEW STORE, NO NEW REGISTER, NO NEW WIDTH, NO NEW WINDOW, NO NEW MEGABYTE**: the store COUNT through both windows is unchanged, both are still the same two stores to \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` inside the same \`#if >= 14\` and \`#if >= 16\` windows with the same restore calls, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` is still read and never written, and the enable word each window stores is PUBLISHED AS WHAT WAS WRITTEN (\`xnu_live_storage_ena_wrote\` and \`xnu_live_storage_cid_ena_wrote\` - keys that ALREADY EXIST, so this arm adds NO NEW KEY AT ALL) so that the arm's own constant is read out of the log and not inferred from the source. **783 named a window-scope check as owed and this arm is the reading that closes the scope**: at value 28 no command window in this ladder writes the one-bit word, and \`tools/report_int_enable_windows.py\` - whose assignment map was keyed by the local NAME of the word it stores, so three functions' local \`ena\` collapsed into one producer list, which is 739's shape and is repaired in this same step - now reads the five-bit word built at three sites and reaching three stores. **THIS ARM STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 28, MEASURED TWICE ON THE TWO ARTIFACTS THAT MATTER** - the value-27 payload is byte-identical to the parked \`armed-storage-d5d98738\` (\`93026ca1\`) and the value-27 entry image is byte-identical to that park's own \`xnu_arm_entry.bin\` (\`d5d98738\`) - so ONE press answers rung 28's \`_pad_raw\` four-row fork AND this arm's two windows, and rung 28 need never be pressed on its own. **AND THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE TWO ZEROS THIS ARM CHANGES WERE READINGS ABOUT A MASK, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT.** 765 showed this controller's \`INT_STATUS\` is a GATED VIEW - a status bit is visible only while its enable stands - so on the rung-28 arm \`_cmd1_status_any = 0\` and \`_cid_status_any = 0\` over their full 1.2 s windows could not have shown anything else, and \`CMD_TIMEOUT\` is a COMMAND-LEVEL bit whose absence from those counts is exactly what a one-bit mask produces. **READ EACH WINDOW'S \`_status_any\` AGAINST ITS OWN \`_any_polls\`, AS FOUR ROWS**: \`_status_any\` still 0 with \`_any_polls\` large - the block set NO bit of \`INT_STATUS\` AT ALL during that command, neither the completion nor TIMEOUT, which is rung 22's silence (764 section 4) reproduced at a window that COULD have shown a timeout; bit 15 alone - the command was issued, held the line, and the block's own response timeout fired; CRC, END_BIT or INDEX - the card answered and the answer was malformed; bit 0 alone - the completion bit is visible and the command finished. **AND CMD1 IS \`SEND_OP_COND\`**, the question 764 section 5 left open, so this is the first arm on which that question can be asked at the window that issues it rather than through a later latch. **A NEGATIVE IS READ ONLY WHERE THE LINE MOVED**, for 771's reason one window over: \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` and the \`_inhibit_seen\` cells are read BESIDE the status counts, so 'nothing was ever driven onto the bus' and 'the block drove the line and no bit of the gated view rose' stay distinguishable - and \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen = 0\` is a reading only if the sampler outran the bus, which is why \`_cmd1_polls\` and \`_cmd1_ticks\` are read beside it."
    elif [[ $wst == 29 ]]; then
      # **787: RUNG 30 IS ONE INTERVAL AND NOTHING ELSE - THE INTERVAL CMD1 HAS BEEN RUNNING IN, WITH
      # AN ENABLE THAT COULD NOT SEE IT, SINCE RUNG 14.**
      #
      # 785 widened st_cmd_path's window believing it covered CMD0 and CMD1, and rung 29's own press
      # refuted that out of the capture's line order: rung 14's restore - `_ena_wrote_back =
      # 0x00000000` - runs on the line after CMD0's publishes and BEFORE the between-commands gate, so
      # CMD1's interval has carried an INT_ENABLE that enables neither the completion nor any error
      # detail on every arm from rung 14 on. `_cmd1_status_any = 0` over `_cmd1_polls = 0x004da000`
      # (5,087,232) with `_cmd1_timeout = 1` is therefore the arm's own 1.2 s bound and nothing else:
      # a poll that cannot see the bit it polls for reports exactly its own bound.
      #
      # CMD1 is `SEND_OP_COND` - the command an eMMC must answer for this ladder to move at all, and
      # the question 764 section 5 left open. So this arm is not another reading of a register: it is
      # the first time the ladder gives that command an enable that can report anything.
      #
      # THE ACT IS TWO MORE STORES TO THE SAME `INT_ENABLE 0x34`, IN AN INTERVAL THE LADDER HAS BEEN
      # RUNNING CMD1 IN ALL ALONG: the same register, the same constant (ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD =
      # 0x000F0001, rung 23's five bits), the same width, no new address, no new megabyte, no new
      # device, and `SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38` still read and never written - so nothing here can raise a
      # line this image cannot service. The open and the close are bodies of their own, exactly as
      # rung 14's close is, which keeps `st_cmd_path`'s own device surface unchanged and keeps every
      # rung-14 clause in `build_entry.sh` true at the digit.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 787's CMD1-INTERVAL arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=29, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 29, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 30**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS RUNG 29 PLUS ONE INTERVAL, AND THE INTERVAL IS CMD1'S OWN.** 786 established, out of rung 29's own capture, that rung 14's restore stores ZERO into \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` on the line after CMD0's publishes - \`_ena_wrote_back = 0x00000000\` - so from rung 14 onward CMD1 ran with the COMPLETION BIT NOT ENABLED, and \`_cmd1_status_any = 0\` over \`_cmd1_polls = 0x004da000\` (5,087,232) with \`_cmd1_timeout = 1\` was that mask and nothing about the block or the card. **This arm opens a THIRD window between the between-commands gate and CMD1's own publishes**: the same two stores to the same \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`, the same constant rung 23 put in \`st_cmd3_noidx\`, SIGNAL_ENABLE still read and never written, and NO NEW ADDRESS, NO NEW WIDTH, NO NEW MEGABYTE, NO NEW DEVICE. The open and the close are two bodies of their own (\`st_cmd1_enable_open\`, which RETURNS the word it wrote, and \`st_cmd1_enable_restore\`, which puts that same word back) so that \`st_cmd_path\`'s own device surface is rung 14's UNCHANGED - which is what keeps the rung-14 clause group in \`build_entry.sh\` true rather than relaxed. **\`_c1_ena_wrote == _c1_ena_wrote_back\` is therefore a READING and not two evaluations of one expression.** NO NEW KEY AND NO NEW STATUS CELL: the two bodies publish only the four words that make the window itself readable - \`_c1_ena_wrote\`, \`_c1_ena_held\`, \`_c1_ena_wrote_back\` and \`_c1_ena_readback\` - and NEITHER BODY TOUCHES \`INT_STATUS 0x30\`, so what this arm changes is what the CMD1 cells the ladder ALREADY CARRIES can mean (\`_cmd1_status_any\`, \`_cmd1_err\`, \`_cmd1_complete\`, \`_cmd1_timeout\`, and \`_cmd1_stale\` / \`_cmd1_status_after\`, \`st_send_command\`'s own entrance and exit reads of \`0x30\`) and not a new reading beside them. **THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE PRESS WAS SPENT, THE FIRST OF THE FOUR ROWS IS THE ONE THAT CAME BACK, AND THE LADDER'S OWN MASK IS WHAT THE STALL WAS.** 2026-09-27 23:43:48-23:45:03 UTC, EXIT 0, 75 s gate-to-capture; ONE gate exit 0 / 606 lines; ONE runner; \`fastboot boot\` only; nothing flashed; capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung30-c1win-20260927-234510-last_kmsg.txt\` 636,238 B sha \`af710630...\`, archived by hand; \`33e80afe\` absent from both device lists. The runner's verdict is **7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD - eleven CRITERIA printed TWICE in one log**, so counting LINES gives 14/2/6 and is 756's arithmetic; the one FAIL is the known \`seam_sp\` criterion, so the press ADDED NO FAILURE. **\`_cmd1_complete = 0x1\` and \`_cmd1_status_any = 0x00000001\` - BIT 0, \`SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\`, AND NO ERROR BIT OF ANY KIND.** The poll collapsed from rung 29's \`_cmd1_any_polls = 0x004da000\` (5,087,232) to **\`0x000004d4\` (1,236)** and \`_cmd1_timeout\` went 1 to 0, so the four rows above are decided: \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` is not the row, CRC/END_BIT/INDEX is not the row, 'nothing at all' is not the row - **with all four error bits enabled the block raised NONE of them, and the 1.2 s 'timeout' every arm since rung 14 has reported was this ladder's own mask.** The window's own cells confirm it was open: \`_c1_ena_wrote = _c1_ena_wrote_back = 0x000f0001\` and \`_c1_ena_held = _c1_ena_readback = 0x000f8001\`. **AND THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THAT A MEASUREMENT**: \`_cmd0_ticks\` - the same boot, the same bus, the same clock, the one command known to complete with NO response demand - is 4,903 on rung 29 and **4,906** on rung 30, reproducing to **three ticks (0.06 per cent)**; so \`_cmd1_ticks / _cmd0_ticks = 10,275 / 4,906 = 2.094\`, and a ratio near 2 is what a command PLUS ITS 48-BIT RESPONSE looks like beside a command alone. **That is an ARGUMENT and not a proof** - two points cannot separate the frame length from the fixed overhead, and a block whose completion latency simply differs for a response-demanding word would also give a ratio above 1 - and the model is named rather than hidden. **\`0x40ff8080\` IS STILL NOT AN ANSWER, AND THE PRESS MAKES THAT STRONGER RATHER THAN WEAKER**: the word is IDENTICAL on rungs 19, 29 and 30 - including the two whose CMD1 window was CLOSED - with \`_cmd0_resp = 0x00000000\` on all three, so it is not this arm's own reading and it is not evidence that anything this arm changed reached the RESPONSE registers. What the sequence does say, and it depends on NO enable bit: \`_cmd0_resp\` is read BEFORE CMD1 and \`_cmd1_resp\` AFTER it, the image never writes those registers, so **the word entered them DURING CMD1 on every arm, seen or not** - and rung 19's own PRE-read of those same registers, which stands at the same \`0x40ff8080\`, taken before CMD3 was on the bus, is a third point on it. **THE COMMENT AT THE HEAD OF CMD1 THAT SAYS OF THIS VALUE 'the card ANSWERED' IS STILL UNSUPPORTED AND IS STILL COST-OWED**, and so is the cheapest fix the press made visible: **there is no \`_cmd1_raw_pre*\` / \`_cmd1_raw_post*\` pair around CMD1's own RESPONSE registers**, unlike CMD2's \`_cid_raw0..3\` and CMD3's raw pair, so the one measurement that would turn a shared word into a reading does not exist. **AND THE FRONTIER MOVED ONE COMMAND DOWN AND DID NOT MOVE AT CMD2**: CMD2 is BYTE FOR BYTE unchanged from rung 29 (\`_cid_status_any = 0x00028000\` = ERR|CRC at \`_cid_any_polls = 0x1\`, \`_cid_err = 0x00020000\`, \`_cid_complete = 0\`), and **CMD3 was NOT SENT on this arm where rung 29 sent it** - \`_nidx_calls = 1\` but \`_nidx_sent = 0\`, \`_nidx_word = 0\`, \`_nidx_polls = 0\` - because \`st_send_command\`'s \`CMD_INHIBIT\` guard (the driver's own \`sdhci.c:1096\` bound) returned with \`sent\` still 0, proven to be THAT return and not the latch guard below it by \`_nidx_clear_after = 0\`, which the second guard cannot produce. The block was still inhibited by CMD2 because **CMD2's inhibit tail differs between the arms with CMD2's status word identical**: \`_cid_inhibit_after\` 0 to 1 and \`_cid_inhibit_last\` \`0x01f80000\` to \`0x01f80001\`. **THE NEXT ARM'S SUBJECT is why CMD2 gets an immediate CRC while CMD1 completes**, and three things separate them and only three - response length 48 versus 136 bits, CRC off versus on, opcode - with CMD2's error at poll 1, about 0.9 microseconds after the store, in a window whose 136-bit response cannot have been shifted. **AND 786 SECTION 4 AND 787 SECTION 4 ARE SUPERSEDED AS STATEMENTS**: 786's conclusion that CMD1's row would not have moved is REFUTED, 787's candidate (b) that CMD1 could have been timing out invisibly is REFUTED, and 787's candidate (a) reached the right consequence on a mechanism 788 had already killed - the account that fits is 788 section 3's, the narrow window was BLIND, carried to the CMD1 window. **THE GOAL IS STILL NOT MET** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached, no mount is made, so TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD - and **NO FURTHER ARM IS ARMED AND NO PRESS IS AUTHORIZED**."
    elif [[ $wst == 30 ]]; then
      # **792: RUNG 31 IS ONE BIT OUT OF CMD2's FLAG WORD, AND IT IS THE LAST CORNER OF A TABLE THE
      # LADDER ALREADY HOLDS.** 791 censused every CMD2 this ladder has ever sent - nine arms carry
      # its cells - and found the immediate `ERR | CRC` on exactly the two whose CMD2 window carried
      # the CRC enable. It then isolated the correlate rather than naming a cause: the command word is
      # a property of the COMMAND and not of the enable, and the three commands the ladder has given a
      # wide enable differ in exactly one respect that matters - CMD1 (`0x0102`, 48-bit, CRC check
      # OFF) COMPLETED, CMD3 (`0x030a`, 48-bit, CRC check ON) latched `ERR | TIMEOUT` with bit 17
      # NEVER set, and CMD2 (`0x0209`, 136-bit, CRC check ON) latched `ERR | CRC` at poll 1. So the
      # correlate is the 136-BIT RESPONSE REQUEST.
      #
      # **The corner the archive has never measured is 136-bit with the CRC check off, and it is one
      # bit.** Dropping a CRC check from a 136-bit response is legal on the wire - the card returns
      # the CID either way - so an arm that completes here has TAKEN THE CID and not merely removed a
      # check, which is what makes its first row the largest single step available without leaving
      # early bring-up.
      #
      # **Written as a whole alternative rather than as a variable assigned in two arms**, so that at
      # every value below 30 the two lines of `st_all_send_cid` are the original text character for
      # character and containment is STRUCTURAL; measured anyway - the value-29 entry build from this
      # source reproduces the spent rung-30 park's `xnu_arm_entry.bin` byte for byte.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 792's ONE-CONSTANT CMD2 arm** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=30, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`) - **switch VALUE 30, which this record counts as its ORDINAL rung 31**, the two spellings being one apart (755 section 7). **IT IS RUNG 30 PLUS ONE BIT OUT OF CMD2's OWN FLAG WORD, AND THE RUNG IT MOVES FROM IS THE SPENT RUNG 30.** 791 censused every CMD2 this ladder has ever sent: nine arms carry its cells and the immediate \`ERR | CRC\` (\`0x00028000\` at the FIRST poll, \`_cid_ticks = 0x11\` = 17 ticks) appears on exactly the TWO arms whose CMD2 window carried the CRC enable. The command word is a property of the COMMAND and not of the enable, and the ladder has now given three commands a wide enable - CMD1 (\`0x0102\`, 48-bit, CRC check OFF, which COMPLETED at poll 1,236), CMD3 (\`0x030a\`, 48-bit, CRC check ON, which latched \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` with bit 17 NEVER set) and CMD2 (\`0x0209\`, **136-bit**, CRC check ON, which latched \`ERR | CRC\` at poll 1) - so **the correlate is the 136-BIT RESPONSE REQUEST and not the CRC check**, and the missing corner of that table is 136-bit with the CRC check off. **That corner is this arm.** \`st_all_send_cid\` sends \`ALL_SEND_CID\` with \`ST_MMC_RSP_R2_NOCRC\` in place of \`ST_MMC_RSP_R2\` - the same opcode, the same argument, the same window, the same position in the driver order and the same five-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` - so the word moves from \`0x0209\` to \`0x0201\` and NOTHING ELSE MOVES. **IT ADDS NO NEW ADDRESS, NO NEW WIDTH, NO NEW MEGABYTE, NO NEW DEVICE AND NO NEW KEY**: \`_cid_flags\` publishes the value and \`_cid_word\` / \`_cid_word_read\` publish the folded word, so the whole change is readable out of keys the ladder already carries. **THE WINDOW'S OWN DEVICE SURFACE DOES NOT MOVE WHEN A CONSTANT MOVES**, which is why the rung-16 clause group that reads this body out of the linked image - its device set, its counts, its program order, its empty image side - is unchanged, and why the new clause reads the FLAGS ARGUMENT at the call site instead: \`st_all_send_cid\` loads \`[r0=#2 (ALL_SEND_CID), r1=#0 (no argument), r2=#3]\` immediately before its \`bl\` to \`st_send_command\`, where the rung below loads \`#7\`, and the expected value is DERIVED FROM THE SWITCH so that a constant which leaked DOWN the ladder is refused by the same clause that catches a rung which did not take effect. **The command WORD that constant folds to is pinned on the other side of that call by a \`_Static_assert\` pair in the source**, because the clause can see the argument and not the word: one assertion on each side of the \`bl\`, and neither is a sentence in a comment. **THE WAIT RUNG 9 INTRODUCED IS INHERITED UNCHANGED HERE, WITH ITS BOUND A PART OF THIS ARM'S NAME: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_arm+=" **THIS ARM HAS BEEN PRESSED AND IS SPENT.** 2026-09-28 03:43:44 UTC, **EXIT 0**, with the operator's authorization taken BEFORE the gate was run; readiness 5 of 5 exit 0, ONE gate exit 0 / **607 lines**, ONE runner, \`fastboot boot\` only, nothing flashed, nothing written to storage, \`33e80afe\` absent from BOTH lists at fire time, and the runner's verdict **7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD COUNTED PER CRITERION** - identical to rungs 29's and 30's, so this press added no failure. Capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung31-nocrc-20260928-034344-last_kmsg.txt\` 633,586 B sha \`9c0baf80...\`, archived by hand (the runner does not archive). **IT ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER IS ROW 2: \`ERR | TIMEOUT\`.** \`_cid_flags = 0x00000003\`, \`_cid_word = _cid_word_read = 0x00000201\`, \`_cid_status_any = 0x00018000\`, \`_cid_err = 0x00010000\`, \`_cid_any_polls = _cid_polls = 0x00000705\` = 1,797, \`_cid_ticks = 0x000031e2\` = 12,770 = **665.2 us**, \`_cid_complete = 0\`, \`_cid_inhibit_seen = _cid_inhibit_after = 0\`, \`_cid_cmdlow_seen = 0x1bd\` = 445 of 1024, \`_cmd0_ticks = 0x1327\` = 4,903. "
      entry_conseq+=" **THE ARM IS ONE BIT OUT OF CMD2's FLAGS AND ITS FIRST ROW REACHES THE CARD's CID - THE LARGEST SINGLE STEP AVAILABLE FROM HERE.** CMD2 (\`ALL_SEND_CID\`, opcode 2, argument 0) goes on the bus with \`PRESENT | 136\` and the CRC flag dropped, word \`0x0201\` in place of \`0x0209\`, and dropping the CRC check from a 136-bit response is legal on the wire: the card still returns the CID and the block simply does not check it, so an arm that completes here has TAKEN THE CID and not merely removed a check. **FOUR ROWS, AND THE FIRST ONE MOVES THE LADDER PAST EARLY BRING-UP.** (1) **\`_cid_complete = 1\`** - the CID is taken and the driver order opens: CMD9 (CSD), CMD7 (select), CMD16 (block length) and then data, so the ladder stops asking what the block does with a malformed response and starts reading the card. (2) **\`ERR | TIMEOUT\` (\`0x00018000\`)** - the block armed its OWN response timeout and not the CRC check, which says the CRC latch needs the command's own CRC flag and that the real condition at CMD2 is a 136-bit response that never arrives; the subject then moves to the card and to the power/clock path 784 gave a shape to. (3) **\`ERR | CRC\` (\`0x00028000\`) ANYWAY** - which is the strongest reading this arm can return, because the CRC bit would then NOT be about the command's CRC flag at all: it would put the 136-bit path itself on the block, refuting 791 section 4's isolation with the one row that could. (4) **nothing at all over the full bound**, with the next window's own wide read as the tie-breaker. **AND THE CONTROL IS IN THE SAME LOG**: \`_cmd0_ticks\` is the same counter over the same bus in the same boot for one 48-bit no-response frame - 4,901 to 4,909 across the seven arms that carry it, a spread of 0.16 per cent - and CMD1's own completion (poll 1,236, no error bit) brackets the new word from the other end, so a completion in tens of ticks, a timeout at the bound and a CRC at poll 1 are all distinguishable without a clock frequency. **AND A NEGATIVE IS READ WHERE THE LINE MOVED**: \`_cid_cmdlow_seen\` beside the status counts, and \`_cid_ticks\` / \`_cid_polls\` beside them, keep 'the block never drove the bus' apart from 'the block drove it and no bit rose'. **AND NO CELL OF THIS ARM IS AN ANSWER FROM THE CARD UNTIL THE COMPLETION IS**: \`0x40ff8080\` stands in the RESPONSE registers on rungs 19, 29 and 30 whether the window was open or closed, so a non-zero response word is not evidence on its own - it is \`_cid_complete\` and the four-word 136-bit read beside it that are. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM ALONE** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made - and TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD until one does."
      entry_conseq+=" **AND 793 CORRECTS THAT ROW BEFORE THE PRESS: IT IS TWO ROWS AND NOT ONE, BECAUSE \`_cid_complete\` IS THE COMMAND'S LATCH AND NOT THE RESPONSE.** The four \`RESPONSE\` words are read UNCONDITIONALLY in this body (value 16, \`entry_storage.c:3341-3366\`, no \`if\` in the block), and 793 censused every arm that carries them: NINE arms - seven narrow, two wide enough to report an error - hold \`_cid_raw0..3\` = \`0x0040ff80 / 0x80000000 / 0 / 0\` and \`_cid_resp0..3\` = \`0x40ff8080 / 0 / 0 / 0\`, BYTE FOR BYTE THE SAME ON ALL NINE. A 128-bit CID cannot have three of its four words zero, so **the ladder has never read a CID and completing the command is not by itself what would give it one**. Read the row as TWO: **1a** \`_cid_complete = 1\` AND the register block CHANGED - any of \`_cid_resp0\` off \`0x40ff8080\`, or \`_cid_raw2\`, \`_cid_raw3\` or \`_cid_resp1..3\` off ZERO, all of which have been zero on every arm this ladder has ever run - means **THE CID IS TAKEN** and the driver order opens (CMD9 CSD, CMD7 select, CMD16 block length, then data); **1b** \`_cid_complete = 1\` with the nine-arm pattern UNCHANGED is a FIFTH ROW AND A STRONGER ONE THAN 1a - the block completed a 136-bit command and its response register file NEVER FILLED - which separates the completion latch from the response path and puts every \`_cid_complete\` reading on this ladder under suspicion, 790 section 1's CMD1 row and its 2.094 ratio included. **AND THE BLOCK IS NOT A DEFAULT**: \`st_resp_before\` reads the same four offsets in the same arithmetic immediately before \`st_cmd_path\`, and on the three arms that carry both bodies its log line stands FIRST - rung 30: \`_rb_raw0 = 0\` at 8674, \`_cmd0_resp = 0\` at 8777, \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\` at 8827, \`_cid_raw0 = 0x0040ff80\` at 8876 - so the register was ZERO when the ladder arrived and everything it later holds the block put there itself. **The value of this press went up; its cost did not.**"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE PRESS WAS SPENT AND IT ANSWERED: ROW 2, \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` - AND WHAT IT REFINES IS BIGGER THAN THE ROW.** The immediate CRC that made this rung the frontier is **GONE**: the latch moved \`ERR | CRC\` (\`0x00028000\`) at poll **1** in **17 ticks** to \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` (\`0x00018000\`) at poll **1,797** in **12,770 ticks** - **751 times longer**. So **the immediate CRC needs BOTH conjuncts**: a 136-bit request AND the CRC check in the command word. 791 section 4 named the length and missed that the flag is necessary - CMD3 carries the flag and never latched bit 17, and this arm does not either. **AND THE 136-BIT COMMAND WAS ACTUALLY DRIVEN THIS TIME**: \`_cid_cmdlow_seen\` moved \`0\` -> \`445 of 1024\`, where on rung 30 the CRC landed before the command went on the wire, and \`_cid_inhibit_after\` moved \`1\` -> \`0\`, so CMD2 no longer wedged and **CMD3 WAS SENT** (\`_nidx_sent = 1\`) for the first time since rung 19. **12,770 TICKS IS 665.2 us, AND THAT IS A CONSTANT**: CMD3 in the same boot reads \`_nidx_polls = 1,793\`, \`_nidx_ticks = 12,772\` = **665.3 us**, \`_nidx_status_any = 0x00018000\`, \`_nidx_err = 0x00010000\` - the same bound to within 0.02 per cent, and the same bound rung 24 measured. **AND THE REGISTER THAT SETS IT HAS NEVER BEEN WRITTEN**: \`grep 'ST_SDHCI_TIMEOUT_CONTROL'\` returns the define and ONE READ; \`_nidx_tout_ctl = 0x00000000\`, the reset value, the SHORTEST the register can express - because the vendor writes it only from \`sdhci_prepare_data\` inside \`if (data || (cmd->flags & MMC_RSP_BUSY))\`, and every command this ladder sends is data-less. **THE TIMING LADDER**: CMD0 255.4 us, **CMD1 535.2 us and COMPLETED**, CMD2 and CMD3 **665.2 us and timed out**. CMD1's 48-bit response arrives **130 us inside the bound** - the narrowest margin in the ladder, and why CMD1 is the one command that completes. **AND THE ARITHMETIC, ASSUMPTION NAMED**: one 48-bit response added 5,373 ticks; a 136-bit response is 88 bits more, so at the same per-bit rate it lands 9,850 ticks after CMD1's - **20,126 ticks = 1,048.2 us against a 665.2 us bound**, which is 63 per cent. The rate that implies is 5.83 us/bit = **171.5 kHz** against rung 6's configured 400 kHz, and **this step does not explain that**; the conclusion survives either rate (at 400 kHz the answer still needs 875 us). **WHY CMD3 FAILS IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION**: its 48-bit response should land at CMD1's 535.2 us and it does not, so this is not timing - the claim offered is the protocol's own, that a card enters IDENT only by accepting CMD2 and CMD3 is only accepted in IDENT, so **CMD2 has never completed and the card has never been listening**. Named as a claim, not measured, and falsifiable by the next arm. **SO THE NEXT ARM IS THE FIRST REPAIR THIS LADDER HAS HAD: ONE BYTE STORE to \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` above 0, before CMD2** - \`0x01\` doubles the bound to 1,330.4 us, \`0x02\` to 2,660.8 us - with a NUMERIC prediction behind it: if the arithmetic is right the CID arrives in the four words the ladder already reads (\`_cid_raw0..3\`, \`_cid_resp0..3\`), and on the protocol claim CMD3 answers too. \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` again at a LONGER tick count refutes the arithmetic and says by how much. **AND IT EXPLAINS 786 section 7's ANOMALY**: the CRC reported at poll 1 of a window whose response cannot have been shifted was never a report about the card - it is what this block raises when handed a 136-bit request with its CRC check enabled. **AND \`0x40ff8080\` IS STILL NOT AN ANSWER**: identical on rungs 19, 29, 30 and 31, including one where the block drove the bus and timed out. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached, no mount is made - **TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD**. "
      entry_conseq+=" **AND 794 CORRECTS WHAT ROW 1a BUYS, AND THE CORRECTION IS A MEASUREMENT OF THE TREE RATHER THAN A READING OF A CAPTURE.** 792 section 7 wrote that row's consequence as 'the driver order opens: CMD9 (CSD), CMD7 (select), CMD16 (block length), then data' and 793 section 5 kept it. **That is true of the Linux MMC driver and FALSE OF THIS IMAGE.** The image's entire command vocabulary is FOUR opcodes - \`grep -cE '^#define ST_CMD_OP_' src/entry/entry_storage.c\` is \`4\`, and they are opcodes 0, 1, 2 and 3: CMD0, CMD1, CMD2, CMD3. **There is no CMD9 define at all**, no body, and no call site. \`st_cmd_path()\` is called EXACTLY ONCE and the only thing after it in \`entry_storage_probe\` is \`st_pwr_irq_after()\` and then a return, so the sequence is CMD0, CMD1, CMD2, CMD3 and THE FOURTH IS THE LAST. **AND THERE IS NO DATA PATH**: anchored and case-SENSITIVE, \`grep -rnE '\\b(BLOCK_SIZE|BLOCK_COUNT|TRANSFER_MODE|ADMA|SDHCI_DATA|SDHCI_BLOCK|DMA_ADDRESS)\\b' src/ --include=*.c --include=*.h\` returns TWO lines and both are comments, one of them the source's own 'no data-path register at all'. \`ST_SDHCI_DMA_ADDRESS 0x00\` is defined and is READ TWICE as a version/identity word, never as a transfer address. **SO A PERFECT CID AT THIS RUNG DOES NOT MOVE THE LADDER ONE COMMAND CLOSER TO A MOUNT.** Row 1a is still worth the press - it is the last item this sequence asks for and it settles the 136-bit question - but its consequence was overstated, and the distance to 「挂载存储」 is TWO frontiers: the CMD2 bit, and THE BREADTH GAP no step had named, being the bodies past CMD3 (CMD9, CMD7, CMD16) and a data path behind them. **The next BUILD is about the first body past CMD3, not another bit at CMD2.**"
    elif [[ $wst == 31 ]]; then
      # **796: RUNG 32 IS THE FIRST REPAIR THIS LADDER HAS EVER MADE, AND IT IS ONE BYTE IN ONE
      # REGISTER.** 795 read rung 31's press and found the frontier is not CMD2 and not the card: it is
      # `TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E`, WHICH NO RUNG HAS EVER WRITTEN. `grep` returns the define and ONE READ,
      # and rung 31's own capture read the register back as `_nidx_tout_ctl = 0x00000000` - the reset
      # value and the shortest bound the register can express - because the vendor writes it only from
      # `sdhci_prepare_data` (`sdhci.c:827-828`), inside `if (data || (cmd->flags & MMC_RSP_BUSY))`,
      # and EVERY command this ladder sends is DATA-LESS. So the 665.2 us that CMD2 and CMD3 both hit,
      # and that CMD1 completes 130 us inside, is this block's own default and not a property of the
      # card. This arm raises it to `0x03` for the CMD2 window alone and puts it back afterwards.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 796's STORAGE arm, THE FIRST REPAIR ANY RUNG OF THIS LADDER HAS MADE** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=31, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is 792's arm with ONE ACT ADDED AND NO MEASUREMENT REMOVED: the same CMD0 and CMD1, the same \`st_all_send_cid\` with the SAME word \`0x0209\` (rung 31 sent \`0x0201\` and this arm does NOT), the same five-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`, the same \`st_cmd3_noidx\` - plus TWO BODIES of its own, \`st_tout_open\` (\`0x8000de60\`) and \`st_tout_restore\` (\`0x8000e250\`), whose ENTIRE device surface is \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` read and written AS A BYTE, one below \`SOFTWARE_RESET 0x2F\` and one above \`CLOCK_CONTROL 0x2C\`, with the wait's bound inherited from rung 9 and written into this arm's name: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THIS ARM HAS BEEN PRESSED AND IT IS SPENT, AND ITS ANSWER REFUTED THE MECHANISM IT WAS BUILT ON.** 2026-09-28 08:12-08:13 UTC, **EXIT 0**, with the operator's authorization taken BEFORE the gate was run; readiness 5 of 5 exit 0, ONE gate exit 0 / **608 lines**, ONE runner, \`fastboot boot\` only, nothing flashed, nothing written to storage, no reboot; \`33e80afe\` absent from BOTH lists; capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung32-tout-20260928-081329-{last_kmsg.txt,gate.log,run.log}\`, archived by hand, last_kmsg 633,759 B sha \`0e33bbcd...\`; **the machine stayed up** (\`_post_end_calls = 0x7\`, \`_post_elapsed = 6004.8 ms\` at 19.2 MHz, so the 6000 ms deadline fired) and the runner's verdict is **7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD COUNTED PER CRITERION**, identical to rungs 29, 30 and 31, so this press added no failure. **THE REPAIR RAN AND IS VISIBLE**: \`_tout_was = 0\` (the register held ZERO, the reset value, exactly as predicted), \`_tout_held = 3\` (the block held what was written), \`_tout_wrote_back = 0\`, \`_tout_readback = 0\`. **AND THE BOUND DID NOT MOVE BY ONE TICK-TIME**: rung 31 read \`_cid_ticks = 0x31e2\` = 12,770 under the 665.2 us reset bound; THIS arm raised \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` to \`0x03\` = 5,321.6 us, **EIGHT TIMES the bound**, and read \`_cid_ticks = 0x31e4\` = **12,772 = 665.3 us**, with \`_cid_status_any = 0x00018000\` (\`ERR | TIMEOUT\`) at poll 1,797 on both arms and \`_cid_complete = 0\`. **So \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` DOES NOT GOVERN THIS EVENT** - the specification's timeout register scales the DATA timeout and the command response timeout is fixed in the block - and **795 section 5's extrapolation is UNTOUCHED AND UNTESTED**, because the card is not answering at all and no response ever raced the bound. It is the pre-registered refutation row in the STRONGER form: 796 section 8 named a LONGER tick count, and the SAME count refutes the MECHANISM rather than the arithmetic. **AND IT IS A CLEAN ONE-VARIABLE COMPARISON, MEASURED**: rung 31 carries 481 storage keys and this one 487, the six extra being the \`_tout_*\` cells, and every other cell is identical to within timer jitter. **AND THE FINDING THE PRESS MADE AVAILABLE IS NOT ABOUT THE REGISTER AT ALL: CMD1 IS SENT ONCE WHERE THE DRIVER LOOPS, AND THE GATE READS THE DRIVER'S OWN CONDITION BACKWARDS.** \`mmc_ops.c:141-166\` is \`for (i = 100; i; i--)\` with \`mmc_delay(10)\` between passes, exiting on \`if (cmd.resp[0] & MMC_CARD_BUSY) break;\` at \`:157\` - **the driver PROCEEDS WHEN BIT 31 SETS**. This ladder's \`entry_storage.c:4702\` is a SINGLE \`st_send_command(...SEND_OP_COND...)\` with no loop, and \`:4785\` is \`(c1.resp & ST_MMC_CARD_BUSY) == 0u\` - **bit 31 CLEAR proceeds**. And the measurement puts the ladder on the WRONG SIDE: \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\` has bit 31 CLEAR, so **the driver's loop would have retried 99 more times** - the ladder proceeded (\`_cid_gated = 0\`). **So CMD2 goes on the bus for a card that has never reported power-up complete, and the card does not answer** - that is what the 665.2 us is. It also explains 795 section 6 one step earlier than it said: CMD1 IS answered, because a card answers CMD1 WHILE it initializes, which is what the retry loop is for. The caveat is named: \`0x40ff8080\` may not be a real OCR at all (790 section 5), and the next arm is sound either way. **AND 796's OWN PROSE IS REFUTED BY THIS PRESS**: it claimed value 31 sends word \`0x0209\` and that rung 31 sent \`0x0201\` \"and this arm does NOT\" - the capture reads \`_cid_word = 0x0201\`, because \`entry_storage.c:3328\`'s guard is \`>= 30\` and rung 32 INHERITED rung 31's CRC-dropped word, making it a strict superset. **The clause family passed and the prose lied**: \`xnu_entry_792\` asserts the flags are \`#3\` and is itself guarded \`>= 30\`, so nothing compared the sentence against the arm. **AND ONE OWED ITEM IS NOW PAID IN THE READING**: this arm's row 4, \"\`ERR | CRC\` returning refutes 795 section 2\", could not have fired, because the CRC flag was not carried. **THIS PRESS BUYS A NUMBER AND NOT A DIRECTION, WHICH IS WHY THE PREDICTION IS ARITHMETIC AND CAN BE WRONG BY A MEASURED AMOUNT.** 795's timing ladder - one boot, one counter - reads CMD0 at 255.4 us, CMD1 at 535.2 us and COMPLETED, CMD2 and CMD3 at 665.2 and 665.3 us and TIMED OUT. The register's field is a power of two, so \`0x03\` is **5,321.6 us: 8.0x the 665.2 us bound this ladder has always run under, 5.08x the 1,048.2 us that 795 section 5's extrapolation puts a 136-bit response at (the per-bit rate ASSUMED, and 795 names the assumption), and 225x UNDER the driver's own 1.200 s poll bound** - the other end, because at or above \`0x0B\` the ladder's own poll gives up before the device timeout could fire and the cell stops meaning anything. Those two ends are on the constant at COMPILE TIME, in two \`_Static_assert\`s. **FOUR ROWS, AND THEY ARE THE ONES 795 section 7 PRE-REGISTERED.** \`_cid_complete = 1\` with \`_cid_raw0..3\` or \`_cid_resp0..3\` MOVED off the nine-arm pattern (\`0x0040ff80 / 0x80000000 / 0 / 0\` and \`0x40ff8080 / 0 / 0 / 0\`) is **THE CID TAKEN** - 793 section 1's row 1a, and a 128-bit CID cannot have three of four words zero; \`_cid_complete = 1\` with that pattern UNCHANGED is 793 section 5's fifth row, now on an arm where the bound is not the suspect; **\`ERR | TIMEOUT\` AGAIN, AT A LONGER TICK COUNT, REFUTES 795's ARITHMETIC AND SAYS BY HOW MUCH** - the one row that is a measurement of a number rather than of a state; and \`ERR | CRC\` RETURNING refutes 795 section 2 and puts the immediate CRC back on the block independent of the bound. **AND THE CONTROL IS IN THE SAME LOG**: \`_cmd0_ticks\` (4,901 to 4,909 across seven arms, 0.16 per cent) and CMD1's own completion at poll 1,236 bracket the new number from both ends, so a completion in tens of ticks, a timeout at a raised bound and a CRC at poll 1 are distinguishable without a clock frequency. **THE WINDOW'S OWN KEYS ARE THE REPAIR MADE VISIBLE**: \`_tout_calls = 1\` (the open ran), \`_tout_was\` (the byte found - EXPECTED 0, the reading that says no rung and no earlier consumer left this register set), \`_tout_wrote = 3\`, \`_tout_held = 3\` (the readback), \`_tout_wrote_back = 0\` and \`_tout_readback = 0\`. **\`_tout_was = _tout_held = 0\` WOULD BE THE ONE FAILURE THE REST OF THE LOG CANNOT SHOW** - every other cell would read exactly as rung 31's did - which is why the clause \`xnu_entry_796\` asserts the store's OFFSET, its WIDTH and its VALUE out of the linked image rather than trusting the source. **AND CMD3 STAYS AT THE RESET BOUND ON PURPOSE**: its 48-bit response fits inside 665.2 us, so it is the INDEPENDENT TEST of 795 section 6 - a card enters IDENT only by accepting CMD2, and CMD3 is only accepted in IDENT. **The falsification is named: if CMD2 completes and CMD3 STILL times out, 795 section 6 is wrong.** **THIS ARM IS ARMED AND NOT PRESSED, AND THE PRESS IS THE OPERATOR'S**; \`out/\` holds it, its park is \`out/stage90/frozen/armed-storage-c1f89600/\` and its value-30 containment is TEN OF ELEVEN MEMBERS byte-identical to the spent rung-31 park. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made - so **TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD**."
    elif [[ $wst == 32 ]]; then
      # **800: RUNG 33 IS THE FIRST RUNG OF THIS LADDER WHOSE ACT IS THE DRIVER'S OWN CONTROL FLOW
      # RATHER THAN ONE OF ITS CONSTANTS.** 798 read rung 32's press and found the frontier is not the
      # block's timeout register and not the card: it is the COMMAND SEQUENCE. `mmc_ops.c:141-166` is
      # `for (i = 100; i; i--)` with `mmc_delay(10)` between passes, exiting on `mmc_ops.c:157`'s
      # `if (cmd.resp[0] & MMC_CARD_BUSY) break;` - THE DRIVER PROCEEDS WHEN BIT 31 SETS - and
      # `entry_storage.c:4702` sends CMD1 ONCE with argument 0 while `:4785` proceeds when bit 31 is
      # CLEAR. `_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080` has bit 31 clear, so the driver's loop would have retried 99
      # more times and this ladder went on to CMD2. **So CMD2 has been going on the bus since rung 16
      # for a card that has never reported power-up complete**, which is what the 665.2 us is.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 800's STORAGE arm, THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE ACT IS THE DRIVER'S OWN CONTROL FLOW RATHER THAN ONE OF ITS CONSTANTS** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=32, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is the rung below with the driver's TWO MISSING CMD1 STEPS PORTED: the same CMD0, the same \`st_all_send_cid\`, the same five-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\`, the same \`st_tout_open\`/\`st_tout_restore\` window, the same \`st_cmd3_noidx\` - plus TWO BODIES of its own, \`st_op_cond_arg\` (\`0x8000de18\`, 96 bytes) and \`st_op_cond_loop\` (\`0x8000de78\`, 320 bytes), BOTH \`noinline, noclone\`, EACH CALLED ONCE FROM \`st_cmd_path\`, and BOTH WITH AN EMPTY DEVICE SET read out of the linked image rather than asserted from the source - so this rung adds NO STORE TO ANY REGISTER, no new address, no new width and no new key of any device register, and the ONLY act it has that touches the block is the command it sends, which belongs to \`st_send_command\` whose own body and whose own \`st_cmd_path\` call count (exactly two) are unchanged; with the wait's bound inherited from rung 9 and written into this arm's name: $wpt_txt$wpt_which"
      entry_conseq+=" **THE READING THIS ARM ACTS ON IS 798 section 5's, AND 799 SECTION 2 SHOWED THE IMAGE HAS NEITHER OF THE DRIVER'S TWO MISSING STEPS.** The driver's own order is \`mmc.c:1923\` \`mmc_send_op_cond(host, 0, &ocr)\` - argument 0, and by \`mmc_ops.c:148-150\`'s \`if (ocr == 0) break;\` a SINGLE PASS BY THE DRIVER'S OWN DESIGN - then \`mmc.c:1951\` \`host->ocr = mmc_select_voltage(host, ocr)\`, then \`mmc.c:1359\` \`mmc_send_op_cond(host, ocr | (1 << 30), &rocr)\`, THE LOOPED CALL. Anchored and case-sensitive over \`src/entry/\`: \`select_voltage\` occurs ONCE AND IN A COMMENT with no define, no body and no call; the CMD1 argument is the literal \`0u\`; and \`1u << 30\` occurs once in the whole file as \`ST_HC_DLL_RST\` (\`:258\`, a different register) - **so bit 30 has never been set in any command word this image sends.** **THE ACT IS THE DRIVER'S, IN THE DRIVER'S ORDER**: derive \`(c1.resp & ~0x7F) | (1 << 30)\` from the probe's own response (\`mmc.c:1943-1948\`'s \`ocr &= ~0x7F\` and \`mmc.c:1359\`'s bit 30), then send that argument up to \`mmc_ops.c:145\`'s 100 times with \`mmc_ops.c:164\`'s 10 ms between passes, EXITING WHEN BIT 31 SETS. Every one of those four constants - \`#127\`, \`#1073741824\`, \`#100\`, \`#101\` - is asserted as an immediate IN THE LINKED BODIES by \`xnu_entry_799\`, and so is the EXIT TEST'S DIRECTION: exactly one sign-conditioned branch (\`blt\`/\`bmi\`) leaves the loop, which is what \`(resp & 0x80000000) != 0\` compiles to, because that direction is 798 section 5's whole subject and a differently-lowered test refuses the build for a human to read. **IT DOES NOT PORT \`mmc_select_voltage\`**: the card's own OCR passes through the driver's mask and bit 30 with no intersection against the host's windows, because the image has no representation of the host's available windows at all and inventing one is a different arm from porting a control flow - and on this card the two derivations COINCIDE, so the choice is not load-bearing for the press. "
      entry_conseq+=" **EIGHT NEW KEYS, AND TWO OF THEM ARE THE ARM.** \`_opcond_calls\` (the argument body ran), \`_opcond_from\` (the word it derives from), \`_opcond_arg\` (the word it derived - THE ARM'S ONLY NEW NUMBER), \`_opcond_loop_calls\` (the loop ran), \`_opcond_sends\` (**HOW MANY SENDS IT TOOK, 1..100** - 799 section 3a named this as the one cell the ladder does not already carry), \`_opcond_busy_seen\` (**WHETHER BIT 31 EVER SET** - \`mmc_ops.c:157\`'s own test, run more than once in a boot for the first time in this ladder's life), \`_opcond_last_resp\` (the last response word) and \`_opcond_ticks\` (the loop's own cost). **AND THE PREDICTION IS A NUMBER RATHER THAN A DIRECTION, WHICH IS SHARPER THAN ANY RUNG BEFORE IT**: \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\` already has bit 30 SET and its LOW SEVEN BITS ZERO, so \`(0x40ff8080 & ~0x7F) | (1 << 30) == 0x40ff8080\` - **THE DERIVATION IS A NO-OP ON THIS CARD'S OWN WORD AND \`_opcond_arg\` SHOULD EQUAL \`_opcond_from\` EXACTLY**; a value that differs says the derivation moved something the response did not already satisfy, which is the only way this rung can report on its own first body at all. **THE COST IS NAMED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED**: the worst case is 100 sends plus 99 ten-millisecond delays, and at the measured 665.2 us per timed-out command that is ABOUT 1.06 s ADDED TO THE STORAGE PROBE, spent inside \`entry_storage_probe\` at the OS's idle-exit where the run's own deadline is 6,000 ms (\`_post_elapsed\`) - the first rung of this ladder whose cost is a second of BUS TIME rather than a bounded number of reads, and \`_opcond_ticks\` is the cell that measures what it actually cost. "
      entry_conseq+=" **FOUR ROWS, AND THE FIRST IS THE ONE THAT WOULD OPEN THE LADDER.** (1) \`_opcond_busy_seen = 1\` on send \`k <= 100\` - **THE CARD FINISHES POWER-UP AND THIS LADDER HAS BEEN SENDING CMD2 TOO EARLY SINCE RUNG 16**, and \`k\` is a number no arm has carried. (2) \`_opcond_busy_seen = 0\` over the FULL 100 SENDS - the card is not powering up on this path at all, which puts the subject on **POWER AND CLOCK** (784's rails are RPM resources with no \`reg\`) and **NOT** on the command sequence. (3) \`_opcond_last_resp\` **DIFFERS** between sends - the block is reading something that MOVES with each CMD1, which separates a real OCR from a static pattern WITHOUT needing to know which it is. (4) \`_opcond_last_resp\` **BYTE-IDENTICAL** every send and equal to \`_cmd1_resp\` - \`0x40ff8080\` is a static pattern and 790 section 5's discount extends to every arm. **AND THE CONTROL IS STILL IN THE SAME LOG**: \`_cmd1_resp\` and \`_cmd1_resp_busy\` are published by the rung below unchanged - \`_cmd1_resp_busy\` has read \`0x00000000\` on ALL TWELVE occurrences across the whole archive, and on the success row it becomes \`1\` FOR THE FIRST TIME, which is what makes \`_opcond_busy_seen\`'s own reading checkable against a key the ladder already had. **IT DOES NOT REPAIR \`entry_storage.c:4785\`** - the gate's direction is still inverted against the line it cites - and that is deliberate: this rung makes the gate's PREMISE measurable and does not move it, so the press says whether the loop ever sees bit 31 before anything is built on it. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made - so 「让os可以正常启动并且挂载存储」 is not reached and **TWRP-TO-STORAGE STAYS WITHHELD**. **THIS ARM WAS ARMED AND NOT PRESSED WHEN THIS PARAGRAPH WAS WRITTEN, AND IT HAS SINCE BEEN PRESSED - THE PRESS IS RECORDED IN THE NEXT PARAGRAPH AND IT IS SPENT**; \`out/\` held it, its park is \`out/stage90/frozen/armed-storage-ce2f589c/\` with eleven members verified 11 ok / 0 failed, and its value-31 containment is TEN OF ELEVEN MEMBERS BYTE-IDENTICAL to the spent rung-32 park \`armed-storage-c1f89600\`, the eleventh being the entry sources record - exactly two of its 28 lines, the two files this step touched."
      entry_conseq+=" **THIS ARM HAS BEEN PRESSED AND IT IS SPENT, AND IT ANSWERED: THE CARD FINISHES POWER-UP ON THE SECOND CMD1 AND THE CID COMES BACK.** 2026-09-28 12:30:03-12:31:20 UTC, **EXIT 0** (77 s), with the operator's authorization taken BEFORE the gate was run; readiness 5 of 5 exit 0, ONE gate exit 0 / **609 lines**, ONE runner, \`fastboot boot\` only, nothing flashed, nothing written to storage, no reboot; \`33e80afe\` absent from BOTH lists at fire time; capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung33-opcond-20260928-123120-{last_kmsg.txt,gate.log,run.log}\`, archived by hand, last_kmsg 637,213 B sha \`6fe60a12...\`; **the machine stayed up** (\`_post_end_calls = 0x8\`, \`_post_cntfrq = 0x0124f800\`) and the runner's verdict is **7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD COUNTED PER CRITERION**, identical to rungs 29, 30, 31 and 32, so this press added no failure. **THE HEADLINE IS ROW 1.** \`_opcond_calls = 0x1\`, \`_opcond_from = 0x40ff8080\`, **\`_opcond_arg = 0x40ff8080\` - EQUAL to \`_opcond_from\`, THE DERIVATION IS A NO-OP ON THIS CARD'S OWN WORD EXACTLY AS PRE-REGISTERED** - \`_opcond_loop_calls = 0x1\`, **\`_opcond_sends = 0x2\`** (THE LOOP EXITED ON SEND TWO; the 100-send bound was never approached), **\`_opcond_busy_seen = 0x1\` - BIT 31 SET, THE CARD REPORTED POWER-UP COMPLETE** - and \`_opcond_last_resp = 0xc0ff8080\` (\`0x40ff8080 | 0x80000000\`: **the same OCR the probe read, with the busy bit now set**), costing \`_opcond_ticks = 0x340ff\` = 213,247 = **11.107 ms**. **THE ARITHMETIC IS EXACT**: 213,247 minus \`mmc_ops.c:164\`'s \`mmc_delay(10)\` (192,000 ticks) leaves 21,247 ticks = **1,106.6 us FOR TWO COMMANDS, about 553 us each**, both inside the block's 665.2 us window - which is why both completed. **The record named 1.06 s as the worst case; what it actually spent is 1.107 ms, 958x less.** **AND CMD2 ANSWERED: THE CID COMES BACK AFTER NINE ARMS OF SILENCE.** \`_cid_complete = 0x1\` (was \`0x0\` on every arm since rung 16), \`_cid_status_any = 0x00000001\` (**bit 0 \`SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\` AND NO ERROR BIT OF ANY KIND**, against \`0x00018000\` \`ERR|TIMEOUT\`), \`_cid_err = 0x0\`, \`_cid_ticks = 0x49e2\` = 18,914 = **985.1 us** (the command really ran, against the 665.2 us of the block's own timeout), \`_cid_inhibit_after = 0x0\` (no longer wedged - it was \`0x1\` on rung 30). **THE FOUR RESPONSE WORDS MOVED OFF THE NINE-ARM PATTERN IN EVERY WORD**: \`_cid_resp0..3\` = \`0x45010053 / 0x44573136 / 0x47014a2f / 0xe00bb100\` against the nine-arm \`0x40ff8080 / 0 / 0 / 0\`, and the \`_cid_raw_*\` family is **THE SAME SIXTEEN BYTES SHIFTED ONE BYTE**, so two independent readings agree. **IT DECODES AS A REAL CID**: MID \`0x45\` = 69 = **SanDisk**, OID \`0x01 0x00\`, **PNM \`SDW16G\`** (six ASCII characters - the spec's product-name field), PRV \`0x01\`, PSN \`0x4a2fe00b\`, MDT \`0xb1\`. **SO 793 SECTION 1's ROW 1a IS MET AND 793 SECTION 5's FIFTH ROW IS REFUTED IN THE SAME EVENT**: the completion latch fired AND the response file filled. **It also retires 790 section 5's and 787 section 6's caveat** - \`0x40ff8080\` was the card's OCR all along, with bit 31 clear because the card had not finished power-up; the word did not change between rungs, its MEANING did. **AND CMD3 ANSWERED TOO**: \`_nidx_complete = 0x1\`, \`_nidx_status_any = 0x00000001\`, \`_nidx_err = 0x0\`, \`_nidx_ticks = 0x2b22\` = 11,042 = **575.1 us**, and **\`_nidx_resp = 0x00000500\` - THE RCA IS \`0x0500\`** - with \`_nidx_resp_pre = 0x2fe00bb1\` (the CID's tail) replaced by it. **795 section 6's protocol claim is CONFIRMED in the direction it predicted**, and this press supplies the CAUSE it was missing: CMD3 failing was always a CONSEQUENCE of CMD2 failing, and neither command was the problem - **the card was not ready, and CMD1 is where readiness is asked for.** **THE READING IS ONE SENTENCE**: every arm from rung 16 through rung 32 was sending CMD2 and CMD3 to a card that had not finished power-up, and the ladder's own gate is why. \`entry_storage.c\`'s CMD2 gate tests **THE PROBE's** response (\`c1.resp\`, the single CMD1 with argument 0) where the driver tests **THE LOOP's** (\`mmc_ops.c:157\`). **THE GATE IS NOT MERELY INVERTED; IT IS READING THE WRONG WORD** - the probe is a single pass by the driver's OWN design (\`mmc_ops.c:148-150\`'s \`if (ocr == 0) break;\`), so its response says nothing about readiness. **THE REPAIR IS ONE LINE AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: gate CMD2 on the loop's result. **The 665.2 us 'timeout' that nine rungs chased was never a timeout at all** - it is what a correctly configured block reports when the card is not listening. **AND THE SAFETY CONTRACT HELD CELL BY CELL**: nothing flashed, nothing written to storage, no reboot (the gate's own last line is \`booted, never flashed, so no outcome of this run can write to storage.\`); \`xnu_live_storage_writes\` 0 -> 4 is the same four inherited mode-sequence stores every arm from rung 3 carries and **NO NEW STORE OF ANY KIND**, rung 33's own two bodies having an EMPTY DEVICE SET asserted out of the linked image by \`xnu_entry_799\` before the press; the 11.107 ms of bus time is the whole of this arm's new cost. **AND ONE DIFFERENCE THIS RECORD DOES NOT EXPLAIN, NAMED RATHER THAN GLOSSED**: \`_post_end_calls\` moved \`0x7\` -> \`0x8\` and \`_post_elapsed\` last \`0x06df35b6\` (6.005 s) -> \`0x092a857e\` (8.009 s), one more post-end call and about two more seconds than every arm since rung 24 - the storage probe's own delta is 11.107 ms and cannot account for 2 s. **OWED A CELL, NOT A SENTENCE.** **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM**: no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made, and 794 section 1's BREADTH GAP is untouched and is now the whole distance (four opcodes, no CMD9 define, no data path) - so 「让os可以正常启动并且挂载存储」 is not reached and **TWRP-TO-STORAGE STAYS WITHHELD**. **AND NO ARM IS ARMED AND NO PRESS IS AUTHORIZED**: rung 33 is spent and the next build's first item is the gate repair this press measured."
    elif [[ $wst == 33 ]]; then
      # **804: RUNG 34 IS THE FIRST RUNG OF THIS LADDER WHOSE ACT IS A CONDITION RATHER THAN A BODY.**
      # 803 measured the cost of the old one: every arm from rung 16 through rung 32 put CMD2 and CMD3 on
      # the bus at a card that had not finished power-up, because the CMD2 gate tested the PROBE's
      # response - a single pass by the driver's own design (`mmc_ops.c:148-150`) - where the driver
      # tests the LOOP's (`mmc_ops.c:157`). The gate was not inverted; it was reading the WRONG WORD.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 804's STORAGE arm, RUNG 34, THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE ACT IS A CONDITION RATHER THAN A BODY - THE CMD2 GATE GIVEN THE WORD THE DRIVER ACTUALLY TESTS** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=33, resolved set \`armed-storage-7341f5f6\`), which is the rung below with ONE EXPRESSION CHANGED AND NOTHING ELSE MOVED: \`c1.sent != 0u && (c1.resp & ST_MMC_CARD_BUSY) == 0u\` becomes \`c1.sent != 0u && op_busy != 0u\`, where \`op_busy\` is the value \`st_op_cond_loop\` has returned since rung 33 and which the rung below computes and throws away with a \`(void)\` cast. The two arms call the same bodies, in the same order, at the same place in the driver's own sequence; what changes is WHICH WORD DECIDES WHETHER THE SECOND OF THEM RUNS. with the wait's bound inherited from rung 9 and written into this arm's name: \`STAGE90_XNU_PWR_WAIT_TICKS=384000\`, 20 ms at this device's own 19,200,000 Hz **AND IT ADDS NO STORE, NO ADDRESS, NO WIDTH, NO MEGABYTE, NO DEVICE AND NO KEY OF ANY DEVICE REGISTER**: the rung-33 arm's two bodies keep their EMPTY device sets, \`st_cmd_path\`'s device surface is rung 14's unchanged, and \`st_send_command\` is still called exactly twice from it. **THE PRESS THAT MEASURED IT IS IN THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE**: \`_opcond_sends = 2\` with \`_opcond_busy_seen = 1\` and \`_opcond_last_resp = 0xc0ff8080\` against \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\` - THE SAME OCR DIFFERING BY EXACTLY THE BUSY BIT - and then \`_cid_complete = 1\` with a real SanDisk \`SDW16G\` CID. The two words are the answers to two different questions and the gate was asking the one the driver does not."
      entry_conseq+=" **RUNG 34 IS A REPAIR THAT IS SUPPOSED TO CHANGE NOTHING ON THIS CARD, AND THAT IS WHY ITS ROWS ARE ABOUT THE REPAIR ITSELF.** 803 measured BOTH conditions TRUE on the same boot - the probe's word has bit 31 clear and the loop saw it set - so the two gates agree here and the only thing a press can show is that the ladder still reaches the CID through the corrected one. **TWO NEW CELLS, PUBLISHED ON BOTH PATHS, ARE THE REPAIR MADE READABLE**: \`_cid_gate_word\` is the word the gate tested, read out of the LOOP's own result struct, and \`_cid_gate_busy\` is the test's own result printed beside its source, the shape \`_cmd1_resp_busy\` already has four lines above. On a row where the card became ready \`_cid_gate_word\` differs from \`_cmd1_resp\` by exactly the busy bit; on a row where the loop never saw the card out of reset the two are EQUAL and \`_cid_gated\` is 1 - **which is the row the old gate could not produce at all**. \`c1.sent != 0\` is KEPT so \`_cid_gated_reason\` still separates 1 (CMD1 never reached the bus) from 2 (it did, and the loop never saw the card out of reset); reason 2's MEANING is what moves. **FOUR ROWS.** (1) \`_cid_gated = 0\` with \`_cid_gate_busy = 1\`, \`_cid_gate_word = 0xc0ff8080\` and every rung-33 cell reproduced (\`_cid_complete = 1\`, the SanDisk CID, \`_nidx_resp = 0x00000500\`) - **THE EXPECTED ROW: the repaired gate reaches the same place and the repair is behaviour-preserving on this card**, which is what makes it safe to build CMD9 on. (2) \`_cid_gated = 1\` with \`_cid_gated_reason = 2\` - the loop never saw bit 31 this time, so the repair CHANGED the outcome and the card's readiness is NOT reproducible boot to boot; that row makes the fix load-bearing rather than tidy, and it is also the first arm that can tell the two apart. (3) \`_cid_gate_word\` EQUAL to \`_cmd1_resp\` - the loop's struct is being read at the wrong moment or the two cells are one word wearing two names, which is the m-class this project keeps catching. (4) \`_cid_gate_busy = 0\` beside \`_cid_gated = 0\` - **IMPOSSIBLE BY CONSTRUCTION** (the gate tests exactly that flag) and therefore a refutation of this arm's own claim rather than a reading. **AND THE CONTROL IS IN THE SAME LOG**: \`_cmd1_resp\`, \`_cmd1_resp_busy\` and \`_opcond_*\` are all published by the rungs below unchanged, so the two words can be compared in one capture without trusting this paragraph. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made, and 794 section 1's breadth gap (four opcodes, NO CMD9 DEFINE, no data path) is the whole distance; CMD9's \`MMC_RSP_R2\` is the next command in the driver's order and the breadth gap's first body, and THIS rung is what makes it reachable. So 「让os可以正常启动并且挂载存储」 is not reached and **TWRP-TO-STORAGE STAYS WITHHELD**. **THIS ARM IS ARMED AND NOT PRESSED, AND THE PRESS IS THE OPERATOR'S**; \`out/\` holds it, its park is \`out/stage90/frozen/armed-storage-7341f5f6/\` with eleven members verified 11 ok / 0 failed, and its value-32 containment is TEN OF ELEVEN MEMBERS BYTE-IDENTICAL to the spent rung-33 park \`armed-storage-ce2f589c\`, the eleventh being the entry sources record - exactly two of its 28 lines, the two files this step touched."
    elif [[ $wst == 34 ]]; then
      # **812: RUNG 35 IS THE DRIVER'S FIRST COMMAND ABOVE CMD3.** 809 counted that it needs NO NEW
      # MECHANISM - its R2 flags, its 136-bit assembler and its RCA argument all exist because CMD2
      # built them. What this rung had to settle before it could be built is the ARGUMENT: **an MMC
      # card does not return an RCA**, so CMD9's argument is the constant the DRIVER assigns. The
      # paragraph is per-rung because it is about what THIS arm does to THIS card.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 812's STORAGE arm, RUNG 35, THE DRIVER'S FIRST COMMAND ABOVE CMD3 AND THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE ARGUMENT IS A VALUE THE DRIVER ASSIGNS RATHER THAN A WORD THE CARD RETURNED** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=34, VALUE 34 = ORDINAL RUNG 35 - the switch counts the VALUE and captures and commit subjects count the ARM - resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is the rung below plus ONE NEW COMMAND BODY AND ONE NEW CALL TO IT: \`st_send_csd\`, guarded \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 34\`, puts the driver's own word \`0x0909\` - opcode 9, \`RESP_LONG 0x01 | CRC 0x08\`, and NO \`INDEX\` bit, because \`MMC_RSP_R2\` is PRESENT|136|CRC - on the bus with argument \`ST_MMC_RCA_1\` and flags \`ST_MMC_RSP_R2\`, opens and closes the same five-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` window every command body above it opens, and assembles the 136-bit response in the driver's word-3-first order (\`sdhci.c:1167\`'s \`(3-i)*4\`). \`st_cmd_path\` calls it exactly ONCE, on the line below rung 21's CMD3, and the ladder's own bound moves \`> 33\` to \`> 34\`. **IT MOVES NO BODY THE RUNG BELOW RUNS AND IT ADDS NO STORE TO ANY DEVICE THE RUNG BELOW ALREADY REACHES**: the new body's device surface is the same \`INT_ENABLE\`, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE\`, \`RESPONSE\`, \`INT_STATUS\` and \`PRESENT_STATE\` the arms above it use, and every one of its three argument words was read out of the LINKED IMAGE before this sentence was written - r0=9, r1=65536, r2=7. \`STAGE90_XNU_PWR_WAIT_TICKS=384000\` is inherited from rung 9 unchanged - 20 ms at this device's own 19,200,000 Hz - and it is written here because **\`STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE\` names the RUNG and this key names WHICH ARM AT THAT RUNG** (715 section 5). **AND IT CARRIES A CORRECTION TO THE ARCHIVE**: CMD9's argument is \`card->rca << 16\` with \`card->rca = 1\` ASSIGNED BY THE DRIVER ITSELF nine lines above the CMD3 that carries it (\`mmc.c:1400\`), because CMD3's response is R1 - the 32-bit card status, \`R1_CURRENT_STATE\` at bits 12:9 and \`R1_READY_FOR_DATA\` at bit 8, with NO RCA FIELD AT ANY OFFSET - so the value this ladder's archive has been calling \"RCA 0x0500\" was read out of \`_nidx_resp = 0x00000500\`'s bits 15:0, a field that exists in neither response format (an SD card returns one in R6, and even there the field is bits 31:16).** **AND THIS ARM IS THE 814 REPAIR OF THIS RUNG'S FIRST BUILD.** Rung 35 was built, parked as \`armed-storage-6cd6fae8\` and NEVER PRESSED, and 814 found before the press what the press would have found the hard way: the body's six CSD decodes applied the shifts and masks of \`UNSTUFF_BITS(resp, start, size)\` to the four RAW \`RESPONSE\` registers, and \`resp[]\` in that macro is the array \`sdhci_finish_command\` ASSEMBLES - \`resp[i] = (raw_i << 8) | (raw_{i+1} >> 24)\` - so every field came out ONE BYTE TOO HIGH and \`_csd_structure\`/\`_csd_mmca_vsn\`, the two cells this rung's outcome table turns on, were read off the one byte the assembler SHIFTS OUT. The four assembled words are now bound to locals \`a0..a3\` and the six decodes read THOSE; the published \`_csd_resp*\` cells are UNCHANGED IN VALUE, NO SWITCH MOVED, and \`xnu_arm_entry-config.txt\` differs from the withdrawn arm's in exactly one line - the artifact's own hash. **THE WITHDRAWN ARM IS NOT AN ARM TO PRESS - it is kept in \`out/stage90/frozen/\` as the record of what was built.** \`tools/check_response_word_order.py\` gained rule 4 for the class: a right shift of a raw response word is now a BUILD REFUSAL, offset 0 exempt because the vendor's short-response branch reads it with no shift at all."
    elif [[ $wst == 35 ]]; then
      # **819: RUNG 36 IS THE DRIVER'S OWN NEXT COMMAND, AND IT IS THE FIRST WORD IN THIS LADDER
      # THAT ASKS THE BLOCK TO CHECK AN OPCODE ECHO.** `mmc.c:1436`'s `err = mmc_select_card(card);`
      # is the statement immediately after `mmc.c:1420`'s CMD9, and `mmc_ops.c:37` gives it
      # `MMC_RSP_R1 | MMC_CMD_AC` - flags 21, `PRESENT | CRC | OPCODE`, whose third bit
      # `sdhci.c:1140-1143` maps onto `SDHCI_CMD_INDEX` `0x10`. **That bit is the one rung 21
      # removed from CMD3 and has not sent since**, on the evidence of the rung-19 press, and 817
      # read that press and the whole archive and measured that CMD1 had never completed on any arm
      # before rung 30 - so the hang that justified the removal was a dead chain and the deviation
      # was inherited from an artifact. Putting it on THIS command and not back on CMD3 is one rung
      # moving one thing: CMD3's word stays `0x030A` in the same boot, and `_nidx_*` beside `_sel_*`
      # is then the two words compared on one bus. **AND 819 CORRECTS ITS OWN RECORD HERE TOO**:
      # `MMC_RSP_R1` is the flag word `0x15` and NOT `0x031A`, which is CMD3's command word; CMD7's
      # command word is `0x071A`, and the compiler refused the first draft of the source's own
      # `_Static_assert` for exactly that conflation.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 819's STORAGE arm, RUNG 36, THE DRIVER'S OWN NEXT COMMAND AND THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE WORD ASKS THE BLOCK TO CHECK AN OPCODE ECHO** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=35, VALUE 35 = ORDINAL RUNG 36 - the switch counts the VALUE and captures and commit subjects count the ARM - resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is the rung below plus ONE NEW COMMAND BODY AND ONE NEW CALL TO IT: \`st_select_card\`, guarded \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 35\`, puts \`mmc_ops.c:33\`'s \`MMC_SELECT_CARD\` (opcode 7), \`mmc_ops.c:36\`'s \`card->rca << 16\` (\`ST_MMC_RCA_1\`) and \`mmc_ops.c:37\`'s \`MMC_RSP_R1 | MMC_CMD_AC\` (flags 21) on the bus, opens and closes the same \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` window every command body above it opens, and decodes the R1 fields off its own response through \`st_send_command\`'s result struct. **IT MOVES NO BODY THE RUNG BELOW RUNS AND IT ADDS NO STORE TO ANY DEVICE THE RUNG BELOW ALREADY REACHES**: the new body's device surface is the same \`RESPONSE\`, \`INT_ENABLE\`, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE\`, \`INT_STATUS\`, \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL\` and \`PRESENT_STATE\` the arms above it use, and every one of its three argument words was read out of the LINKED IMAGE before this sentence was written - r0=7, r1=65536, r2=21. **AND THIS RUNG'S CALL IS UNGATED, WHICH NO OTHER COMMAND'S IS.** Every gate below it is a statement about the command BELOW (\`cid_sent\`); CMD7's precondition is the card's state after CMD3 and this file has no cell that measures it - the register read before CMD7 holds the last 32 bits of the CSD, which is not an R1 - so a gate there would be the m812/m819 defect installed as a control-flow decision. The reading is the command's own R1, and \`_sel_pre_cid_sent\` publishes what a gate WOULD have read. **IT IS SAFE BECAUSE OF WHAT CMD7 IS**: \`ac [31:16] RCA R1\`, no data phase, no storage write and no register this image can reach - one 48-bit frame on CMD, the same shape as the CMD3 four lines up that this ladder sends on every press. \`STAGE90_XNU_PWR_WAIT_TICKS=384000\` is inherited from rung 9 unchanged. **AND IT CARRIES FIVE OWED CITATION REPAIRS**, free under this arm because the COST reason that deferred them has expired: \`sdhci.h:162\` for \`HOST_CONTROL2\`, the five \`mmc.c\` lines of the CSD field table, and 813's four \`sdhci.c:1162-1175\` sites - **all comment-only, and MEASURED so**: the entry bin rebuilt after them is byte-identical."
    elif [[ $wst == 36 ]]; then
      # **822: RUNG 37 IS A COMMAND THAT CHANGES NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ITS DESIGN.**
      # CMD13 `SEND_STATUS` (`mmc.h:42`, `ac [31:16] RCA R1`) is `mmc_ops.c:470-485`'s
      # `mmc_send_status`, and its flag word is **NOT rung 36's**: `mmc_ops.c:479` sets
      # `MMC_RSP_SPI_R2 | MMC_RSP_R1 | MMC_CMD_AC` = `0x80 | 0x100 | 0x15` = **`0x195` = 405**, where
      # CMD7's was 21. `sdhci.c:1140-1143` reads exactly five bits out of a flag word and
      # `MMC_RSP_SPI_S1`/`MMC_RSP_SPI_S2` (`core.h:40`, `:41`, `:70`) are none of them, so the two
      # numbers give ONE command word, `0x0D1A` - asserted in the source, asserted again against
      # `r2` in `build_entry.sh`'s `xnu_entry_822`, and read off the bus as `_sta_word`. The rung is
      # CMD13 because CMD7's own answer could not settle whether CMD7 landed: this command reads the
      # same field without being able to move the card.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 822's STORAGE arm, RUNG 37, THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE COMMAND CANNOT CHANGE WHAT IT READS AND THE FIRST WHOSE FLAG WORD IS THE DRIVER'S SPI FORM RATHER THAN THE LADDER'S** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=36, VALUE 36 = ORDINAL RUNG 37, resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`). **ITS WHOLE ACT IS ONE 48-BIT COMMAND FRAME AND ONE RESPONSE, on a controller whose command path this ladder has been running since rung 11** - no data phase, no store to any device CMD7's own body did not already touch, and its device surface is byte-for-byte CMD7's (\`RESPONSE\` read four times on each side of the command, the \`INT_ENABLE\` window opened and closed, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE\`, \`INT_STATUS\`, the \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL\` byte *read*, \`PRESENT_STATE\`). **FOUR ROWS AND EVERY ONE NAMES THE NEXT ACT**: \`_sta_state = 4\` (TRAN) with \`_sta_err = 0\` is CMD7 LANDED and the frontier is the DATA PATH; \`_sta_state = 3\` (STBY) again is CMD7 DID NOT LAND and the next arm moves SOMETHING ELSE (the measured candidate is the \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL\` byte, still \`0x00\` on every press); \`_sta_illegal = 1\` is THE CARD REFUSING CMD13 ITSELF; \`_sta_complete = 0\` with \`CMD_INHIBIT\` asserted is a hang, and \`_sta_err\` carrying \`SDHCI_INT_INDEX\` is a frame whose opcode is not 13. \`STAGE90_XNU_PWR_WAIT_TICKS=384000\` is inherited from rung 9 unchanged."
    elif [[ $wst == 37 ]]; then
      # **824: RUNG 38, AND IT IS THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE COMMAND HAS A DATA PHASE.** The paragraph this
      # branch prints is read by the arm-naming row below, which asks two things of it: that it quote
      # the rung the entry record carries (`STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=37`), and that every backticked
      # `_name` in it be the suffix of a `xnu_live_` string this image actually publishes - a name the
      # image cannot print is what a rename makes of prose (698's census; `_mode_hci_version` named
      # `xnu_live_storage_mode_dma_address`). Both keys were read out of the parked ELF before this
      # sentence was written, not remembered: the `_ext_` set is 80 keys and every name below is from
      # it.
      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 824's STORAGE arm, RUNG 38, THE LADDER'S FIRST DATA-PHASE COMMAND AND THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE CENTRAL NUMBER IS A DATA-PHASE BIT RATHER THAN A FLAG** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=37, VALUE 37 = ORDINAL RUNG 38 - the switch counts the VALUE and captures and commit subjects count the ARM, the two spellings one apart - resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`), which is the rung below plus ONE NEW COMMAND BODY AND ONE NEW CALL TO IT: \`st_send_ext_csd\`, guarded \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 37\`, puts \`mmc_ops.c:263\`'s \`SEND_EXT_CSD\` (opcode 8) with NO ARGUMENT AT ALL (\`mmc_ops.c:263\`'s \`struct mmc_command cmd = {}\`) and \`mmc_ops.c:263\`'s \`MMC_RSP_SPI_R1 | MMC_RSP_R1 | MMC_CMD_ADTC\` (flags 181) on the bus, then reads a 512-byte payload out of the block's PIO window. **IT IS THE FIRST ARM THIS LADDER HAS PRESSED WHOSE WINDOW MOVES A MULTIPLE OF ITS OWN CLOCK AND THE FIRST WHOSE WORD CARRIES \`SDHCI_CMD_DATA\`**: the five-bit \`sdhci_cmd_to_flags\` mapping of 181 gives byte \`0x1A\` (word \`0x081A\`), and \`sdhci.c:1146-1149\` ORs \`SDHCI_CMD_DATA 0x20\` when \`cmd->data != NULL\` (\`core.c:338\`), so the word the block receives is \`0x083A\` - and \`st_send_command\` ITSELF gained the conditional OR for it under this rung's guard, because the branch below this one passes flags whose ADTC bit is clear and the change is an identity there. **THE GATE, AND IT IS THE FIRST GATED COMMAND IN FOUR RUNGS**: the body re-reads \`RESPONSE 0x10\` at decision time and refuses with \`_ext_gated=1\`/\`_ext_done=0\` unless \`R1_CURRENT_STATE\` (bits 12:9, \`_ext_gate_state\`) is 4 (TRAN) - the state the rung-37 press measured - so the sequence gate/argument/word the three gates below use is repeated here against a value the last press produced rather than one this ladder assumed. **AND THE TIMEOUT IS COMPUTED FROM RUNG 35's CARRIED CSD AND NOT RE-DERIVED FROM \`RESPONSE\`** (m814 rule 4): the four assembled words are carried in \`st_csd_words[4]\` with \`st_csd_words_valid\` beside them, the body does NOT open on \`if (!st_csd_words_valid)\` but on walking them, and the arithmetic is the driver's own \`sdhci_calc_timeout\` over \`TAAC\` and \`NSAC\` (\`_ext_csd_tacc_ns\`, \`_ext_csd_tacc_clks\`, \`_ext_csd_carried\`) doubled to the first count that covers it and capped at \`_ext_tout_max\` because \`SDHCI_QUIRK2_USE_RESERVED_MAX_TIMEOUT\` is set at \`sdhci-msm.c:2904\`. **THE PIO PATH IS IN THE VENDOR'S OWN ORDER**: the \`RESPONSE\` gate read first, then \`HOST_CONTROL 0x28\` (\`_ext_host_control_pre\`), then the window opened on \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` and closed after the command, \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` written BEFORE \`BLOCK_SIZE 0x04\` (\`sdhci.c:827-828\` above \`sdhci.c:831\`; \`_ext_tout_was\`, \`_ext_tout_held\`, \`_ext_tout_readback\`, \`_ext_tout_restore\`), \`BLOCK_SIZE 0x04 <- 0x7200\` (\`_ext_blksz\`, \`_ext_blksz_held\`) and \`BLOCK_COUNT 0x06 <- 1\` (\`_ext_blkcnt\`, \`_ext_blkcnt_held\`) and \`TRANSFER_MODE 0x0C <- 0x0012\` (\`_ext_trns\`, \`_ext_trns_held\`) each written as a halfword and read back, then ARGUMENT and COMMAND, then 128 32-bit reads of \`BUFFER 0x20\`, then the window's close, \`INT_STATUS 0x30\` (\`_ext_int_status_end\`, \`_ext_int_data_avail\`, \`_ext_int_data_end\`) and \`PRESENT_STATE 0x24\` (\`_ext_ps_end\`). **IT MOVES NO BODY THE RUNG BELOW RUNS AND ADDS NO MEGABYTE**: the new body's device surface is the controller's own, already mapped since rung 14, and every one of its argument words was read out of the LINKED IMAGE before this sentence was written - r0=8, r1=0, r2=181, the word it STORES \`0x083A\` asserted, and the SAME FIVE-BIT MAPPING of 181 asserted to be \`0x081A\` and refused as a stored word by a second clause on the same body. **714's CLASS IS REFUSED HERE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS**, which is why two clauses assert two different immediates on two otherwise identical bodies. \`STAGE90_XNU_PWR_WAIT_TICKS=384000\` is inherited from rung 9 unchanged - 20 ms at this device's own 19,200,000 Hz. **NO PRESS HAS BEEN MADE AND NONE IS OWED: this arm is PARKED and NOT ARMED-FOR-PRESS, and the park is a RECORD and not a queue.**"
    elif [[ $wst == 38 ]]; then
      # **825: RUNG 39, AND ITS WHOLE CONTENT IS ONE IMMEDIATE IN AN OR.** The paragraph this branch
      # prints is read by the arm-naming row below, which asks two things of it: that it quote the rung
      # the entry record carries (`STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=38`), and that every backticked `_name` in
      # it be the suffix of a `xnu_live_` string this image actually publishes - a name the image cannot
      # print is what a rename makes of prose (698's census). Every `_ext_` name below was confirmed
