      entry_arm="$entry_arm - **and this is NOT any of those arms either: it is 851's STORAGE arm, RUNG 52 - THE GPT HEADER DECODED OUT OF THE SECTOR THE SECTOR-1 READ ALREADY LANDED** (STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE=51, VALUE 51 = ORDINAL RUNG 52 - the switch counts the VALUE and the arm counts the RUNG, one apart - resolved set \`${SET:-<unresolved>}\`). **IT IS THE RUNG-51 PRESS'S OWN FRONTIER, AND IT ADDS NO COMMAND, NO REGISTER, NO WINDOW AND NO BYTE OF THE MEDIUM.** The rung-51 press (850) measured \`_rd_w0 = 0x20494645\` (\"EFI \") and \`_rd_w1 = 0x54524150\` (\"PART\") with \`_rd_gpt = 1\` - **LBA 1 IS A GPT HEADER** - so the 512 bytes are ALREADY in \`st_read_block\`. **Rung 52 is a body that DECODES that buffer and nothing else**: \`st_gpt_parse\`, called ONCE after \`st_read_single_block\`, which makes NO device access at all - a fact the build clause makes STRUCTURAL (every \`bl\` in its body is to \`entry_live_write\`, and a body naming the controller window at all is REFUSED). **THE ONE FIELD THE FRONTIER NAMES IS \`PartitionEntryLBA\`** - the GPT header's byte offset 72, word 18 (\`72/4\`), the SECTOR the 128-byte partition ENTRY ARRAY lives in. That number is the NEXT arm's CMD17 argument, so **rung 52 has the shape of 50 -> 51: only the sector moves again.** **READ THE ROWS AS THE ARM'S OWN TABLE.** \`_gp_sig_ok = 1\` is **THE SECTOR IS A GPT HEADER AND THE NUMBERS BELOW ARE A HEADER** - the module re-checks \`0x20494645\`/\`0x54524150\` ITSELF rather than trusting rung 51's derived cell, so \`_gp_sig_ok = 0\` beside \`_rd_complete = 1\` is a sector the walk refused to treat as a GPT header (a stale or gated read left the buffer holding something else) and the numbers are then NOT a header. \`_gp_my_lba\` is the header's OWN SELF-CHECK against the sector this rung read (sector 1): \`_gp_self_ok = 1\` is **the header agrees with the address the read used** - a second producer agreeing with the decode, from a field the HEADER carries rather than one the MB