    entry_conseq+=" **THIS ARM READS A BLOCK THE IMAGE HAS NEVER TOUCHED, SO ITS ANSWER IS A READING AND ITS HAZARD IS A BUS WAIT.** The reads are gated and the gate is published: \`xnu_live_storage_gate\` is \`BIT(0)\` of SDCC1's CBCR (\`0xFC4004C4\`, in a megabyte \`0xFC4\` **and 692 WAS PRESSED, so this is now measured rather than inferred: that megabyte is NOT mapped, and the probe's third line faulted on it.** \`xnu_live_sleh_far_frame=0xfc4004c0\` with \`fsr_frame=0x5\` (a section translation fault, on a read), \`pc=0x8000d0c4\` = \`entry_storage_probe+0xcc\` = \`ldr r1,[r3,#1216]\` with \`r3=0xfc400000\` (692's BCR read), and the panic's own \`r0=0x80487fc8\` is the pointer to the string \`xnu_live_storage_bcr\`. **That \`r0\` is a reading of 692's entry image and NOT of this one, and the difference is a defect a reader can be caught by**: the string pool moved three keys, so \`0x80487fc8\` is \`xnu_live_storage_gcc_section\` in this arm's image and a fault at the same instruction here would print the leftover \`r0=0x80488168\` (\`_ttbr1\`, the last key written before the gate's loads are scheduled). **The \`pc\` identifies the site; a data abort's \`r0\` is a leftover register.** **The parenthetical here used to read \"in a megabyte \`entry_epilogue\`'s own PS_HOLD store proves mapped\", and that proof took a STORE inside a code path for evidence an ADDRESS is mapped** - the class of \`mi4-a-device-address-can-be-right-and-undereferenceable\`, whose citation is 532, the experiment this arm's plan came from. It was contradicted by the previous arm's own parked log, unjoined until 692: 690's fatal abort is \`pc=0x8047b488\` = \`entry_seam_end_run+0xc\`, the \`str r3,[r2,#0x65c]\` with \`r2=0x0fa00000\` (RESTART_REASON), \`far_frame=0x0fa0065c\` - **the forced ending faulted on its FIRST store** and never reached the \`dsb\`, the PS_HOLD store or the \`wfe\`, so no arm has been observed to complete a PS_HOLD store. **So read this arm's press in the order the probe runs and stop where the log stops.** The five keys 692 got - \`_calls=1\`, \`_live_state=1\`, \`_section=0x00000f98\`, \`_hc_mem_section=0x00000f98\` and \`_windows_share_section=1\` - are still published first and are still the same readings (both windows really are in one 1 MB section, so 532 section 6's step 2 is one install); then, on 693's arm only, the GATE's install publishes \`_gcc_map\`, \`_gcc_slot_before\` and \`_gcc_desc\`, and \`_gcc_section\`/\`_gcc_share_section\` were published with the five; then the controller's install publishes \`_map\`, \`_slot_before\`, \`_desc\`, \`_l1\`, \`_l1_moved\`, \`_ttbr0\` and \`_ttbr1\`; then \`_gate_read\` with \`_bcr\`, \`_cbcr\` and \`_gate\`. **A \`_gcc_map\` of 0 is the cell no earlier arm could have**: it says the GATE's megabyte could not be installed, and on that reading none of \`_bcr\`/\`_cbcr\`/\`_gate\` is published at all, so the run answers 692's fault without answering the controller. **The three end cells are told apart by three keys and not by two, because the two installs come first**: \`_gcc_map=0\` (the gate's megabyte is absent and nothing was read through it - 692's fault, with the reason in \`_gcc_slot_before\`/\`_gcc_desc\`), \`_map=0\` with \`_gate_read=1\` (the gate was read and the controller's own section refused), and \`_gated_out=1\` with \`_loads=0\` (both sections are installed, the gate was read and is closed, and the block was deliberately not touched). A \`_loads=6\` with \`_writes=0\` is the run this arm exists for. **\`xnu_live_storage_writes=0\` is the arm's own safety reading and not a comment**: the vendor's own probe opens with three writes (\`CORE_HC_MODE <- 0\`, \`CORE_SW_RST\`, \`HC_MODE_EN\`) and 531 section 8 adds a fourth reason not to - writing 0 to \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\` **is** a bus-off request - so this arm's answer is bought with loads alone. **The two pre-registered cells are the bits 531 section 6 left open**: \`xnu_live_storage_mode_bit\` (\`CORE_HC_MODE & HC_MODE_EN\`) reads 1 if the block the bootloader handed over is already in SDHCI mode - the mode sequence is then a *re-do* - and 0 if it is still ahead of us, and \`xnu_live_storage_sw_rst\` (\`CORE_POWER & CORE_SW_RST\`) reads 1 if something is holding the core in reset, which would make the version word's answer meaningless rather than absent. A \`_mci_version\` of 0 or 0xffffffff is *the block did not answer*, which is a reading of the same arm and not a failed run. **The negative cell is a NON-RETURN (exit 2) and it is this arm's own hazard, named here rather than discovered**: a load from a block whose clock is off is a bus wait nothing ends, the run does not come back, and it owes a power press - which is why the gate read exists and why the exit-2 cell means \`the gate was READ, said the branch was on, and the block still did not answer\`, not \`the mapping failed\` (a mapping failure with a live gate is a translation fault and comes back with a log - **and 692 measured WHICH address faults first, which is why this arm installs the GATE's megabyte before it reads anything**: the fault was the gate's own \`0xfc4004c0\` and not the controller's \`0xf9824000\`, and the readiness cell that named the controller's address **named the wrong site while getting the shape right**. 693's arm is that one change - \`entry_mmio_section\` for \`0xFC4\` ahead of the read, at an L1 index of its own so 532 section 3.2's occupied-slot refusal cannot fire, and the storage block's install moved ahead of the read too so a gated-out run still publishes its four numbers - and the same question then applies to the reset path's own two stores, \`0x0fa0065c\` (faulted on 690's arm) and \`0xfc4ab000\` (in this arm's \`0xFC4\` megabyte, so \`_gcc_map=1\` beside a readable \`_bcr\` is also the measurement that store becomes reachable)**). **The ending is 690's clock and it did not move**, so \`xnu_live_post_end_calls\` is still this arm's own ending cell and every consequence above about the elapsed pair holds unchanged; the probe's records precede it because the call is placed before the clock's block."
    if [[ $wst == 2 || $wst == 3 || $wst == 4 || $wst == 5 || $wst == 6 || $wst == 7 || $wst == 8 || $wst == 9 || $wst == 10 || $wst == 11 ]]; then
      # **The correction is the rung-2 AND rung-3 arm's, because the sentence it corrects is the shared
      # rung-1 one and a rung-3 arm carries it too** - `_loads=6` with `_writes=0` is the rung-1 arm's
      # run, and every rung from 2 on writes. (Measured on the 698 press's own readiness: while this
      # guard read `$wst == 2` alone, a rung-3 arm got the rung-1 sentence with no correction beside it,
      # which is the same defect one paragraph over - a correction that is not carried to the arm it is
      # true of is a sentence the reader is left with.)
      entry_conseq+=" **AND ONE SENTENCE ABOVE IS THE RUNG-1 ARM'S AND NOT THIS ONE.** \"A \`_loads=6\` with \`_writes=0\` is the run this arm exists for\" reads, on THIS arm, as **\`_loads=6\`, then the refusal cell OR four stores**: \`_writes\` is published as the sequence goes (1 after \`CORE_HC_MODE <- 0\`, 2 after \`CORE_POWER <- |CORE_SW_RST\`, 3 after \`HC_MODE_EN\`, 4 after \`|FF_CLK_SW_RST_DIS\`) with \`_mode_stage\` beside it (0 at entry, 2, 4, 8), so a run that stopped mid-sequence is localised by the pair and not by where the log ends. **So \`xnu_live_storage_writes=0\` is NOT this arm's safety reading; these are:** (1) the four stores are the ONLY stores in the whole body - \`build_entry.sh\` refuses the build unless the offsets on the controller's base are exactly \`0x78/0x00/0x78/0x78\` in that order and every other non-\`sp\` store is an offset-0 pointer write, so \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\` (in the OTHER window, \`hc_mem\`, where writing 0 **is** a bus-off request on this SoC) is unreachable by construction rather than by review; (2) the sequence refuses itself before any store unless the block answered a read - \`_mci_version\` of 0 or 0xffffffff gives \`_mode_refused=1\` with \`_mode_stage=0\` and \`_writes=0\`, the only cell in which this arm writes nothing. **The failure path is pre-registered and it is a cell of its own**: \`_mode_timeout=1\` with \`_writes=2\` and \`_mode_stage=4\` says the poll expired on \`CORE_SW_RST\` and the arm made **no further store** - the bound is 1,920,000 CNTVCT ticks = 100 ms at the 19,200,000 Hz this device reported, with \`_mode_rst_ticks\` recording what the reset actually took (the vendor's own comment says ~40 us). **And the arm's question is answered by \`_mode_bit_after\`**: 1 means \`CORE_HC_MODE\` now carries \`HC_MODE_EN\` where 694 measured it clear (\`_mode_bit=0\`, the reading that made this sequence a prerequisite), with \`_mode_w2_read=0x00002001\` as the vendor's own end state bit for bit; \`_mode_dma_address\`/\`_mode_capabilities\` re-read in SDHCI mode (697s arm and captures name the first of those two \`*hci_version\` - same offset, same read, renamed by 698 after \`sdhci.h:27\`; **this line was the FOURTH reader of that name and 698s rename census counted three**, because the census greps the full \`xnu_live_storage_\` name and this prose writes the abbreviated form - which is why the naming row below now checks every name this narration writes against the keys the image actually publishes) are the first spec-valid look at that register file (694's pair was taken through a block **not** in SDHCI mode, so a differing pair is the mode change made visible and an identical one is the finding that this bit alone does not move those two words); and \`_mode_pwrctl_status\` is the hazard this arm names rather than manages - the vendor's comment says the reset may latch a power-IRQ status when the previous state was BUS_ON, which \`_core_power=0x441\` says it was. **The negative cell is a NON-RETURN (exit 2) and it is this arm's own, for the reason the bound cannot answer**: the tick bound bounds the POLL, and a load from a block whose clock is off is a bus wait nothing ends - which is why the gate is read first and why \`_gate_read\`/\`_bcr\`/\`_cbcr\` are published before any store. A non-return refutes none of the cells above; it means the gate was wrong, or the block did not tolerate the reset. **Nothing else about the run changed**: the same operation, the same ending on the same 690 clock, the same key order with the eleven \`_mode_*\` keys last."
    else
      # **776: AND THE SAME CORRECTION FOR EVERY VALUE THE GUARD ABOVE DOES NOT COVER.** That guard was
      # widened once already (a rung-3 arm was getting the rung-1 sentence with no correction beside it)
      # and it stops at 11, so values 12..27 carried the shared paragraph's "a \`_loads=6\` with
      # \`_writes=0\` is the run this arm exists for" with NOTHING beside it - leaving three separate
      # \`rung_para\` calls (24, 25 and 27) to disclaim it in their own words, which is the shape this
      # file's own \`rung_para\` comment names: a correction that is not carried to the arm it is true of
      # is a sentence the reader is left with. **The \`else\` is the repair** - the correction now travels
      # with the sentence for EVERY value instead of for a range somebody remembered to extend.
      entry_conseq+=" **AND ONE SENTENCE ABOVE IS THE RUNG-1 ARM'S AND NOT THIS ONE.** \"A \`_loads=6\` with \`_writes=0\` is the run this arm exists for\" is the rung-1 arm's run and its cells are its own; **this arm's record carries no \`_loads\` and no \`_writes\` pair at all**, so that sentence is the record of what rung 1 asked rather than a reading of the log this readiness describes."
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 3 ]]; then
    rung_para 3 " **AND THE RUNG-2 SENTENCES ABOVE HOLD FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED - ITS ADDITION IS THAT IT READS, AND THE READS ARE WHAT THIS PRESS BUYS.** Every cell the arm pre-registered is filled by 699's press, and three of them are what make the NEXT step cheap rather than discovered: (1) \`_reg_power_control\` is **the before-value the driver's own \`sdhci_reset(SDHCI_RESET_ALL)\` needs** - bit 0 is \`BUS_POWER\`, and on this press it read 0x00, so the standard block does not believe the bus is powered and the reset's own \`sdhci_writeb(host, 0, SDHCI_POWER_CONTROL)\` is harmless by construction rather than by argument (698 section 2's branch, and the publisher of that reset must read this register back); (2) \`_reg_pwrctl_mask\` answers **the half a status register cannot** - 697's \`_mode_pwrctl_status\` of 0 means the power IRQ did not latch, and the mask (0x0000000f on the press) is what says one would have been routed, so the two together read NOTHING LATCHED WHILE THE MASK WAS ARMED rather than NOTHING WAS LISTENING; (3) \`_mode_host_version\` is **the register 697 section 3 proved the image was not reading** - 0x00001102 is spec 2 and vendor 0x11, and the vendor's own driver branches on that nibble against \`SDHCI_VER_100\` 0x2B (\`sdhci-msm.c:2909-2914\`), so a value that is not 0x2B means the \`SDHCI_QUIRK2_SLOW_INT_CLEAR\` branch is not this hardware's. **The widths are a property of the artifact and not a style**: for a rung of 3 or more \`build_entry.sh\` refuses the build unless every access in the probe whose base is not \`sp\` has a plain immediate offset satisfying its own width's alignment, because an unaligned access to a Strongly-ordered device section FAULTS on ARMv7 - 692's abort class, reached by alignment instead of by translation. **AND THE SHARED SENTENCE ONE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS CORRECTED FOR THIS RUNG TOO**: \`_loads=6\` and \`_writes=4\` are BOTH present on a completed run of this arm, with the census's \`_reg_loads=10\` behind them, so the rung-1 sentence's \`_writes=0\` is not a cell of this arm's log at all. **What is NOT new here**: no store, no command, no sector read, nothing mounted - the four device stores are still the whole write set and \`_reg_loads=10\` is the count of the reads, so a \`_reg_*\` key in this log is a survey of registers and never an act on the medium; and a 696 or 697 capture carries NO \`_reg_*\` key at all, because the block did not exist in those arms. **The ending is 690's clock and it does not move**, the census running after the mode sequence and before the clock's block, so a run that ends at the ending still carries all of \`_reg_*\`."
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 4 ]]; then
    rung_para 4 " **AND THE RUNG-2 AND RUNG-3 SENTENCES ABOVE HOLD FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED - ITS ADDITION IS ONE BYTE WRITTEN TO ONE REGISTER, AND THE FIVE READS BESIDE IT ARE WHAT MAKE THAT BYTE A MEASUREMENT.** The before-values are 699's press and that is why this rung could be designed at all: \`_reg_software_reset\` read 0x00, so the poll's premise (nothing is in progress, so a bit that does not clear afterwards is the reset FAILING and not a leftover) is a reading rather than an assumption; \`_reg_power_control\` read 0x00, so the standard block does not believe the bus is powered; \`_reg_present_state\` had both inhibit bits clear, so the reset is not being asked to abort anything; and \`_reg_pwrctl_mask\` read 0x0000000f, so the power-IRQ hazard is ARMED and readable as armed. **The cells this arm publishes, in the order they mean something**: \`_rst_wrote\` is 1 if the store was reached and \`_rst_stage\` says how far the function got (0 at entry, 1 after the store, 2 in the poll, 3 done), so a stopped run is localised by the pair; \`_rst_refused\` is 1 only if \`SOFTWARE_RESET\` already carried the reset bit at entry - the register's own self-clearing contract makes that the one state in which writing it would act on a block mid-reset, and 699's reading of 0 is why it is the refusal path and not the expected one; \`_rst_polls\` is 1 in the expected case (697 measured the same controller's \`CORE_SW_RST\` already clear on its first read), \`_rst_steps\` and \`_rst_ticks\` separate a slow reset from a hit bound, and \`_rst_timeout\` with \`_rst_cleared=0\` is the driver's own \`Reset 0x%x never completed\` path - **a reading of the hardware and not a failed run**. \`_rst_stores\` is the count of stores this rung makes, counted bottom-up like \`_reg_loads\`, so the record's \"one store\" and the log's number are two derivations of one fact. **\`_reg_power_control_after\` is the cell 698 section 2 owed**: 0x00 means the standard reset did NOT turn the bus on, and a value with bit 0 set would mean the reset changed the block's belief about the bus, which the driver would then have to reconcile with the vendor's own power state - and \`_reg_pwrctl_status_after\` is the hazard's cell, 0 meaning nothing latched even though \`_reg_pwrctl_mask\` says four bits would have been routed. **WHAT PROVES THE RUNG DID NOT WANDER IS A BUILD CLAUSE AND NOT THIS SENTENCE**: for a rung of 4 \`build_entry.sh\` refuses the build unless the stores on \`core_mem\` are exactly \`0x78/0x00/0x78/0x78\` in that order AND every store in \`hc_mem\` is exactly one, at offset 47 (0x2F), as a BYTE store - so \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\`, four offsets away in the same window, where writing 0 **is** a bus-off request on this SoC, is unreachable by construction; and below rung 4 ANY store in \`hc_mem\` refuses the build. **The negative cell is a NON-RETURN (exit 2), and it is this rung's own on the WRITE side**: a store to a block whose clock is off is a bus wait nothing ends, the run does not come back, and it owes a power press - which is the reason the write is one byte to one register and not a sequence. **What is NOT new here**: no clock, no \`POWER_CONTROL\` write, no \`CORE_PWRCTL\` write, no \`CLOCK_CONTROL\` write, no command, no sector read, nothing mounted - \`_rst_stores=1\` and the four inherited \`_writes=4\` are the whole write set, and a \`_rst_*\` or \`_reg_*_after\` key in this log is still a register and never the medium. **The ending is 690's clock and it does not move**, the reset running after the census and before the clock's block."
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 5 ]]; then
    rung_para 5 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, BECAUSE ITS ADDITION IS TWELVE READS AND NOTHING ELSE - AND ONE OF THOSE READS CORRECTS THE SENTENCE THAT HAS GUARDED THIS WHOLE LINE SINCE 692.** Since 692 the probe has published \`xnu_live_storage_gate\` as \`_cbcr & BIT(0)\` and this narration has called it the branch enable. \`BIT(0)\` of a CBCR is \`CBCR_BRANCH_ENABLE_BIT\` - **the bit the driver WRITES, i.e. the REQUEST** (\`clock-local2.c:62\`, written at \`:380-382\`) - and what \"the clock is running\" means is a second bit two lines away, \`CBCR_BRANCH_OFF_BIT\` = \`BIT(31)\` (\`:63\`), which the framework polls to a bound before it believes the clock is on (\`:386-387\`) and which its own handoff test reads ALONE (\`branch_clk_handoff\`, \`:490-497\`). **THE TWO CAN DISAGREE**, and the ordinary state in which they do is a branch whose enable is requested while the ROOT above it is off - the clock has not propagated, and a register read through it is the bus wait nothing ends. 694's \`_cbcr = 0x00004ff1\` satisfies BOTH halves, so nothing measured so far is overturned; what was wrong is that the gate was reading HALF OF ITSELF, and this arm publishes all three bits - \`_clk_<branch>_en\` (BIT(0)), \`_clk_<branch>_off\` (BIT(31)) and \`_clk_<branch>_hw\` (BIT(1), \`CBCR_HW_CTL_BIT\`, set means the framework skips its own halt check) - for each of the four branches. **THE CELL THAT MATTERS MOST IS \`_clk_ahb_en\`**: \`SDCC1_AHB_CBCR 0x04C8\` is the \`pclk\` \`sdhci_msm_prepare_clocks\` enables (\`sdhci-msm.c:2315\`), **no run in this project has ever read that register**, and the register file answering today only SUGGESTS the branch is on. A 0 there beside an answering controller would be a finding about which clock actually gates it, and its \`branch_clk\` is the one carrying \`has_sibling = 1\`, which the framework turns into \`-EPERM\` for \`round_rate\`/\`list_rate\` (\`:444-446\`, \`:460-462\`) - so enabling it is a different act from enabling the apps branch. **\`_clk_bcr_ares\` IS THE OTHER ARTICLE**: 694 published \`_bcr = 0\` without a key saying what a bit of it means, and \`BCR_BLK_ARES_BIT\` = BIT(0) (\`:66\`) clear is what makes the branch readings mean \"the clock is off\" rather than \"the block is held in reset and nothing it says is about anything\". **\`_clk_rcg_update\` IS THE NEXT WRITE RUNG'S PREMISE**: \`rcg_update_config\` (\`:90-108\`) sets \`CMD_RCGR\`'s update bit and polls THAT SAME BIT clear to a bound of \`UPDATE_CHECK_MAX_LOOPS 500\` (\`:44\`), so a 0 before anything writes is what makes \"a bit that does not clear afterwards is a FAILED UPDATE\" a reading and not an assumption - and \`_clk_rcg_root_en\`, \`_clk_rcg_src\`, \`_clk_rcg_div\` and \`_clk_rcg_m/n/d\` are the apps root's current configuration, i.e. the words \`set_rate_mnd\` (\`:126-146\`) will write. **WHAT THIS ARM DOES NOT DO, and the two claims it must not be read as making**: it publishes NO RATE (the MND and source-select FIELDS are published; converting them to Hz needs the parent's rate - \`gpll0\`/\`gpll4\`/\`cxo\`, \`clock-8974.c:1564-1574\` - which is a table this image does not carry, and a computed frequency beside the framework's own cached rate would be the same one-value-two-definitions defect one level up); and it ENABLES NOTHING: if \`_clk_ahb_en = 0\` the finding is published, not acted on. **WHAT PROVES THE RUNG DID NOT WANDER IS A BUILD CLAUSE AND NOT THIS SENTENCE**: since 704 \`build_entry.sh\`'s store classifier knows the GCC megabyte as its own window and asserts its store set is EMPTY AT EVERY RUNG, so a store to \`0xfc400000\` refuses the build - and before 704 such a store was refused anyway, by the \`DEVBAD\` clause whose stated subject is \"not one of the two windows this controller declares\": true, and a statement about the classifier's scope rather than about the clock. **The negative cell is a NON-RETURN (exit 2)**: a store to a clock-control register whose parent root is off is the bus wait nothing ends - which is why this arm writes nothing and why the writer (\`sdhci_msm_set_clock\`) is a step of its own. **What is NOT new here**: no clock, no \`POWER_CONTROL\` write, no \`CLOCK_CONTROL\` write, no command, no sector read, nothing mounted - the write set is still \`_rst_stores=1\` plus the four inherited \`_writes=4\`, and every \`_clk_*\` key in this log is a register and never a rate."
    fi

    if [[ $wst == 6 ]]; then
    rung_para 6 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, BECAUSE ITS ADDITION IS THE VENDOR'S OWN CLOCK SET AND NOT A NEW KIND OF ACT - WITH THREE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **THE SD CLOCK ENABLE BIT IS SET FOR THE FIRST TIME BY THIS IMAGE**, and it is the last store of the rung: \`_clk_set_cc_card\` is the word written (expected \`0xE045\` = the divisor 960 packed beside \`SDHCI_CLOCK_INT_EN | SDHCI_CLOCK_CARD_EN\`), against 705's \`_clk_clock_control_after=0x0003\` and 699's \`_reg_clock_control=0x0003\` before it - so a run whose last key is \`_clk_set_cc_int\` (\`0xE041\`, SD clock still off) either stopped between the two stores or did not reach the second. (2) **IT IS THE FIRST RUNG WHOSE WRITES COULD STALL THE BLOCK**, so the negative cell is again a NON-RETURN (exit 2) and it is localised rather than discovered: \`_clk_set_writes\` is published after EVERY store, so a run that stops inside the sequence comes back with the count it had reached and the run that hangs is the one whose next store is the missing number - \`_clk_set_ahb_*\`/\`_apps_*\`/\`_ff_*\`/\`_sleep_*\` carry each branch's own before/after and the halt check's poll count and terminal state. (3) **THE CARD IS UNPOWERED AND NOTHING FOLLOWS THE CLOCK**: the driver's own order is clock-then-power (its comment says so, \`sdhci.c:1651-1657\`), so the SD clock is enabled at 200 kHz into a bus whose power register is still 0 exactly as 699 left it, and THIS ARM ISSUES NO COMMAND, READS NO SECTOR AND MOUNTS NOTHING. **WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO IS THE NEXT RUNG'S SUBJECT**: the same \`sdhci_do_set_ios\` call reaches \`sdhci_set_power\`, whose first act is to write **0** to \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\` - the bus-off request 531 section 8 names - and then to call \`check_power_status\`, which on this SoC is an unbounded \`wait_for_completion\` (\`sdhci-msm.c:2179-2209\`); both are kept out of this image by the \`hc_mem\` clause and both are named in experiment-706 section 4."
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 7 ]]; then
    rung_para 7 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, BECAUSE ITS ADDITION IS ONE BYTE AND NOT A NEW KIND OF ACT - WITH THREE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **\`_pwr_after\` IS THE READBACK THAT SAYS THE STORE TOOK, AND RUNG 6 IS WHY THAT IS THE CELL TO READ**: \`_pwr_before\` -> \`_pwr_after\` expected \`0x00\` -> **\`0x0B\`**, with \`_pwr_wrote\` = \`0x0B\` (\`SDHCI_POWER_180 | SDHCI_POWER_ON\`) and \`_pwr_vdd\` = \`7\`; \`0x0F\` here would mean the 3.3 V capability bit was set and this arm's reading of \`_pwr_cap\` was wrong, and \`_pwr_refused\` = 1 means the derivation found no map entry (the vendor's own \`BUG()\` arm), so the store did NOT happen and \`_pwr_done\` is 0. \`_pwr_cap\` is a RE-READING of 705's \`_mode_capabilities\` (\`0x742dc8b2\`), not a constant - a different word is a reading about this boot. (2) **THE WAIT IS NOT TAKEN AND THAT IS THIS RUNG'S ONE JUDGEMENT, PUBLISHED RATHER THAN HIDDEN**: \`sdhci_msm_check_power_status\` takes its \`done\` only from the threaded \`sdhci_msm_pwr_irq\`, which this image cannot deliver (measured silent in 708 section 1.3: \`_reg_pwrctl_mask\` \`0x0f\`, \`_reg_pwrctl_status_after\` \`0\`, \`_mode_pwrctl_status\` \`0\`, \`_reg_pwrctl_ctl\` \`0\`), so a faithful port of the vendor's power path would \`wait_for_completion\` FOREVER - so read \`_pwr_status_after\` (\`CORE_PWRCTL_STATUS 0xDC\`, 32-bit) as the reading that handler would have taken and \`_pwr_done\` as the arm's own completion, and read \`SDHCI_QUIRK_SINGLE_POWER_WRITE\` being set (\`sdhci-msm.c:2897\`) as why the driver's zero/bus-off write is NOT on this path: its absence is not a missing step. (3) **THE SEAM MOVED, AND NOTHING IN THE ARITHMETIC WOULD HAVE PREDICTED IT**: this arm's entry group crossed a page boundary, so the seam went \`0x800472dc\` -> **\`0x800482dc\`** and every kernel-text address moved with it (+0x1000 for 15,979 of the image's 26,533 symbols) - and \`entry_trace.c\`'s constant and \`run_and_capture.sh\`'s literal were re-pinned TOGETHER, with the build refusing to link until they agreed with the image. The remaining cells: \`_pwr_cc_before\` expected **\`0xE045\`** - THE CELL THAT STATES THE ROUTE, because the driver's own pass A sees \`0x0000\` there while this arm runs after 706's clock set, whose card-enable readback is that word; \`_pwr_cc_after\` unchanged at \`0xE045\` is the expected reading and anything else is a coupling this ladder has not seen; \`_pwr_mask\` expected \`0x0000000f\` (four power events armed, so a status bit is evidence); \`_pwr_ctl\` expected \`0\`; and \`_pwr_ps_before\`/\`_pwr_ps_after\` expected \`0x01f80000\`, whose CARD_PRESENT bit is clear - the controller's own statement that no card is in the slot, made before and after the store. **The arm publishes no rate, no command, no sector and no mount**; what this press buys is whether the byte takes, what the power-control words answer, and which of the two routes \`_pwr_cc_before\` states"
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 8 ]]; then
    rung_para 8 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, BECAUSE ITS ADDITION IS A CLIENT AND NOT A NEW KIND OF ACT - WITH FIVE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **THE LINE NOW HAS AN OWNER, AND THAT IS THE ARM'S WHOLE PURPOSE.** 709 ended on \`_irq_other_count=0x00000001\` / \`_irq_other_iar=0x000000aa\`; on THIS arm the same event must reach \`st_pwr_irq\` instead, so read \`_pwr_irq_calls\` (>=1 - the client was called at all), \`_pwr_irq_intid\` (=170), \`_pwr_irq_reg_rc\` (=0, the registration was accepted) and \`_pwr_irq_arm_rc\` beside \`_irq_other_count\`, whose expected value here is **not 1** - and a log that carries BOTH the client's keys and \`_irq_other_*\` is the more interesting reading, because it says the dispatcher answered this line and something else was still unowned. (2) **\`_pwr_irq_calls_once_before_byte = 0\` IS THE CELL THAT MAKES THE BYTE THE CAUSE**, and it is the only cell that can: the handler records \`g_pwr_irq_calls\` at the moment the byte is written, so a nonzero value there means the line was already asserted before this arm's store - 709's latch, still standing - and a zero means this store raised it. (3) **THE ACKNOWLEDGE IS A STORE, SO ITS OWN READBACK IS THE CELL.** \`_pwr_irq_status\` = \`0x02\` beside \`_pwr_irq_status32\` = \`0x00000002\` (the SAME register read at two widths from one moment - the two-width pair the vendor's own \`readb_relaxed\`/\`readl_relaxed\` pair makes), \`_pwr_irq_ack\` = \`0x01\`, and **\`_pwr_irq_status_after\` = \`0x00\`** is the rung's own falsifier read the other way: a \`0x02\` there is a latch that did NOT let go, so the level line re-asserts, the client is called to \`STAGE90_IRQ_CLIENT_CAP\` 64, and the log carries \`_irq_cli_storm\` instead of the 690 ending. (4) **THE TWO VENDOR-SPEC ADDRESSES ARE ALSO A BUILD FACT AND NOT ONLY A CELL.** \`_pwr_irq_vendor_core\` / \`_pwr_irq_vendor_hc\` are \`core_mem + 0x10C\` and \`hc_mem + 0x10C\`; the probe's own store census on THIS arm is \`hc_mem [47 44 44 41 ]\` and **not** \`[47 44 44 41 268]\` - experiment-710 section 2 predicted the second and the build falsified it, because the handler is its own symbol (\`st_pwr_irq\`, 0x1cc bytes at \`0x8000cff8\`, which pushed \`entry_storage_probe\` to \`0x8000d1c4\`) and its \`0x10C\` store is outside \`entry_storage_probe\`'s window. (5) **THE THREE ARMS THAT MAY SLEEP ARE ABSENT BY CONSTRUCTION, AND THAT IS A HAZARD STATEMENT RATHER THAN A CELL.** The client runs in \`fleh_irq_kernel\`'s frame: a handler that called a regulator or pinctrl routine would sleep in an exception, which is why the vendor asked for a threaded handler and why this image refuses such a build outright. **AND THE HAZARD THIS PRESS CARRIES IS THE ONE 709 MEASURED, FROM THE OTHER SIDE**: the line stays asserted until the latch is cleared, so a client that READ the status and did not clear it is a storm, and the only bound on it is \`STAGE90_IRQ_CLIENT_CAP\` - read \`_irq_cli_storm\` in the log beside \`_pwr_irq_calls\`, never \`_pwr_irq_calls\` alone"
    fi
    if [[ $wst == 9 ]]; then
    rung_para 9 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, BECAUSE ITS ADDITION IS A WAIT AND NOT A NEW KIND OF ACT - WITH FIVE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **THE RUNG'S OWN ANSWER IS THE PAIR \`_wait_timeout\` AND \`_wait_ctl_after\`, AND BOTH VALUES ARE INFORMATIVE.** \`_wait_ctl_before\` is the controller's \`CORE_PWRCTL_CTL\` byte before the wait (expected \`0x00\`, the reading rung 7's press took) and \`_wait_ctl_after\` is the same byte after it: **\`0x01\` is the controller acking the BUS_ON request (\`BUS_SUCCESS\`, \`sdhci-msm.c:72\`) and \`0x00\` is the ack NOT arriving.** \`_wait_timeout\` = 0 beside \`_wait_ctl_after\` = 0x01 is the handshake completing inside the budget; \`_wait_timeout\` = 1 is the poll spending its whole bound and going on; and \`_wait_ctl_after\` = 0x00 with \`_wait_timeout\` = 0 is impossible by construction - which is why the pair and not either cell alone. \`_wait_polls\` and \`_wait_ticks\` are the wait's own cost (\`_wait_ticks\` is in ticks of the 19,200,000 Hz counter, so this arm's bound $wpt is $(( wpt / 19200 )) ms and a small number is the ack arriving fast), and **\`_wait_bound\` = $wpt is the switch itself, and it must read the value this record carries and not a constant**: the build asserts the budget's \`mov\`/\`movw\` + \`movt\` pair is present in \`st_pwr_wait\`'s OWN body (both spellings - on 716's arm the pair is \`mov r9, #0xDC00\` + \`movt r9, #5\`, a rotated immediate and NOT a \`movw\`; the clause accepts both and the BUILD's success echo still says \`movw\`/\`movt\`, recorded as m724), because a switch whose value never reaches the image is \`mi4-off-option-two-spellings\` with a shorter fuse and would leave this record claiming a bound the code does not carry. (2) **THE TWO-MEMORY CELL IS THE DEVICE PAIR AGAINST THE IMAGE PAIR, AND IT IS THE CELL THIS RUNG EXISTS FOR.** Read \`_wait_ctl_after\` (the controller's own byte) BESIDE \`_wait_done\` (the image-side flag \`g_pwr_irq_done\`) and \`_wait_calls\` (whether the handler ran at all). Device ack with the flag SET is the handshake complete on both sides; **device ack with the flag CLEAR is a driver-side completion that did not cross the cache boundary** - the shape 686's correction of 652 describes, one context over, and the reason the end condition is the device register; \`_wait_ctl_after\` = 0x00 with \`_wait_calls\` >= 1 is a handler that RAN and did not ack, which is rung 8's decode failing rather than its line; and \`_wait_calls\` = 0 with a nonzero \`_irq_other_count\` means the event went to nobody, which is 709's press exactly. \`_wait_state_before\` / \`_wait_io_before\` and their \`_after\` twins are the vendor's own predicate fields, so \`_wait_done_before\` = 1 with \`_wait_reset\` = 1 is the vendor's already-satisfied branch rather than a broken poll. (3) **\`CLOCK_CONTROL\` READ TWICE IS 711 SECTION 3's OPEN QUESTION, ANSWERED BY CONSTRUCTION.** \`_wait_cc_before\` and \`_wait_cc_after\` are \`hc_mem + CLOCK_CONTROL 0x2C\` read as a **halfword** either side of the wait (the vendor's own \`readw\`, \`sdhci.h:100\`), so the two boots that disagreed about bit 1 are replaced by one run that reads the same register on both sides of a hundred milliseconds: **equal halves say the bit is the machine's state, a half that changes across the wait says it is a race with the driver's own clock block** - and either reading is a fact about the MACHINE, which is what 711 could not get from two runs. (4) **\`_wait_reset\` NAMES THE BRANCH, AND IT IS NOT A FORMALITY.** 0 means the poll ran; 1 means the vendor's own already-satisfied arm was taken, the completion was RESET (\`:2202-2203\`) and NO wait happened - so on that branch \`_wait_polls\` = 0 and \`_wait_ticks\` = 0 are CORRECT and not a dead loop, and \`_wait_cpsr\` is published from both paths so the log always names which one ran. (5) **THE ARM MAKES NO DEVICE STORE AT ALL, AND THAT IS A BUILD FACT AND NOT A PROMISE.** \`st_pwr_wait\`'s device accesses are asserted by the build as EXACTLY \`[f98240e8:ldrb f982492c:ldrh]\` with minimum counts \`[f982492c:ldrh >= 2, f98240e8:ldrb >= 2]\` (two \`CLOCK_CONTROL\` halfword readings either side, and the poll's \`CTL\` byte; the counts are a MINIMUM because the compiler may duplicate a read across the vendor's own two decode branches), its non-device accesses as exactly \`ldr\`/\`str\` over the four words \`g_pwr_irq_calls\`, \`g_pwr_curr_state\`, \`g_pwr_curr_io\`, \`g_pwr_irq_done\` - resolved to those four \`.bss\` symbols in the LINKED image, so \"the waiter and the handler are two halves of ONE handshake\" is a property of the artifact and not of two source files agreeing - and the probe's single \`bl\` to it. **A \`str\`/\`strb\` anywhere in that window therefore fails the set equality rather than a separate assertion**, because the ack the poll waits for is the HANDLER's store and a waiter that wrote \`CTL\` would be answering its own completion. **The hazard this press carries is the wait's own cost**: the poll is bounded, so the run always continues - but a log whose last key is \`_wait_bound\` or \`_wait_ticks\` is a run whose ending clock was spent on the wait, and the store set is still \`_rst_stores=1\` plus the four inherited \`_writes=4\` plus the power byte: **no command, no sector, no partition table, no mount**, and every \`_wait_*\` key in this log is a register and never the medium"
    fi

    rung_para 10 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, WITH FIVE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **TWO CELLS DECIDE WHETHER THE ARM IS A READING AT ALL, AND NEITHER OF THEM IS THE ANSWER.** \`_wait_cpsr\` must read **\`0x80000013\`** - SVC, \`I\` clear, \`F\` clear, \`N\` set, i.e. \`0x80000093 & ~0x80\`, the value rungs 9 and 10 read as \`0x80000093\`: a reading of \`0x80000093\` here means the \`cpsie\` did not take (or the compiler moved the \`mrs\` above it) and the run is **VOID AS A COMPARISON rather than negative**, with the disassembly of \`st_pwr_wait\` the first thing to re-read; and **\`_wait_cpsr_after\`** must read **\`0x80000093\`**, which is the state the probe GIVES BACK - the same value rungs 9 and 10 read as their premise, so the pair says the mask was restored from the SAVED register and not from a constant that happens to look tidy. (2) **THE OUTCOME IS THE PAIR \`_wait_timeout\` AND \`_wait_ctl_after\`, AND BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE INFORMATIVE.** \`_wait_timeout\` = \`0\` with \`_wait_ctl_after\` = \`0x01\` is the handshake COMPLETING inside the poll - the first time on this ladder - and \`_wait_timeout\` = \`1\` is the poll spending its whole bound with the mask OFF, which is the more interesting negative: the mask was off, the bound was $wpt ticks ($(( wpt / 19200 )) ms), and the handler still did not run. \`_wait_polls\` and \`_wait_ticks\` size the delivery (small numbers are the completion arriving at once, against 86,016 polls and 386,131 ticks on 716's arm, whose whole bound was spent on its own mask), and **the new reading is \`_pwr_irq_at\` against \`_wait_t0\`**: on rungs 9 and 10 that difference WAS the spin (1,922,347 and 387,298 ticks); here it must collapse to the delivery's own cost - the interval from the mask coming off to the exception being taken. (3) **\`_wait_done\` = 1 BESIDE \`_wait_calls\` = 1 IS THE VENDOR'S PREDICATE SATISFIED, AND IT IS ALSO THE CACHE-BOUNDARY COMPARISON THIS LADDER HAS NEVER HAD.** On rungs 9 and 10 the waiter read \`g_pwr_irq_done\`, \`g_pwr_curr_state\` and \`g_pwr_curr_io\` BEFORE the handler had run, so the flag-against-ack pair had no comparison; here the handler has already written all three when the waiter reads them. **Device ack (\`_wait_ctl_after\` = 0x01) with the flag SET is the handshake complete on both sides; device ack with \`_wait_done\` = 0 is 686's cache-boundary defect measured instead of argued** - and inside this window the expected answer is agreement through DRAM, because \`xnu_live_seam_sctlr\` reads \`C\` clear and the seam is why the end condition is the device register at all. (4) **A NEGATIVE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THE MASK: \`_wait_cpsr\` = 0x80000013 WITH \`_wait_timeout\` = 1.** That says something BELOW the CPSR gates this line in this context - the CPU interface's priority mask (\`GICC_PMR\`), the line's priority, its group or its target - and the next rung is then those four readings taken inside the unmasked window. It is the more valuable reading and the unlikely one: the handler has been taken at this site three times, so the line is enabled, in Group 0, at a priority the mask admits and targeted at this CPU. (5) **THE HAZARD IS THE CONTEXT AND NOT THE BOUND, AND IT IS THIS ARM'S OWN NEGATIVE CELL.** The client now runs in \`fleh_irq_kernel\`'s frame with \`SCTLR.C\` clear INSIDE the payload's own idle-exit code - a state no earlier arm entered, and the state the payload masks \`I\` to avoid. The poll is still bounded, the gate is still read before any store, the watchdog is still armed and nothing is flashed, but a non-return (exit 2), an \`_irq_other_*\` key or an ending on \`exception: irq line\` is a READING HERE and names the line or the fault rather than refuting the cells above. **The write set is UNCHANGED, and that is a build fact and not a promise**: this arm's own build printed \`st_pwr_wait\`'s device accesses as EXACTLY \`[f982492c:ldrh f98240e8:ldrb]\` (counts \`ldrh=2\`, \`ldrb=4\`) and its image accesses as exactly \`ldr\`/\`str\` over the four words \`g_pwr_irq_calls\`, \`g_pwr_curr_state\`, \`g_pwr_curr_io\`, \`g_pwr_irq_done\` - because a published cell is a CALL to \`entry_live_write\` with a string and is not an access that window's census can see, which is why the new \`_wait_cpsr_after\` changes neither set - with the probe's single \`bl\` to it and the budget pair in its own body. **So: no command, no sector, no partition table, no mount** - the arm's whole device act is still two halfword reads of \`CLOCK_CONTROL\` and a poll of one \`CORE_PWRCTL_CTL\` byte"
    rung_para 11 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, WITH SIX THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **THE GATE ABOVE THE GATE: IF \`_cmd_enabled_out\` = 1 THE RUN IS VOID AS A COMPARISON AND THE FINDING IS A REAL ONE.** \`_cmd_int_enable\` or \`_cmd_sig_enable\` non-zero means the SDHCI block CAN raise \`hc_irq\` (SPI 123 -> intid 155), a line this image hands to nobody and whose delivery the dispatcher turns into \`_irq_other_count\` and an ENDED RUN - so \`_cmd_refused\` = 1 and \`_cmd_sent\` = 0, no command was issued, and the per-command keys are ABSENT rather than zero (an absent key and a zero are two different records). The next rung is then a client for that line (rung 8's shape, intid 155) or a read of the distributor's \`ISENABLER1\` to see whether it is even enabled at boot. (2) **TWO CELLS DECIDE WHETHER THE ARM IS A READING AT ALL, AND NEITHER IS THE ANSWER.** \`_cmd0_word\` must read **\`0x0000\`** and \`_cmd1_word\` must read **\`0x0102\`** - they are the transcription of \`sdhci.h:57\` over \`sdhci.c:1130-1140\`'s decode for \`MMC_RSP_NONE\` and \`MMC_RSP_R3\`, so a mismatch means the record is wrong and not the hardware; and \`_cmdN_inhibit_before\` must read \`0\` (rung 11's \`_pwr_ps_after\` = \`0x01f80000\` says the controller is idle), with \`_cmdN_inhibit_timeout\` = 0. (3) **THE THREE-WAY ANSWER ON CMD1, AND EACH BRANCH IS A DIFFERENT NEXT RUNG.** \`_cmd1_complete\` = 1 with \`_cmd1_err\` = 0 and \`_cmd1_resp\` of the answered-card shape (bit 30 set, the voltage window in bits 23:15 non-zero - \`0x40ff8080\` and \`0xc0ff8080\` are the two reference words) is **THE CARD ANSWERING**, and the push is \`mmc_attach_mmc\`'s next statements: CMD2 (\`mmc_all_send_cid\`, the first 136-bit response - four words with the driver's own shift, \`sdhci.c:1164-1172\`), CMD3 \`mmc_set_relative_addr\`, CMD9 \`SEND_CSD\`, CMD7 \`SELECT_CARD\`, and then the first DATA command, CMD17 at LBA 1, where 531 section 9's self-verifying reading lives (the eight bytes \`EFI PART\`, or an MBR ending \`55 aa\`). \`_cmd1_complete\` = 0 with \`_cmd1_err\` = **\`0x00010000\`** (\`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT\`) is **the command path working and NOTHING ANSWERING** - a finding about the CARD and the first one this line has ever had - and it re-opens the steps this rung's pre-registration talked out of it, **in a measured order**: the CMD line's own level in \`_cmd1_ps_after\`, the SDCLK actually reaching the card (the RCG rate write: the divisor is computed against \`host->max_clk\` = 384 MHz while the register file is at 192 MHz, so the card sees 200 kHz - inside the 100-400 kHz identification range, which is why this rung could be built before it), and the card's rail (the vendor's three sleeping arms). A \`_cmd1_err\` with any OTHER bit set, or \`_cmd0_err\` non-zero (which withholds CMD1: \`_cmd_gated\` = 1, \`_cmd_sent\` = 1), is the CONTROLLER failing and the first thing to re-read is \`st_send_command\`'s own disassembly. (4) **TWO REFUSALS THAT ARE NOT FAILURES.** \`_cmdN_clear_after\` != 0 means the latch would not clear, so \`_cmdN_sent\` = 0 and no command was issued - because a poll whose end condition is a bit that is already set is not a reading; and \`_cmdN_timeout\` = 1 with no error bit means OUR 1,200 ms bound expired before the controller's own ~1 s one, which is a reading about the controller's timeout rather than a silence. (5) **THE HAZARD IS THE BUS, AND IT IS NAMED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED.** This is the first act of this line that puts a transaction on the medium's own wires: a card that drives a line into a state the controller waits on is bounded by \`_cmdN_ticks\`' budget, the inhibit gate is bounded by the driver's own 10 ms, an enable that could let a delivery reach the dispatcher refuses the whole act, and no data phase exists to hang in - but a non-return (exit 2) costs this press and refutes none of the cells above. (6) **THE WRITE SET IS THE SAFETY CONTRACT, AND IT IS THREE STORES IN ONE FUNCTION.** \`_cmdN_stale\` (the latch as found), \`_cmdN_clear_wrote\` (the write-1-to-clear, equal to the stale value by construction - a CONSISTENCY cell and not evidence, m723's rule) and the two command words are the arm's whole device write set; \`TRANSFER_MODE 0x0C\` is not written because \`sdhci_set_transfer_mode\` returns on its first line for a data-less command (\`sdhci.c:985-986\`), \`POWER_CONTROL 0x29\` stays out of the linked image (\`hc_mem\` is still \`47 44 44 41\`), the GCC and \`core_mem\` sets are rung 6's, and **no byte of the medium can change**: CMD0 and CMD1 are \`bc\`/\`bcr\` commands with no data phase. **So: no block, no sector, no partition table, no filesystem, no mount - what the arm adds is two commands on the bus and the answer to the second one, if there is one**"
    # **732: the arm's own consequences, and the first is that its ending is a DESIGNED one.**
    rung_para 14 " **AND EVERY SENTENCE ABOVE HOLDS FOR THIS ARM UNCHANGED, WITH FIVE THINGS THAT ARE NEW AND ARE THE ONES TO READ.** (1) **THE ENABLE IS THE ARM AND THE RESTORE IS THE SAFETY ARGUMENT, SO READ THEM AS A PAIR.** \`_ena_wrote\` must be \`0x00000001\` (the gate authorised \`int_enable | SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\` and \`INT_ENABLE\` read zero before it, so the word written is 1) and \`_ena_held\` must read the same 1 - a \`_ena_held\` of \`0x00000000\` is a store that did not take, and a \`_ena_held\` with any other bit set is a bit this rung did not authorise. (2) **THE TWO RESTORES ARE SEQUENCED AND BOTH MUST APPEAR**: \`_ena_restore_seq\` = 2 with \`_ena_wrote_back\` = \`0x00000000\` and \`_ena_readback\` = \`0x00000000\` is the arm closing its own window at the end of the body; a run whose log carries only seq 1 left the enable set for the rest of the press, and a run with a \`_ena_readback\` that disagrees with \`_ena_wrote_back\` is the block refusing the restore - either is a finding and neither is a device that said nothing. (3) **\`_cmd_gated = 0\` BESIDE \`_cmd1_*\` IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE PRESS.** 726's and 730's runs both ended the command path at gate 1 with \`_cmd_gated = 1\` and no \`_cmd1_*\` key of any kind, so **CMD1 has never been issued by this line** - and if the enable and the signal line are ANDed in hardware, the line still never rises and this run ends the same way, which is why the sequence cells above are read first. A run with \`_cmd_gated = 1\` and \`_cmd_gate_kind\` = 2 is the ladder's most informative negative: it says \`INT_ENABLE\` was ALREADY holding a bit other than RESPONSE. **AND 736 MEASURED WHAT THAT ACTUALLY MEANS, BECAUSE THIS SENTENCE SAID “a fact about the block” AND THAT IS WRONG: the rung-16 press read \`_cmd_gate_kind = 0x2\` with \`_cmd_int_enable = 0x00008000\`, and the 0x8000 was set by THAT RUN'S OWN quiet store about two hundred lines earlier - bit 15 is set by any write to \`0x34\` and is not cleared by one, which 730 and 733 had already measured. So the reading is a fact about THIS RUN'S EARLIER WRITES, not about the block, and the gate was right to refuse (\`_cmd_sent = 0\`, no command on the bus).** Only a run with no earlier write to \`0x34\` can read it as a property of the block, and 736's log is where that was measured (docs/experiments/experiment-736-*). (4) **\`_cmd1_err\` STILL CARRIES THE ONE BIT THAT SEPARATES THE TWO FAILURES** - \`0x00010000\` (\`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT\`) is the command going out and the CARD not answering, a finding about the card; any other bit is about the controller - and \`_cmd1_resp\` is pre-registered as a SHAPE and not a value, because the OCR is the card's to state. (5) **IF THIS BLOCK DOES RAISE ITS LINE THE RUN ENDS AT THE DISPATCHER, AND THAT IS A DIAGNOSIS AND NOT A LOST DEVICE**: \`hc_irq\` is SPI 123 -> intid 155 and nobody in this image owns it, so a delivery reaches the dispatcher as \`_irq_other_count = 1\` with \`_irq_other_iar = 155\` - **an ending this ladder already reads and survives** (709's rung-7 press ended exactly that way on intid 170, and the log came back with every cell it promised). \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` at ZERO is the one thing 730's press measured is safe and it is still zero here, which is why the delivery is improbable and the restore is the arm's real guarantee."
    # **734: the consequent reading, and it is a two-way cell rather than a pass/fail one.**
    rung_para 15 " **THIS RUNG'S CELL IS THE ONE 733 PREDICTED WRONG, AND BOTH OF ITS ANSWERS ARE READINGS.** \`_quiet_status_after\` is read on a block that has never carried a command, and the two mechanisms 733 left standing predict OPPOSITE values: **(A)** a write to \`0x34\` makes \`0x30\`'s RESPONSE bit read 1, so \`_quiet_status_after = 0x00000001\`; **(B)** the block was holding a completion and re-latched it when the enable returned - 726's sixth hypothesis - which cannot happen here, so \`_quiet_status_after = 0x00000000\`. **Read the pair and never the single cell**: \`_quiet_status_after\` beside \`_quiet_status_before\` (which must read 0 and is the reading that says the block was quiet at the store) and beside \`_cmd0_any_polls\` in this same log (538 samples of zero with the enable standing before CMD0, with \`_cmd0_status_any = 1\` first appearing at the poll's LAST sample). **(A) with a late \`_cmd0_status_any\` is a coherent pair** - the enable makes the bit READABLE and the command's own completion is what SETS it - and it retires 726's sixth hypothesis rather than confirming it; **(B)** is that hypothesis confirmed at the one moment it could be tested. **Neither reading is a failure, and neither is an absent key to be explained away.** The safety cells are the other half: \`_quiet_sig_before\` must read ZERO (the signal enable is what puts the line up, and this body never writes it), \`_quiet_wrote_back\` must equal \`_quiet_enable_before\` (this body's own read, not a constant), and \`_quiet_readback\` is the block's readback of the restore - **which 730 and 733 measured is a PARTIAL restore**: bit 15 (\`SDHCI_INT_ERROR\`) is SET by a write to \`0x34\` and is not cleared by one, so \`_quiet_enable_before = 0\` beside \`_quiet_readback = 0x00008000\` is the expected shape and not a restore that failed. **And this arm inherits the rung above's cells unchanged** - \`_cmd_gated = 0\` with \`_cmd1_*\` keys present, \`_cmd0_complete = 1\`, \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\` - so a log whose rung-15 keys are all there while the command-path cells have moved is a log about the new body's PLACE and not about the block."
    rung_para 16 " **THIS RUNG'S NEW READING IS A 136-BIT RESPONSE, AND THE CELL THAT SAYS THE COMMAND RAN IS THE ONE THE LADDER BELOW COULD NOT PRODUCE.** CMD2 is the first command on this bus whose answer is four words wide, and the driver reads it in a shape no other command shares: \`_cid_raw0\`..\`_cid_raw3\` are the four 32-bit words at \`0x1c\`, \`0x18\`, \`0x14\` and \`0x10\` in that order - word 3 first - and \`_cid_resp0\`..\`_cid_resp3\` are the same four after \`sdhci.c:1164-1172\`'s \`<< 8\` and its byte fill, which is the shift the driver's own comment calls *CRC is stripped*. **Read the pair and never the raw alone**: \`_cid_resp3\` is \`raw3 << 8\` with NO byte read in it (the driver's own \`if (i != 3)\`), so a reader comparing the two sets can see exactly which low byte came from where - and a \`resp\` whose low byte is zero where its \`raw\` is not is the driver's shift doing what its comment says rather than a card that answered nothing. **The card's own CID is not a constant this document can predict**, so what is asserted here is the SHAPE and not a value: the four raw words are a reading, and a CID is a value only if the card answered - which is what \`_cid_complete\` and \`_cid_status_any\` are beside. **THE COMMAND'S OWN CELLS ARE THE RUNG'S OTHER HALF AND THEY ARE THE ONES 733 COULD NOT GET**: \`_cid_sent = 1\` with \`_cid_word = 0x0209\` and \`_cid_word_read\` equal to it (the block holds the driver's own word - rung 12 added that read precisely because an absence of interrupt has two producers), \`_cid_inhibit_seen > 0\` with \`_cid_inhibit_last\` (the block started it) and \`_cid_complete = 1\` with \`_cid_status_any != 0\` (it finished and said so) - **and this is the reading rung 14 could not take**: CMD1 ran with the window closed on 733's press and was answered by the card while latching nothing at all, so \`_cid_complete = 1\` here is the first completion this ladder has seen from a command sent through this window since CMD0. **A \`_cid_complete = 0\` with \`_cid_resp0 != 0\` is NOT a failure and it is a reading**: it says the response arrived and the status bit did not latch, which is 726's sixth hypothesis confirmed a second time on a command whose answer is eight times as wide - read it beside \`_cid_status_any\`, \`_cid_timeout\` and \`_cid_polls\`. \`_cid_err\` and \`_cid_timeout\` separate the card from the controller: \`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT 0x00010000\` is the CARD not answering, any other command-error bit is the controller, and \`_cid_timeout = 1\` with \`_cid_status_any = 0\` is the bound expiring with the block silent. **The window's own cells are the same four the rung below publishes and they must read the same way**: \`_cid_ena_wrote\` is derived from the gate's read, \`_cid_ena_held = 0x00008001\` is bit 15 riding along with a write to \`0x34\`, \`_cid_sig_enable\` must read ZERO (the signal enable is what puts this block's SPI 123 on the wire, and this body never writes it), and \`_cid_readback = 0x00008000\` is the PARTIAL restore 730 and 733 both measured - **not a restore that failed**. **AND A \`_cid_gated = 1\` IS A COMPLETE ANSWER RATHER THAN A LOST PRESS**: \`_cid_gated_reason = 1\` says CMD1 was never sent (the between-commands gate refused above it, so read \`_cmd_gated\` and \`_cmd0_complete\` for why) and \`2\` says CMD1 was sent and the card was still busy (\`_cmd1_resp\`'s bit 31 set). Both are published deliberately, so a refusal appears as this rung's own cell with a cause beside it and never as a set of \`_cid_*\` keys that are merely absent. **And this arm inherits every cell below it unchanged** - \`_cmd_gated = 0\` with \`_cmd1_*\` present, \`_cmd0_complete = 1\`, the \`_ena_*\` window pair, the \`_int_status_*\` pair and rung 15's \`_quiet_*\` cells ARE NOT IN THIS IMAGE AT ALL (its body is compiled for the value 15 and no other, and the build refuses its presence here), so a log from this arm with a \`_quiet_*\` key in it is a log from an arm that is not this one. **A hang past the 1.2 s command bound is not a possible failure of this press**: the bound is inside \`st_send_command\` and the run's own ending is unchanged."
    rung_para 17 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS ONE BIT AND ITS QUESTION IS ABOUT THE LADDER'S OWN EVIDENCE, NOT ABOUT THE BLOCK.** \`_rb_resp_zero\` is derived IN THE IMAGE from the four raw words this body reads before any command has ever been on the bus, and it has exactly two readings, both of which name the next act: **\`_rb_resp_zero = 1\`** says \`RESPONSE 0x10\` was EMPTY, so the \`0x40ff8080\` this ladder has been reading since 733 as *the card answered CMD1* was put there by CMD1 - the evidence survives and the next rung continues the driver from a fact; **\`_rb_resp_zero = 0\`** says the register already held a value with NO COMMAND IN THE BLOCK'S PAST, and the ladder's one piece of evidence that a card exists is then RETIRED. **Read the four shifted words beside it and not the aggregate alone**: \`_rb_resp0\`..\`_rb_resp3\` are the same arithmetic over the same offsets that \`_cid_resp0\`..\`_cid_resp3\` are after CMD2 - one derivation taken at two times - so a reader compares them ONE FOR ONE in this same log, and **a \`_rb_resp0\` that equals \`_cid_resp0\` is the whole answer**: the word was in the register before the command and the command did not put it there. 738's own press is why this is worth a press: it read \`_cid_resp0 = 0x40ff8080\` with \`_cid_complete = 0\` over 5,088,256 polls and \`_cid_inhibit_seen = 0\`, so the value is observable in the register with nothing latched and no \`CMD_INHIBIT\` ever seen, and it has an OCR's SHAPE (bit 30 set, voltage window \`0x00ff8000\`) which is why it convinced - **shape is not provenance**. **THE DISCRIMINATOR 738 LEFT IS ALSO IN THIS LOG AND IT IS THE INHIBIT**: \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen = 0x219\` says the block STARTED CMD0, while \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` and \`_cid_inhibit_seen = 0\` say it never showed a start for the two commands whose response words are the ones this ladder has been trusting - and \`_rb_inhibit\` is that same bit read at the pre-command moment, when it must read ZERO. **The safety cells are the arm's whole act**: this rung adds NO STORE ANYWHERE - \`_rb_int_status\`, \`_rb_int_enable\` and \`_rb_sig_enable\` are READ and never written, the build's own clause refuses any store in the body and prints its store set EMPTY, and \`_rb_cmd_word\` (which must read \`0x0000\` if no command has ever carried) is a halfword read. **\`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` IS AGAIN READ AND NEVER WRITTEN**, which is the one thing 730's press measured is safe. **AND THIS ARM IS THE FIRST SINCE 736's THAT MUST NOT CARRY A RUNG-15 KEY**: rung 15's store above \`st_cmd_path\`'s gate leaves bit 15 set in \`0x34\` and refuses the whole command path, so \`st_quiet_enable_probe\` is compiled for the value 15 and no other and the build refuses its presence here - **a log from this arm with any \`_quiet_*\` key in it is a log from an arm that is not this one**. **The rung below is inherited unchanged and its cells are the ones to read it against**: \`_cmd_gated = 0\` with \`_cmd1_*\` present, \`_cmd0_complete = 1\`, \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\`, \`_cid_word = _cid_word_read = 0x00000209\`, and 730's own pair \`_int_status_before = 0x00000000\` -> \`_int_status_after = 0x00000001\`. **A \`_rb_resp_zero = 0\` is not a failed press**: it is this rung answering, and the answer is that the ladder's push from CMD1 has to be re-derived from a signal other than \`RESPONSE\`."
    rung_para 18 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS A PAIR TAKEN AT TWO TIMES, AND IT IS THE FIRST ANSWER IN THIS LADDER THAT NO SINGLE CELL CAN CARRY.** \`_rca_resp_moved\` is derived IN THE IMAGE from \`_rca_resp_pre\` and \`_rca_resp_post\`, which are ONE ARITHMETIC - the driver's own word-0 derivation \`(readl(RESPONSE + 0x1C) << 8) | readb(RESPONSE + 0x1B)\` - read immediately BEFORE the one-bit window opens and again immediately AFTER the command's publishes, and it has exactly two readings, both of which name the next act: **\`_rca_resp_moved = 1\`** says CMD3 MOVED the register, so the sequencer took this command and the ladder's next statement is the driver's own CMD9; **\`_rca_resp_moved = 0\`** says it did not, and then the two cells beside it say what came back instead. **THE THREE DERIVATIONS ARE COMPARABLE ONE FOR ONE IN THIS SAME LOG AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE METHOD**: \`_rca_resp_pre\` and \`_rca_resp_post\` are the same arithmetic \`_rb_resp0\` is before any command (rung 18's read-only body) and \`_cid_resp0\` is after CMD2 (rung 17's), so a reader who takes only the aggregate has this rung's answer and a reader who takes the three words has the reason. **AND \`_rca_resp_is_arg\` NAMES THE ALTERNATIVE PRODUCER RATHER THAN LEAVING IT TO THE READER**: it is 1 iff the word after CMD3 equals the ARGUMENT \`0x00010000\`, so a register that echoes what was written to \`ARGUMENT 0x08\` is a reading this arm publishes and not a coincidence a reader has to think of. **THE SHAPE TEST IS ALSO IN THE LOG AND IT IS THE R1 DECODE**: \`_rca_resp\` is the raw word and \`_rca_state\` (bits 12:9, \`CURRENT_STATE\`), \`_rca_ready\` (bit 8, \`READY_FOR_DATA\`) and \`_rca_illegal\` (bit 22, \`ILLEGAL_COMMAND\`) are its three fields - an R1 CARD STATUS is not an OCR, so **a \`_rca_resp\` that equals \`_cid_resp0\` (or \`_rca_resp_pre\`) is the register answering with its old content and not with CMD3's status**, and that reading is available without a press being re-planned. A card that took CMD3 has \`_rca_illegal = 0\` and answers with state 2 or 3, and \`_rca_complete = 0\` beside a moved response is NOT a failure - it is 726's sixth hypothesis measured a third time, on a command whose answer is one word wide and whose flag word carries the \`INDEX\` bit. **THE INHIBIT TRIPLET IS PUBLISHED FOR CMD3 AS IT IS FOR CMD0, CMD1 AND CMD2, AND ONE LOG NOW HOLDS FOUR COMMANDS' OWN INHIBIT READINGS SIDE BY SIDE**: \`_rca_inhibit_after\` (the bit immediately after the store), \`_rca_inhibit_seen\` (how many of the poll's first 1024 samples showed it) and \`_rca_inhibit_last\` (whether it was still set at the last one) are 740 section 6's next question made answerable rather than asked - CMD0's \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen\` was 0x219 while CMD1's and CMD2's were both ZERO, so CMD3's own triplet against \`_rca_resp_moved\` separates *the sequencer took it* from *the register file answered*. **THE WINDOW'S CELLS ARE THE SAME FOUR THE RUNGS BELOW PUBLISH AND THEY MUST READ THE SAME WAY**: \`_rca_ena_wrote\` is derived from the gate's own read rather than a constant, \`_rca_ena_held = 0x00008001\` is bit 15 riding along with a write to \`0x34\`, \`_rca_sig_enable\` must read ZERO (the signal enable is what puts this block's SPI 123 on the wire and this body never writes it), and \`_rca_readback = 0x00008000\` is the PARTIAL restore 730, 733, 736, 738 and 740 all measured - **not a restore that failed**. **AND A \`_rca_gated = 1\` IS A COMPLETE ANSWER RATHER THAN A LOST PRESS**: \`_rca_gated_reason = 1\` says CMD2 was never SENT, and that is the value \`_cid_sent\` in this same log publishes - the gate and the cell are one reading with two consumers, so a refusal here can never contradict the log it appears in. **THE CELLS THAT SAY THE RUNGS BELOW ARE INHERITED UNCHANGED ARE THE ONES TO READ IT AGAINST**: \`_cmd_gated = 0\` with \`_cmd1_*\` present, \`_cmd0_complete = 1\`, \`_cmd1_resp = 0x40ff8080\`, \`_cid_word = _cid_word_read = 0x00000209\`, \`_cid_resp0 = 0x40ff8080\`, \`_rb_resp_zero = 1\` (740's answer, and the one this rung is built on), and 730's own pair \`_int_status_before = 0x00000000\` -> \`_int_status_after = 0x00000001\`. **AND RUNG 15'S BODY IS STILL EXCLUDED HERE FOR 736's REASON**: \`st_quiet_enable_probe\` is compiled for the value 15 and no other, because its store above \`st_cmd_path\`'s gate leaves bit 15 set in \`0x34\` and refuses the whole command path - so **a log from this arm carrying any \`_quiet_*\` key is a log from an arm that is not this one**. **A hang past the 1.2 s command bound is not a possible failure of this press**: the bound is inside \`st_send_command\` and the run's own ending is unmoved."
    rung_para 19 " **THIS RUNG MOVES ONE VARIABLE AND ITS ANSWER IS A THREEWAY, WHICH IS WHY ITS CELL IS A CONTRAST.** \`_nrsp_complete = 1\` with \`_nrsp_status_any != 0\` says the response DEMAND is what stalled CMD3 - the one command this ladder ever drove to a completion, CMD0, is the one that asks for no response - and then the subject is the response path: \`RESPONSE 0x10\`, \`SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\`, and the \`INT_ENABLE\` bit this ladder has been carrying since rung 13. \`_nrsp_inhibit_seen > 0\` with \`_nrsp_complete = 0\` says a no-response command stalls the same way, so the demand is EXONERATED and the subject is the opcode or the block's own state - and then the census moves to \`SLOT_INT_STATUS 0xFC\` and \`CORE_PWRCTL_STATUS 0xDC\`, the two registers 726 section 3 named and no rung has yet read at a moment when there was a completion to report. \`_nrsp_inhibit_seen = 0\` with \`_nrsp_word_read = 0x0300\` says the block never started it at all. **AND THE SECOND READING IS THE RESPONSE REGISTER**, which this body reads three times through the driver's own word-0 derivation - before the window, inside the command object's own \`resp\`, and after the publishes: with the demand removed nothing should clear \`RESPONSE 0x10\` during the command, so \`_nrsp_resp_post\` should still hold CMD1's word \`0x40ff8080\`, which rungs 17 and 18 both measured there. **If the R1 command emptied the register and the no-response command does not, the CLEARING is tied to the response demand** - and that is 742 section 1's reading confirmed rather than inferred from a single press. **Read \`_nrsp_resp\` with care**: the command asked for no response, so \`rsp_present\` is zero and that word is a reading of the REGISTER, not this command's answer - which is exactly why the three R1 decode cells rung 19 publishes are absent here. **The R1 fields belong to rung 19's log and to no other**: a word decoded here would be CMD1's OCR wearing a card-status shape."

    rung_para 20 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS A VERDICT ON A RULE RATHER THAN ON THE BLOCK, WHICH IS WHY ITS CELL IS A THREE-WAY AND ITS FIRST BRANCH IS AN EXPLANATION.** The rule fits all five words this ladder has put on this bus - \`0x0000\` started and completed, \`0x0300\` started and completed, \`0x031A\` started and never finished, \`0x0102\` and \`0x0209\` never started - and it says a word that asks for a response AND carries no \`INDEX\` is declined. \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0\` beside \`_nidx_word_read = 0x030A\` CONFIRMS it in one reading and explains CMD1 and CMD2 in the same stroke; \`_nidx_inhibit_seen > 0\` with \`_nidx_complete = 0\` kills its \`INDEX\` half and moves the subject back to the response itself; \`_nidx_complete = 1\` kills it outright. **AND THE ROWS THIS VERDICT IS A VERDICT AGAINST ARE NOT IN THIS LOG**: CMD0's completion and rung 19's never-finishing CMD3 are in 746's and 742's captures, so the comparison the three-way rests on is one this press supplies half of. **READ THE RUNG SPELLING WITH CARE**: the switch VALUE is 20 and this record calls the same arm its ORDINAL rung 21, so a sentence quoting one spelling against a capture named in the other is quoting one arm's cell in the other arm's name (755 section 7). **AND READ THE RESPONSE CELLS AS A REGISTER, NOT AS AN ANSWER**: this command does ask for a response so the word is live, and the decode is legitimate - but a field is a decode of whatever the register holds, and \`_nidx_complete\` with \`_nidx_inhibit_last\` is what says whether anything answered at all."

    rung_para 21 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS THREE REGISTERS, AND IT IS THE LADDER'S FIRST ARM THAT COULD RETURN NOTHING TO DO.** 756 section 6 pre-registered three outcomes per register and every one of them names the next act, so a press spent here is never wasted: \`_dll_rst = 1\` with \`_dll_pdn = 1\` kills the vendor-write hypothesis outright and closes the search space 761 section 5 enumerated at two, and NOTHING further is owed on this path. \`_dll_rst = 0\` or \`_dll_pdn = 0\` makes the write the cheapest untried thing - and that write is the NEXT rung, with its own pre-registration, because it is a store to a register no rung has written. \`_dll_ctrl2_uhs = 0\` closes the HOST_CONTROL2 half in one cell; nonzero makes the vendor's \`ctrl_2\` write live as well. **AND THE THREE CELLS ARE TAKEN AT THE ONE MOMENT THEY MEAN ANYTHING**: before the first command and after rung 9's wait, which is the position rung 18's \`st_resp_before\` takes and the reason this rung's clause refuses a body placed on the other side of \`st_cmd_path\`. **READ \`_dll_config\` AND \`_dll_status\` WHOLE AS WELL AS BY BIT**: \`_dll_rst\`/\`_dll_pdn\`/\`_dll_en\`/\`_dll_ckout\` are four of thirty-two bits of one word and \`_dll_lock\` is one of thirty-two of the other, and the words are published so a reader with a different question does not have to spend a press on the twenty-eight bits this arm does not name. **AND THIS ARM STILL SENDS THE RUNG-21 COMMAND**, deliberately: rung 21's call site is guarded \`>= 20\` and this rung moves no guard on a rung that has already been pressed, so the log carries a SECOND SAMPLE of the \`0x030A\` stall beside the DLL cells - which costs no reading and is the reason this rung is purely additive. **A \`_dll_loads = 3\` beside \`_dll_done = 3\` is a complete run of this body**; anything less is the run stopping inside three reads, and the count is published bottom-up so the record's \"three reads\" and the log's number are two derivations of one fact."
    # **766: AND THIS RUNG'S PARAGRAPH IS THE ONE ITS PREDECESSOR'S HAD TO BE CAREFUL ABOUT.** The
    # rung-21 arm could return "nothing further is owed on this path"; this arm cannot, because its
    # whole point is that the ladder's most-quoted negative has been a reading about a MASK and its
    # four outcomes each send the next press somewhere different.
    rung_para 22 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS A FOUR-ROW TABLE, AND IT IS THE LADDER'S FIRST ARM WHOSE QUESTION IS ABOUT ITS OWN EVIDENCE RATHER THAN ABOUT THE BLOCK.** 765 measured that the vendor's \`sdhci_init\` enables ELEVEN status bits into \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` while this ladder has enabled ONE, and that 730's press had already shown, on this very block, that a status bit is latched only while its enable stands - so \`_status_any = 0\` over five million samples is *no ENABLED bit was latched* as much as *the block said nothing*, and the two could not be told apart from any log this ladder has ever produced. A press spent here separates them, and every row of the table names the next act: \`0x00010000\` alone means the CARD is silent and the controller knows it (the subject becomes the card and the bus, not the register file, and the whole 'this controller never sets its status' reading of rungs 13-22 is refuted in one bit); a CRC, END_BIT or INDEX bit means the card ANSWERED and the word was malformed, which puts the subject on sampling and bus width; NOTHING with the enables up is the strong form of the negative - a command held 1.2 s that never armed its own response timeout, a statement about the block's command path; and the sticky bit 15 appearing alone means it is a property of the IP rather than a report. **THE ENABLE WORD IS FIVE BITS AND NOT THE VENDOR'S ELEVEN** - \`ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD\` = \`0x000F0001\`, the completion with TIMEOUT, CRC, END_BIT and INDEX - because the other six are \`AUTO_CMD_ERR\` and the \`DATA_*\` half and neither is a fact about the card. **AND THE SAFETY HALF IS 730'S OWN MEASUREMENT**: the line rises on the CONJUNCTION of the two enable registers, \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` is still never written at this rung, and 730 wrote \`INT_ENABLE\` on this block, took no command and took no interrupt. **TWO NEW CELLS RIDE ALONG AND NEITHER REPLACES THE TABLE**: \`_nidx_status_pre\` (\`INT_STATUS\` with the wider mask in place, before this arm's command) and \`_nidx_tout_ctl\` (\`TIMEOUT_CONTROL 0x2E\` as a byte, the register no rung has read - and a NON-ZERO value there would be a finding, because the vendor's own path writes it only for a command with data or \`MMC_RSP_BUSY\`). **THE PRIMARY CELL IS \`_nidx_status_any\` AND IT IS TAKEN BY MACHINERY THAT ALREADY EXISTS**: \`st_send_command\`'s poll publishes the raw unmasked \`INT_STATUS\` word it first saw non-zero in, so this rung's question is asked by the same 5.09-million-sample poll that has always read zero, under a mask that can now register an answer."
    rung_para 23 " **THIS RUNG ASKS ABOUT A PIN AND NOT ABOUT A REGISTER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF IT.** Every arm since rung 13 has read what the block's own registers hold; 764 reduced the remaining candidate space to three and left TWO of them indistinguishable from any log this ladder has produced - the card not being powered, and the block never having put anything on the bus. **\`PRESENT_STATE 0x24\` bit 24 IS THE CMD LINE'S OWN LEVEL, AND IT IS THE ONLY CELL THAT SEPARATES THEM**, because a line that never goes LOW was driven by nobody and a line that moves was driven by something. **NO ARM HAS BEEN ABLE TO MAKE THAT MEASUREMENT**, and the reason is a one-sample problem: \`_nidx_inhibit_last\` publishes bit 24 as the LAST sample of the run, and HIGH at the end is what an IDLE line and a FINISHED transmission BOTH look like. **This arm COUNTS instead**: over the SAME first 1024 samples \`_nidx_inhibit_seen\` counts, it counts the ones in which the line was LOW. **AND THE COUNT COSTS NO DEVICE ACCESS AT ALL** - the 724 window already reads \`PRESENT_STATE 0x24\` once per iteration into \`inhibit_last\`, so the new cell is one mask on a value already in a register: no new register, no new width, no new window, no new megabyte and NO NEW STORE TO THE BLOCK. That was measured on the built image rather than argued - the classifier reports \`st_send_command\`'s device set and its image-side set UNCHANGED from rung 23 - and a relaxation of that clause was drafted and then DELETED, because a check weakened for a reason that is not true is worse than no change at all. **READ \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen\` AGAINST ITS NEIGHBOUR AND NOT ALONE**: it is over the same window as \`_nidx_inhibit_seen\`, so \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0x400\` with \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen = 0\` is a block that held its inhibit for all 1024 samples while the line never moved. **ITS ZERO IS A READING ONLY IF THE SAMPLER OUTRAN THE BUS**: a sampler slower than one bit period could miss a transmission and report a zero, so the sampling period is read BESIDE the count - \`_nidx_polls\` and \`_nidx_ticks\` are over exactly the window the count is over - and never instead of it. **AND THE ARM STILL CARRIES RUNG 23'S FOUR-ROW ENABLE TABLE UNCHANGED**, so one press answers the pin question and the enable question together: this rung moves no guard on a rung that has already been pressed. **AND IT HAS NOW BEEN PRESSED - 2026-09-27 09:05:22-09:06:36 UTC, EXIT 0, 74 s, capture rung24-cmdline-20260927-090636-last_kmsg.txt - SO RE-PRESSING THIS ARM BUYS NOTHING.** It landed ROW 1 of the four-row table exactly: \`_nidx_status_any = 0x00018000\` = \`SDHCI_INT_ERROR\` + \`SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT\`, \`_nidx_err = 0x00010000\`, \`_nidx_complete = 0\`, a break at poll 1789 (\`_nidx_timeout = 0\` - not the 1.2 s budget) after **665.2 us**, which is \`TIMEOUT_CONTROL\`'s \`0x00\` doing exactly what 2^13 TMCLK cycles predicts. **AND \`_nidx_cmdlow_seen = 0x187\` - the CMD line went LOW 391 of the first 1024 samples** - so 764 section 4's candidate 3 (the block never put anything on the bus) is RETIRED, and candidate 2 (the response timeout not armed) is retired by the 665 us itself. **THE LARGER RESULT IS IN THE SAME LOG FROM RUNG 11: \`_cmd0_complete = 1\` with \`_cmd0_status_any = 0x00000001\` at poll 537 in 255.4 us** - a no-response command completes and says so, while the response-demanding one is issued, drives the line, and arms and FIRES its own timeout. **THE RUNGS-13-22 HEADLINE IS FALSE**: this controller does set its status. **AND 771 REFUTED THE REASON THIS SENTENCE USED TO GIVE, OUT OF THIS VERY LOG.** It read *it did not, because the error-enable half of \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` was zero* - and the same capture's own rows say otherwise: \`INT_ENABLE\` was \`0x00000001\`, bit for bit the same word, when CMD0 was issued (\`_ena_wrote\`) and when CMD2 was (\`_cid_ena_wrote\`), and \`CMD0\` completed and latched \`INT_RESPONSE\` while \`CMD2\` latched NOTHING across \`_cid_polls\` = 5,088,256 polls. **THE ENABLE WORD SEPARATES THOSE TWO ROWS IN NEITHER DIRECTION**, so the attribution is WITHDRAWN: \`_nidx_status_pre = 0x00018000\` is still a real reading, but \`_cid_status_post = 0\` was read immediately before it and the only write between the two is the enable store itself - so whether that word arrived during CMD2 or is the store's own effect on this block is UNSEPARATED, and 771 names the two reads (one \`INT_STATUS\` before the store, one after) that would separate it. **WHAT DOES TRACK THE OUTCOME IS \`CMD_INHIBIT\`**: \`_inhibit_seen\` is 536 / 0 / 0 / 1024 for CMD0 / CMD1 / CMD2 / CMD3 against completed / nothing / nothing / TIMEOUT at 665.2 us - the block's own acknowledgement that it took the command - and CMD2's is 0, which is why the rung-16 window bought it nothing (\`_cid_complete = 0\`, and 771 refutes that window's stated rationale as a PREDICTION). **AND THE ONE CELL THIS ARM'S TABLE IS MISSING COSTS NO DEVICE ACCESS AT ALL**: \`cid_inhibit_after\` is filled into the result struct by every command and is printed by no publish block, so it is absent from EVERY capture in the archive. \`_nidx_sig_enable = 0\` and NO \`_irq_other_*\` key anywhere. **THE FRONTIER IS NOW THE CARD AND THE BUS: the card does not answer, on a bus the controller is demonstrably driving - so the next press's subject is the card's power and the clock at its pins, the two things a register read of the controller cannot see.** **AND 771 NARROWED IT FROM THE SAME LOG**: the CLOCK is not a candidate - \`clk_set_max_clk = 0x16E36000\` = 384 MHz against \`clk_set_real_div = 0x3C0\` = 960 is **400,000 Hz exactly**, and \`cmd2_clock_control = 0x0000E047\` carries \`SD_CLOCK_CARD_EN\` - so every command in the table ran with the card clock enabled and correct, and the remaining subject is the PADS AND THE RAILS and not the clock register. **"the clock at its pins" IS STILL UNREAD BY ANYTHING** - that is what rung 25 reads one register of.  Rung 23's \`armed-storage-3a92aa52\` is a SUPERSEDED, UNSPENT park: its question is answered."
    # **770: THE RUNG-25 PARAGRAPH, AND IT ALSO CARRIES A CORRECTION THIS FILE HAS OWED SINCE RUNG 12.**
    # The shared storage consequence paragraph - the one that opens "THIS ARM READS A BLOCK THE IMAGE
    # HAS NEVER TOUCHED" - is 692/693 era and it closes "a `_loads=6` with `_writes=0` is the run this
    # arm exists for". A guard corrects that sentence for the rungs 2..11, and rungs 3 and 4 correct it
    # again in their own paragraphs - and **every rung from 12 on carries it UNCORRECTED**, which this
    # arm is the first to state in its own paragraph. It is written per-rung rather than as a range
    # guard because a range guard would be one generic sentence about arms this file has not re-read
    # one by one; the same sentence is OWED for values 12..23, which are narrations of arms already
    # spent - so nothing is bought or lost by the omission, and it is recorded rather than fixed blind.
    rung_para 24 " **THIS RUNG'S ANSWER IS ONE READ OF A REGISTER IN A DEVICE THIS IMAGE HAS NEVER TOUCHED, AND NO ARM HAS EVER BEEN ABLE TO ASK FOR IT.** Every rung in this ladder reads the controller at \`0xF98\`; this one reads the TLMM at \`0xFD5\` - the SDC1 dedicated-pad drive/pull register at physical \`0xFD512044\`, which the vendor writes ONLY from inside \`sdhci_msm_pwr_irq\`'s \`CORE_PWRCTL_BUS_ON\` branch (\`sdhci-msm.c:2015-2032\`) and which no rung has ever read or written. **The cell that matters is PULL SDC1_CMD**, because MMC's CMD line is open-drain during identification and returns HIGH only through it - so \`_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` retires 768 section 5's other half and sends the next rung to the PMIC rails, while \`_pad_raw = 0x00000000\`, or any field at odds with the value the board's own DT arrays produce, says the vendor act was never applied on this path and makes the next rung a single read-modify-write of ONE register whose section this arm has already installed. **AND NEITHER ROW IS A VERDICT ABOUT THE CARD**: the read is taken BEFORE the command path, so row 1 is the pads' STATE and not a proof that this image set it, and \`_pad_match = 1\` buys an ELIMINATED CANDIDATE and never a working bus. **AND ONE SENTENCE IN THE SHARED PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS NOT THIS ARM'S**: \"a \`_loads=6\` with \`_writes=0\` is the run this arm exists for\" is rung 1's, and there is no \`_loads\`/\`_writes\` pair in this arm's answer at all - **what this arm exists for is \`xnu_live_storage_pad_raw\`**, with \`_pad_map\`, \`_pad_read\` and \`_pad_writes\` as the three cells that say whether the read was reachable, taken, and unaccompanied by any store. **What is NOT new**: no store anywhere (the body's device set is one \`ldr\` and the build refuses a \`str\`), no command, no sector read, nothing mounted, and \`SIGNAL_ENABLE 0x38\` is still read and never written - so this arm cannot raise the block's SPI 123 line, which is the safety reading every rung since 17 has carried."
    # **772: THE RUNG-26 PARAGRAPH, AND IT NAMES A CLASS RATHER THAN A REGISTER.** Every arm since 13
    # was built by finding a NEW register to read; this one was built by noticing that a cell the
    # ladder ALREADY MEASURES is printed for only one of the four commands that fill it, and that the
    # one field the fourth row of 771's table needs was being computed and dropped. It is recorded
    # per-rung because the correction is about the four commands this ladder issues, and because the
    # value-24 build from the same source is byte-identical to the parked rung-25 arm - MEASURED, so
    # the containment claim is a comparison of two artifacts and not a reading of four `#if`s.
    rung_para 25 " **THIS RUNG'S WHOLE ACT IS TO PRINT FOUR VALUES IT WAS ALREADY MEASURING - AND IT IS THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER WITH NOTHING NEW TO TOUCH.** 771 read the rung-24 press as ONE FOUR-ROW TABLE and found that the four commands this ladder issues are NOT the same experiment: \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen = 0x218\` beside a command that completed and latched \`INT_RESPONSE\`, \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` and \`_cid_inhibit_seen = 0\` beside 5.09 M polls each with NOTHING latched at all, and \`_nidx_inhibit_seen = 0x400\` beside a block that armed AND FIRED its own response timeout at 665.2 us. **AND THE ONLY TRACE OF WHAT THE CMD LINE WAS DOING DURING CMD1 OR CMD2 IS ONE SAMPLE**: \`inhibit_last\` bit 24 - the CMD line's own level - reads LOW for CMD1 and HIGH for CMD2 on EVERY capture in the archive (10 cmd0, 8 cmd1, 7 cid), because the sampler's own COUNT of that bit is filled for EVERY command and rung 24 published it for \`nidx\` ALONE. A single sample taken at the end of a window is exactly the reading rung 24 replaced with a count for CMD3; this arm finishes that repair for the other three. **AND \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` IS THE CELL 771 NEEDS MOST, BECAUSE THE ARCHIVE ALREADY CARRIES A CONTRADICTION**: \`_cmd1_inhibit_last = 0x00F80000\` - bit 24 LOW - on every capture while \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` over all 1024 samples, so on this block the line can sit LOW with \`CMD_INHIBIT\` never rising, and **\`the block never started this command\` and \`the block drove the line and the inhibit bit is not a reliable witness\` are INDISTINGUISHABLE from one sample.** The count separates them: 0 says the line never moved at all, and anything else says it did. **READ EACH COUNT BESIDE ITS OWN NEIGHBOUR AND NEVER ALONE**: \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen\`, \`_cid_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cid_inhibit_seen\`, \`_cmd0_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_cmd0_inhibit_seen = 0x218\` - and \`_cid_inhibit_after\` beside \`_cmd0_inhibit_after = 1\` and \`_nidx_inhibit_after = 1\`, the two rows of 771's table that DO carry it. **ITS ZERO IS A READING ONLY IF THE SAMPLER OUTRAN THE BUS**: a sampler slower than one bit period could miss a transmission and report a zero, so the sampling period is read BESIDE the count - \`_cmd1_polls\` and \`_cmd1_ticks\` are over exactly the window the count is over - and never instead of it. **AND THE CELL IS FREE, WHICH IS THE POINT**: all four keys were already in a register - \`cmdlow_seen\` for cmd0/cmd1/cid, and \`cid_inhibit_after\`, which \`st_send_command\` fills for every command and NO publish block has ever printed - so this arm adds NO device access, NO register, NO window, NO megabyte and NO store of any kind. **AND IT STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 25, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 25\` and the value-24 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-f72e9f18\` (\`f72e9f18...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so one press answers the SDC1 pad question AND these four cells, and **rung 25's park need never be pressed**. **AND NEITHER CELL IS A VERDICT ABOUT THE CARD**: they say what the BLOCK did with the line, and 771 already retired \`RESPONSE\` in both directions, so a nonzero count is not a response and a zero is not proof the card was never addressed. **THE PAD ROW IS CARRIED UNCHANGED**: \`xnu_live_storage_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` retires 768 section 5's other half and sends the next rung to the PMIC rails, while \`0x00000000\`, or any field at odds with the value the board's own DT arrays produce, makes it a single read-modify-write of ONE register whose section this arm has already installed."
    # **775: THE RUNG-27 PARAGRAPH, AND IT IS THE SAME CLASS 772 NAMED - MEASURED AND THROWN AWAY -
    # ONE ROW FURTHER ON.** 772 found cells the sampler fills for every command and prints for one;
    # this finds the OTHER half of that gap: a publish block written at rung 16 that never grew when
    # rungs 12 and 24 added fields to the struct it prints. Recorded per-rung because the correction is
    # about the CMD2 row of 771's table, and because the value-25 build from the same source is
    # byte-identical to the parked rung-26 arm - MEASURED, so containment is a comparison of artifacts.
    rung_para 26 " **THIS RUNG PRINTS THE REST OF THE CMD2 RESULT - ELEVEN FIELDS \`st_send_command\` FILLS FOR EVERY COMMAND AND THE \`st_all_send_cid\` PUBLISH BLOCK HAS NEVER PRINTED, BECAUSE THAT BLOCK WAS WRITTEN AT RUNG 16 AND NEVER GREW WHEN RUNGS 12 AND 24 ADDED THEM TO THE STRUCT.** \`st_all_send_cid\` publishes TEN of the struct's twenty-seven fields; the fifteen it drops include the entrance state, the whole inhibit gate, the clear, the argument and the two companions that make a zero response a reading rather than a silence. **NO NEW DEVICE ACCESS, NO REGISTER, NO WINDOW, NO MEGABYTE AND NO STORE**: every one of the eleven is already in the struct when the block runs, and the build's \`st_send_command\` clause asserts that body's device set UNCHANGED from rung 26. **AND ONE OF THE ELEVEN DECIDES SOMETHING NO OTHER CELL IN THE ARCHIVE CAN.** 771 saw the anomaly and could not read it: the block ACKNOWLEDGES CMD0 (inhibit rises, \`INT_RESPONSE\` latches) and CMD3 (inhibit held for all 1024 samples, \`INT_TIMEOUT\` latches at 665.2 us) but raises NOTHING for CMD1 and CMD2 - \`_cmd1_inhibit_seen = 0\` and \`_cid_inhibit_seen = 0\`, with \`_cmd1_any_polls = 0\` over \`_cmd1_polls\` = 5,090,304 reads that were ALL ZERO. And the press DOES carry \`_nidx_status_pre = 0x00018000\`, so \`ERROR | TIMEOUT\` was latched somewhere between the poll of CMD2 giving up and the entrance of CMD3 - while **the ONLY write in that interval is a store to \`0x34\`, which 736 measured sets bit 15 by itself**. So whether bit 16 (\`INT_TIMEOUT\`) is a real CMD2 timeout that arrived after the poll gave up, or the enable store's own effect, is **UNSEPARATED by every capture on record**. **\`_cid_stale\` IS THAT READING TAKEN ONE COMMAND EARLIER** - \`INT_STATUS 0x30\` as \`st_send_command\` finds it for CMD2, which is the first read of that register since the poll of CMD1 gave up 1.2 seconds earlier. **AND 777 CORRECTED THE SENTENCE THAT USED TO FOLLOW, WHICH WAS FALSE**: it read *in an interval that contains no write to \`0x34\` at all*, and \`st_all_send_cid\` writes \`0x34\` at \`entry_storage.c:3110\` while \`_cid_stale\` is read inside \`st_send_command\` at \`:2560\` - after that store and after its own inhibit gate. By 736's measurement that store raises bit 15 by itself, so **\`_cid_stale >= 0x00008000\` on EVERY run and the ZERO row does not exist**: read \`== 0x00008000\` EXACTLY as *nothing but this arm's own store is in the register*, and anything ABOVE it as *something was latched that this arm did not put there*. **ITS ROW IS A READING ONLY IF SOMETHING WOULD HAVE SET IT, AND ON THIS CELL THE ARM ITSELF SETS IT**, so read it beside \`_cid_stale\`'s neighbours - \`_cid_clear_after = 0\` and \`_cmd1_status_any = 0\` are the same register at two other moments - and beside \`_cid_status_any\`, which is what the poll itself saw. **AND THE OTHER TEN ARE NOT DECORATION**: \`_cid_inhibit_timeout = 1\` would make CMD2 a REFUSAL AT THE GATE rather than a command that ran and stalled (\`_cmd1_inhibit_timeout = 0\` and \`_cid_inhibit_ticks\`/\`_polls\` are what say which), \`_cid_ps_before\`/\`_cid_ps_after\` are the block's own state either side, and \`_cid_rsp_present\` beside \`_cid_resp_read\` is the pair rung 12 introduced because a zero \`RESPONSE\` on rung 11 was the ABSENCE OF A READ wearing the shape of a value. **AND IT STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 26, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 26\`, and the value-25 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-a1378a48\` (\`a1378a48...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so **one press answers the SDC1 pad question, rung 26's four cells AND these eleven, and rungs 23, 25 and 26 need never be pressed.** **AND A BUILD DEFECT IS RECORDED HERE BECAUSE IT IS INVISIBLE IN A DIFF (m778)**: the first build of this arm was refused with \`entry_storage.c:116: error: missing terminating ' character\`, and the cause was an ODD NUMBER OF APOSTROPHES in the appended ladder text - \`#error\`'s text is a single double-quoted string, so an odd count leaves one character constant unterminated. Rewording three possessives took the inserted segment from three apostrophes to none. **AND NEITHER CELL IS A VERDICT ABOUT THE CARD**: they are about what the BLOCK did, and 771 already retired \`RESPONSE\` in both directions."

    rung_para 27 " **THE FIRST ARM IN THIS LADDER THAT STORES TO A SECOND DEVICE - AND ITS FIRST ROW WRITES NOTHING.** 773 section 4 pre-registered this design and 773 section 5 fixed its shape: read \`0xFD512044\`, compare against the value this board's own arrays produce, write ONLY if it does not match, read back. \`st_pad_census\` - the body rung 25 added to READ the TLMM's SDC1 pad-control register - now ends in an \`if\`, so the arm is a FORK: \`_pad_raw = 0x00009F24\` with \`_pad_write_skipped = 1\` and \`_pad_writes = 0\` means **THE PADS WERE ALREADY CONFIGURED** and NO STORE WAS MADE AT ALL, and anything else with \`_pad_write_skipped = 0\` means the vendor's pad act was never applied on this path and the write HAS BEEN MADE, with \`_pad_wrote\`, \`_pad_after\` and \`_pad_after_match\` beside it. **THE ONE ACT IS ONE MASKED READ-MODIFY-WRITE OF ONE REGISTER**: \`(raw & ~MASK) | EXPECT\` over the union of the seven fields (hdrive clk/cmd/data at 6/3/0, pull clk/cmd/data/rclk at 13/11/9/15, union \`0x0001FFFF\`), so the bits outside the seven are the bits the read handed back and this is a field write and not a whole-word write to a register a bootloader may have owned. **AND THE VALUE IS THE VENDOR'S OWN**: \`sdhci_msm_setup_pad\` (\`sdhci-msm.c:964\`) walks those same seven fields to those same two arrays, so the guard is what makes the ANSWER readable and NOT what makes the store safe - even an unguarded fire would be a word Linux itself stores at every \`CORE_PWRCTL_BUS_ON\`. **THE STORE IS A PLAIN RMW WITH A MASK**, field for field \`msm_tlmm_set_field\`'s (\`gpio-msm-common.c:481-496\`) - no unlock, no write-one-to-clear, no FIFO, no shadow register - which is why ONE store is safe where the vendor's six interleaved read-modify-writes would need an argument. **AND THE BUILD REFUSES THE ARM WITHOUT THE GUARD, TWICE AND INDEPENDENTLY**: the \`str\` must be PREDICATED or SPANNED BY A CONDITIONAL BRANCH in the body's own disassembly, AND the source must carry exactly one \`if (raw == ST_TLMM_SDC1_EXPECT) {\` with the store in its ELSE arm, read by line number. **AND THE SAFETY READING IS A COUNT AND NOT A CONSTANT**: \`_pad_writes\` is published as what happened, so \`0\` and \`1\` are the only values a well-formed run can carry. **WHAT IT CANNOT DO**: reach another register, another pad bank (\`0x2044\` is not SDC2's \`0x2048\`, and the seven masks are disjoint so their union is their sum), a FUNCTION (every field is drive strength or pull), the PMIC, the rails or any clock - acts 1 and 3 of the vendor BUS_ON branch are the two 773 counted as unreachable and this arm does not reach them. **AND THE FORK'S SECOND ROW IS NARROWER THAN IT READS (779)**: the guard's word is MTP V5's, and this device's own dt.img declares TWO SDC1 pad words - \`0x9F24\` on four of its trees and \`0x9FE4\` on the fifth - with the bootloader's choice unestablished here. So \`_pad_write_skipped = 0\` means *the register did not hold \`0x9F24\`* and NOT *the vendor's act was never applied*: a register holding \`0x9FE4\` is a configured pad this arm re-configures, and \`_pad_after_match = 1\` is not the statement that the fix landed on an unconfigured pad. \`_pad_raw\` is the reading; the source's citation of \`msm8974pro-ac-pm8941-mtp-v5.dts\` as *the Mi 4's own board file* is wrong and owed (\`entry_storage.c:302-303\`); and \`records/sdc1-pad-candidates.txt\` is the tracked derivation, with \`tools/check_sdc1_pad_expectation.py\` in \`make check\`. **AND IT DOES NOT SAY THE PADS ARE THE CAUSE**: \`0x9F24\` already present is the MORE LIKELY row, and even then \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` is necessary and not sufficient. **AND IT STRICTLY CONTAINS RUNG 27, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED**: every new line sits inside \`#if STAGE90_XNU_STORAGE_PROBE >= 27\` and the value-26 build from this same source came out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the parked \`armed-storage-8096cb2c\` (\`8096cb2c...\`, 5552764 bytes) - so **one press answers the pad question, the fix question, rung 26's four cells, rung 27's eleven publishes and rung 23's window, and rungs 23, 25, 26 and 27 need never be pressed.** **AND THE 737 WINDOW PARAGRAPH IS CORRECTED IN THIS SAME BUILD**: 771 refuted its rationale as a prediction and the correction was owed for a COST reason - a comment that changes forces a build, and this is the arm that carries it. **AND THE FORK IS A TABLE WITH FOUR ROWS AND NOT TWO, BECAUSE THE REGISTER HAS TWO STATES AND THE SECOND ONE IS NAMED IN THE SAME DECLARATIONS (782).** The same device trees that produce the ON words also declare \`qcom,pad-pull-off\` and \`qcom,pad-drv-off\`, and the same arithmetic on those arrays gives the OFF word: \`0x00009E00\` - the SAME pull fields as \`0x9F24\` and \`0x9E00\` of them, with the drive fields at ZERO - and ALL FIVE trees that carry the node declare that same off word. \`tools/derive_sdc1_pads.py\` now derives and prints it beside the on word, \`records/sdc1-pad-candidates.txt\` carries it as a \`# offword 0x........\` line, and \`tools/check_sdc1_pad_expectation.py\` in \`make check\` REFUSES a guard constant equal to an off word - because the guard asks *does the register already hold the word I expect*, so a constant set to the off word would report a powered-down pad as ALREADY CONFIGURED. **READ \`_pad_raw\` AS FOUR ROWS, NOT TWO**: \`0x00009F24\` - the pads are at the vendor BUS_ON state, and \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` is present; \`0x00009FE4\` - the pads are configured to the OTHER declared board's word (CLK hdrive 7 instead of 4), and \`PULL SDC1_CMD\` is present, so 769 section 2's mechanism is retired on this row too; \`0x00009E00\` - the PULLS ARE PRESENT AND THE DRIVE IS OFF, a state no \`CORE_PWRCTL_BUS_ON\` leaves and which a two-row fork merges with "never applied", and on this row the pad candidate is CLOSED for the pull reason while the drive fields stay the subject; \`0x00000000\` or anything else - a state NO declaration in this archive produces, which is the row that would say a bootloader used its own values. **AND ON EVERY ROW THE DRIVE HALF IS NOW STRONGER THAN A MERE COMPARISON**: the off word and the on words differ ONLY in the three hdrive fields, so \`_pad_raw\`'s pull half is the same on rows 1, 2 and 3 - which is why \`_pad_raw\` itself and not \`_pad_write_skipped\` is the cell to read."
    # **785: THE RUNG-29 PARAGRAPH, AND IT IS THE ONE 783 ASKED FOR.** 783 read the SCOPE of rung
    # 23's widening rather than its rule and found it had reached ONE OF THREE command windows; 785
    # widened the other two. Recorded per-rung for rung 27's reason - the paragraph is about the two
    # windows THIS arm moves and not about the ladder's mask in general - and it must not be folded
    # into 783's text, because 783 narrates a READING and this narrates an ACT.
    rung_para 28 " **AND THIS RUNG'S CONSEQUENCE IS THE ONE 783 ASKED FOR, STATED AS A SCOPE: at value 28 NO COMMAND WINDOW IN THIS LADDER WRITES THE ONE-BIT ENABLE.** \`st_cmd_path\` and \`st_all_send_cid\` now store \`ST_SDHCI_INT_ENABLE_CMD\` (\`0x000F0001\`) where they stored \`ST_SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\` (\`0x00000001\`), which is the constant rung 23 gave \`st_cmd3_noidx\` - with 786's correction that \`st_cmd_path\`'s window covers CMD0 ALONE, since the \`>= 14\` restore closes it before the gate and CMD1. **WHAT THAT BUYS IS AN ERROR'S IDENTITY, AND 786's PRESS NARROWED THIS SENTENCE**: the CMD2 window returned \`_cid_status_any = 0x00028000\` = \`SDHCI_INT_ERR | SDHCI_INT_CRC\`, a NAMED error no earlier arm could name - but \`SDHCI_INT_ERR\` (bit 15) is enabled on EVERY arm and \`no write clears it\` (\`_ena_wrote_back = 0\` reads back \`0x00008000\`), and on this block it TRACKS the error bits (\`_nidx_status_any = 0x00018000\` = ERR|TIMEOUT beside \`_cid_status_any = 0x00028000\` = ERR|CRC), so a zero \`_status_any\` on a narrow arm was never blind to the EXISTENCE of an error. 765's gated view is real and NARROWER than this file used to state it: the widening buys identity and not existence. **AND CMD1's ROW DOES NOT MOVE AT ALL**, for the scope reason above. **AND WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THE READING IS NAMED**: a \`_status_any\` of 0 beside a large \`_any_polls\` means the block set NO bit of \`INT_STATUS\` at all during that command - neither the completion nor TIMEOUT - which is rung 22's silence reproduced where a timeout could have been seen; anything else is one of the three rows the arm's own sentence carries. **NO FIGURE FROM THIS ARM IS READ WITHOUT ITS COUNTER**: \`_any_polls\` beside \`_status_any\`, and \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen\` beside \`_inhibit_seen\`, because a zero count is a reading only where the sampler outran the bus (771's rule, one window over). **AND RUNG 28 IS ANSWERED IN THE SAME PRESS, MEASURED**: \`_pad_raw\`'s four-row fork is unchanged by this arm and its two artifacts are byte-identical to the park's."

    # **787: THE RUNG-30 PARAGRAPH, AND IT IS THE FIRST ONE IN THIS FILE THAT CAN SAY THE LADDER'S
    # OWN MASK MIGHT HAVE BEEN THE WHOLE STALL.** 786 read rung 29's capture and found that rung 14's
