    rung_para 29 " **THIS RUNG HAS BEEN PRESSED AND IT ANSWERED: CMD1 COMPLETED, AND THE 1.2 s 'TIMEOUT' EVERY ARM SINCE RUNG 14 REPORTED WAS THIS LADDER'S OWN MASK.** 2026-09-27 23:43:48-23:45:03 UTC, **EXIT 0**, 75 s gate-to-capture, with the operator's authorization taken BEFORE the gate was run; readiness 5 of 5; **ONE gate exit 0 / 606 lines**; **ONE runner, the only one**; no firer and none armed; \`fastboot boot\` only; nothing flashed; nothing written to storage; no reboot; serial 4a2fe00b; \`33e80afe\` absent from BOTH lists at fire time; capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung30-c1win-20260927-234510-{last_kmsg.txt,gate.log,run.log}\`, archived by hand, last_kmsg 636,238 B sha \`af710630...\`. **The runner's verdict is 7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD - eleven CRITERIA PRINTED TWICE IN ONE LOG** (the gate's reading of the log as it stood at gate time, which is the PREVIOUS capture, and the runner's of this one), so counting log LINES gives 14 PASS / 2 FAIL / 6 UNREAD and is the arithmetic 756 recorded as wrong; the single FAIL is the known \`seam_sp ... is not sleh_sp-8\` criterion and the UNREADs are the known absent-key ones, **so this press ADDED NO FAILURE** and its verdict is rung 29's criterion for criterion. **THE READING**: \`_cmd1_complete = 0x00000001\` and \`_cmd1_status_any = 0x00000001\` - **bit 0 (\`SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE\`), and NO ERROR BIT OF ANY KIND** - with \`_cmd1_err = 0\` and \`_cmd1_timeout = 0\`; the poll collapsed from rung 29's \`_cmd1_any_polls = 0x004da000\` (**5,087,232**) to \`0x000004d4\` (**1,236**) and \`_cmd1_ticks\` from \`0x015f9e16\` to \`0x00002823\` (10,275). **So of the four rows this paragraph used to lay out, the FIRST is the one that came back and the other three are dead**: \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` did not appear, CRC/END_BIT/INDEX did not appear, and 'nothing at all' did not appear - with all four error bits ENABLED the block raised none of them. \`_cmd1_stale = 0x00000001\`, \`_cmd1_clear_wrote = 0x00000001\` and \`_cmd1_clear_after = 0x00000000\` say the arm started from a latch it cleared and stayed clear, and \`_cmd1_cmdlow_seen = 0x28b\` (651 of the first 1024 samples, against rung 29's 650) says the line moved essentially the same amount as before - so the reading is about the BLOCK and not about a bus nobody drove. **THE WINDOW'S OWN CELLS CONFIRM IT WAS OPEN**: \`_c1_ena_wrote = _c1_ena_wrote_back = 0x000f0001\`, and \`_c1_ena_held = _c1_ena_readback = 0x000f8001\` - the five-bit word stored and the SAME word put back, which is a READING because the close takes what the open returned rather than re-evaluating the same expression. **AND THE CONTROL THAT TURNS THAT INTO A MEASUREMENT**: \`_cmd0_ticks\` - the same boot, the same bus, the same clock, the one command this ladder has ever seen complete with NO RESPONSE DEMAND - is \`0x1327\` = 4,903 on rung 29 and \`0x132a\` = **4,906** on rung 30, so **CMD0 reproduces across two arms to THREE TICKS, 0.06 PER CENT**, and the tick base is stable between them. \`_cmd1_ticks / _cmd0_ticks = 10,275 / 4,906 = **2.094**\` - CMD1's completed duration is 2.09 times a no-response command's, and a ratio near 2 is what **a command PLUS ITS 48-BIT RESPONSE** looks like beside a command alone. **THAT IS AN ARGUMENT AND NOT A PROOF, and the model is named rather than hidden**: with two points, \`t = N x frame + overhead\` cannot be solved for \`frame\` and \`overhead\` separately, so this is consistent with '+1 frame' WITHOUT fixing the frame length, and a block whose completion latency simply differs for a response-demanding word would also give a ratio above 1. What it does rule out is a CMD1 that took no time at all or the same time as CMD0. **AND 786 SECTION 4 AND 787 SECTION 4 ARE SUPERSEDED AS STATEMENTS, NOT AS READINGS.** 786 section 4's conclusion - *CMD1's row would not have moved at all* - is **REFUTED**: the row moved, from zero to bit 0. 787 section 4's candidate **(b)** - *bit 15 rises only when the specific error bit is enabled, so CMD1 may have been timing out INVISIBLY on every arm from rung 14 on* - is **REFUTED BY THIS PRESS**: the window carried all four error bits and CMD1 produced no error bit, so there was no invisible timeout. 787 section 4's candidate **(a)** reached the RIGHT CONSEQUENCE - *no error occurred at CMD1 and only bit 0 can be new* - **on a mechanism 788 section 3 had already killed** (bit 15 rises with any error whatever its own enable, against 788's 30,537,728 zero reads with bit 15 standing in \`INT_ENABLE\`). **So the account that actually fits is NEITHER (a) NOR (b) AS WRITTEN: it is 788 section 3's - the narrow window was BLIND - carried to the CMD1 window**, and 786 section 4 is right about the OUTCOME and wrong about the REASON, which is the third time on this ladder that a correct conclusion has been reached from a mechanism a later arm killed. **\`0x40ff8080\` IS STILL NOT AN ANSWER, AND THIS PRESS MAKES THAT STRONGER RATHER THAN WEAKER**: \`_cmd1_resp\` is **IDENTICAL ON RUNGS 19, 29 AND 30 - INCLUDING THE TWO WHOSE CMD1 WINDOW WAS CLOSED** - with \`_cmd0_resp = 0x00000000\` on all three, so the word is not this arm's own reading, and 787 section 6's warning holds in the stronger direction: it is not evidence the card answered AND it is not evidence that anything this arm changed reached the RESPONSE registers. **What the sequence does say, and it depends on NO enable bit**: \`_cmd0_resp\` is read BEFORE CMD1 and \`_cmd1_resp\` AFTER it, the image never writes those registers, so **the word entered them DURING CMD1 on every arm, seen or not** - and rung 19's own PRE-read of those same registers, which stands at the same \`0x40ff8080\`, taken before CMD3 was on the bus, is a third point on it. **AND IT IS STILL NOT DECIDABLE AS AN ANSWER**: three different commands read the same word out of it, \`_cmd1_resp_voltage = 0x00ff8000\` is OCR bits 23:15 all-ones - a legal full voltage window and equally what a static pattern looks like - and **there is NO \`_cmd1_raw_pre*\` / \`_cmd1_raw_post*\` PAIR AROUND CMD1's OWN RESPONSE REGISTERS**, unlike CMD2's \`_cid_raw0..3\` and CMD3's raw pair, so the one measurement that would turn a shared word into a reading does not exist. **That pair is the cheapest addition a future arm can make and it is OWED**, as is the comment at the head of CMD1 that says of this value *the card ANSWERED*. **THE FRONTIER MOVED ONE COMMAND DOWN, AND IT DID NOT MOVE AT CMD2.** CMD2 is BYTE FOR BYTE unchanged from rung 29 - \`_cid_status_any = 0x00028000\` (\`ERR | CRC\`) at \`_cid_any_polls = 0x00000001\`, \`_cid_err = 0x00020000\`, \`_cid_sent = 1\`, \`_cid_complete = 0\`, \`_cid_ticks = 0x00000011\` - so the CMD1 window left no trace on CMD2's own status. **AND CMD3 WAS NOT SENT ON THIS ARM, WHERE RUNG 29 SENT IT**: \`_nidx_calls = 1\` and \`_nidx_gated = 0\`, so the body WAS called and its window opened (\`_nidx_ena_wrote = 0x000f0001\`, \`_nidx_ena_held = 0x000f8001\`, \`_nidx_status_pre = 0x00028000\`), but \`_nidx_sent = 0\`, \`_nidx_word = 0\`, \`_nidx_arg_wrote = 0\`, \`_nidx_polls = 0\`, \`_nidx_ticks = 0\` - **the call into \`st_send_command\` RETURNED BEFORE IT STORED ANYTHING** because the driver's own \`CMD_INHIBIT\` guard (\`sdhci.c:1096\`'s bounded wait, which this body carries) expired: the block was still inhibited by CMD2 ten milliseconds after CMD2's window closed. **The evidence it is THAT return and not the latch guard below it is \`_nidx_clear_after = 0x00000000\`** - the second guard returns ONLY when \`clear_after\` is non-zero, so a zero there rules it out BY CONSTRUCTION. **And the cell that would say it directly is NOT PUBLISHED**: \`st_cmd3_noidx\`'s publisher block carries \`c3.timed_out\` as \`_nidx_timeout\` but **NOT \`c3.inhibit_timeout\`**, so this refusal is inferable rather than readable, and that is COST-owed to a build. **AND CMD2'S INHIBIT TAIL IS WHERE THE TWO ARMS DIFFER, WITH CMD2's OWN STATUS WORD IDENTICAL**: \`_cid_inhibit_after\` 0 to 1, \`_cid_inhibit_last\` \`0x01f80000\` to \`0x01f80001\`, \`_cid_inhibit_seen\` 0 to 1 - **CMD2 WEDGED AND STAYED WEDGED**, and no cell in either capture discriminates 'the state CMD2 was entered in' (on rung 30 CMD1 had actually completed, on rung 29 it had timed out) from a bare timing difference. **AND THE PRESS ADDS A FOURTH \`PRESENT_STATE\` WORD, THE ONE 789's 148-READ CENSUS WAS MISSING**: 789 found three values in which \`CMD_INHIBIT\` (bit 0) and the CMD line's level (bit 24) never appeared together - \`0x01f80000\` x142, \`0x01f80001\` x5, \`0x00f80000\` x1 - and rung 30's \`_cid_ps_after\` FIRST producer reads **\`0x00f80001\`: INHIBIT SET, CMD line LOW, at one instant.** It is the first word in the archive that says the block is holding a command in progress while its own CMD line is being driven down, which is the in-flight signature 789 section 1 could not find and 789 section 3 could only approach from one window later. **THE NEXT ARM'S SUBJECT IS WHY CMD2 GETS AN IMMEDIATE CRC WHILE CMD1 COMPLETES**, and three things separate the two commands and only three - response LENGTH (48 versus 136 bits), the CRC check (off versus on) and the OPCODE - with CMD2's error arriving at poll 1, about 0.9 microseconds after the command store, in a window whose 136-bit response cannot possibly have been shifted; that is 786 section 7's anomaly and 788 section 7's owed item, and it is now the cheapest thing in this ladder to pin. **THE GOAL IS STILL NOT MET** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made, so **TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD** - **AND 791 HAS NARROWED THAT SUBJECT HOST-SIDE, WITHOUT A PRESS.** Nine arms carry CMD2's cells and **exactly the two whose CMD2 window carried the CRC enable read non-zero** (\`_cid_status_any = 0x00028000\` = ERR|CRC at \`_cid_any_polls = 0x1\`); the seven narrow arms read zero over about 5.09 million polls each. The block latched it **inside a four-access bracket** - between \`_cid_clear_after = 0x00000000\` (the latch was clean) and the first poll, with only the ARGUMENT write, the COMMAND write and its two readbacks in between - and at **\`_cid_ticks = 0x11\` = 17, which is 0.35 PER CENT of \`_cmd0_ticks\` (4,901 to 4,909 across seven arms)**, so it is far too early for any frame and needs no clock frequency to say so. **AND THE CORRELATE IS THE RESPONSE LENGTH AND NOT THE CRC ENABLE**: CMD3's word \`0x030a\` carries the same CRC check and latches \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` with bit 17 never set, while CMD2's \`0x0209\` is the ladder's ONLY 136-bit request. **AND THE NARROW ZEROS ARE MASKED READINGS, MEASURED RATHER THAN INFERRED**: rung 24's CMD2 window was narrow, its poll read zero 5,091,328 times, and the NEXT body's wide read shows \`_nidx_status_pre = 0x00018000\` sitting in the latch - so \`_cid_status_post = 0\` does not mean a clean latch, which is true of all four \`*_status_post\` cells in the archive because every one of them is read through \`INT_ENABLE = 0\`. **AND THE PRE-REGISTERED NEXT ARM IS ONE BIT OUT OF CMD2's FLAGS**: \`PRESENT | 136\` with the CRC flag dropped, word \`0x0201\` in place of \`0x0209\`, nothing else moved - and its best row is **\`_cid_complete = 1\`, the CID TAKEN**, which is the largest single step available from here. and **THIS ARM IS SPENT, NO FURTHER ARM IS ARMED, AND NO PRESS IS AUTHORIZED.**"

    rung_para 30 " **THIS RUNG IS ONE BIT OUT OF THE CMD2 FLAG WORD, AND ITS FIRST ROW IS THE LARGEST SINGLE STEP THIS LADDER CAN TAKE WITHOUT LEAVING EARLY BRING-UP.** 791 censused every CMD2 the ladder has ever sent and found the immediate \`ERR | CRC\` unique to the two arms whose CMD2 window carried the CRC enable, then isolated the correlate by putting three commands beside each other with a wide enable: CMD1 (\`0x0102\`, 48-bit, CRC check OFF) COMPLETED, CMD3 (\`0x030a\`, 48-bit, CRC check ON) latched \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` with bit 17 NEVER set, and CMD2 (\`0x0209\`, **136-bit**, CRC check ON) latched \`ERR | CRC\` at poll 1 in 17 ticks. **The command word is a property of the command and not of the enable, so the correlate is the 136-BIT RESPONSE REQUEST** - and the one corner of that table the ladder has never measured is 136-bit with the CRC check OFF, which is this arm. **THE ACT IS \`ST_MMC_RSP_R2_NOCRC\` IN PLACE OF \`ST_MMC_RSP_R2\` AT ONE CALL SITE**, so the word moves from \`0x0209\` to \`0x0201\` and the opcode, the argument, the window, the position in the driver order and the five-bit \`INT_ENABLE 0x34\` are all unchanged. **IT ADDS NO NEW ADDRESS, NO NEW WIDTH, NO NEW MEGABYTE, NO NEW DEVICE AND NO NEW KEY** - \`_cid_flags\`, \`_cid_word\` and \`_cid_word_read\` are keys the ladder already carries and they publish the whole change - and because a body's device surface does not move when a constant moves, the clause group that reads \`st_all_send_cid\` out of the linked image is unchanged. **WHAT THE NEW CLAUSE READS INSTEAD IS THE ARGUMENT**, at the one place the linked image is unambiguous about it: the setup immediately before the \`bl\` to \`st_send_command\`, where this arm loads \`[r0=#2 (ALL_SEND_CID), r1=#0, r2=#3]\` and the rung below loads \`#7\`. **The expected value is DERIVED FROM THE SWITCH**, so a constant that leaked down the ladder is refused by the same clause that catches a rung which did not take effect, and the command WORD the constant folds to is pinned on the other side of the call by a \`_Static_assert\` pair - **one assertion on each side of the \`bl\`, and neither is a sentence in a comment**. **FOUR ROWS.** \`_cid_complete = 1\` and **the CID IS TAKEN**, after which the driver order is CMD9 (CSD), CMD7 (select), CMD16 (block length) and then data; \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` means the CRC latch needs the command's own CRC flag and the real condition at CMD2 is a 136-bit response that never arrives, which puts the subject back on the card and on the power/clock path 784 shaped; **\`ERR | CRC\` ANYWAY is the strongest reading the arm can return**, refuting 791 section 4's isolation and putting the 136-bit path itself on the block; and nothing at all over the full bound is the negative, read with the next window's own wide read beside it. \`_cmd0_ticks\` (4,901 to 4,909 across seven arms, 0.16 per cent) and CMD1's own completion bracket the new word from both ends, so a completion, a timeout and a CRC at poll 1 are distinguishable without a clock frequency. **AND \`0x40ff8080\` IS STILL NOT AN ANSWER ON ITS OWN**: it stands in the RESPONSE registers on rungs 19, 29 and 30 whether the window was open or closed. **THE GOAL IS NOT MET BY THIS ARM ALONE** - no transfer completes, no filesystem is reached and no mount is made - so **TWRP-to-storage STAYS WITHHELD**. **AND 793 CORRECTS THIS PRESS'S FIRST ROW BEFORE IT IS SPENT: it is TWO rows, because \`_cid_complete\` is the command's completion latch and the four \`RESPONSE\` words are read unconditionally (\`entry_storage.c:3341-3366\`). On NINE arms - seven narrow, two wide - those eight cells are byte-for-byte identical (\`_cid_raw0..3\` = \`0x0040ff80 / 0x80000000 / 0 / 0\`, \`_cid_resp0..3\` = \`0x40ff8080 / 0 / 0 / 0\`), and a 128-bit CID cannot have three of four words zero, so COMPLETING THE COMMAND IS NOT BY ITSELF TAKING THE CID. **1a**: \`_cid_complete = 1\` AND any of \`_cid_resp0\` / \`_cid_raw2\` / \`_cid_raw3\` / \`_cid_resp1..3\` off its nine-arm value - those cells have been zero on every arm this ladder has run - is THE CID TAKEN. **1b**: \`_cid_complete = 1\` with the pattern unchanged is the FIFTH ROW and the stronger one: the block completed a 136-bit command and the register file never filled, which separates the completion latch from the response path. The register was ZERO before the command path (\`st_resp_before\`, \`_rb_raw0 = 0\` first in the log on all three arms that carry both bodies), so anything in it was put there by these commands. **AND 794 CORRECTS WHAT ROW 1a BUYS: the 'driver order opens (CMD9, CMD7, CMD16, then data)' is TRUE OF THE LINUX DRIVER AND FALSE OF THIS IMAGE.** The image's whole command vocabulary is FOUR opcodes (CMD0-CMD3, \`grep -cE '^#define ST_CMD_OP_'\` = 4; there is no CMD9 define at all), \`st_cmd_path()\` is called once and the only thing after it is the power-IRQ consumer and a return - so THE FOURTH COMMAND IS THE LAST - and anchored, case-sensitive, there is NO data path (\`BLOCK_SIZE\`/\`BLOCK_COUNT\`/\`TRANSFER_MODE\`/\`ADMA\` appear in TWO lines and both are comments; \`ST_SDHCI_DMA_ADDRESS\` is read twice as a version word and never as a transfer address). **So a perfect CID here does not move the ladder one command closer to a mount.** Row 1a is still worth the press; its consequence was overstated, and the distance to 「挂载存储」 is TWO frontiers - the CMD2 bit AND the breadth gap (the bodies past CMD3 and a data path behind them). The next BUILD is about the first body past CMD3. **THIS RUNG HAS BEEN PRESSED AND IT ANSWERED - ROW 2, \`ERR | TIMEOUT\` - AND IT IS SPENT.** 2026-09-28 03:43:44 UTC, **EXIT 0**; one gate (exit 0 / 607 lines), one runner, \`fastboot boot\` only, nothing flashed, \`33e80afe\` absent from both lists; capture \`out/stage90/captures/rung31-nocrc-20260928-034344-last_kmsg.txt\` 633,586 B sha \`9c0baf80...\`; the runner's verdict **7 PASS / 1 FAIL / 3 UNREAD counted per criterion**, identical to rungs 29's and 30's. **THE IMMED